SRSObject.data:
{
            "mnemonic": "This radical is shaped like a {tooltip:Radical:barb}. Like the kind you'd see on barb wire. Imagine one of these getting stuck to your arm or your clothes. Think about how much it would hurt with that little hook on the end sticking into you. Say out loud, \"Oh dang, I got a barb stuck in me!\"",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Barb"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical looks just like a stick figure\u2014more specifically, a really {tooltip:Radical:big} guy with his arms stretched out nice and big. When you see this radical, just think big, because this guy is really BIG!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Big"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You know those big steel girders that are used for the frame of big buildings? This is like the end of one of those, and have a very distinct shape, just like this radical. These girders are used in construction, which is why this radical means {tooltip:Radical:construction}. Funny thing is, you use these girders to put together buildings just like you're going to use this radical to put together kanji!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Construction"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical looks like a {tooltip:Radical:cross}. I mean, just look at it. Two bars going through each other = a cross!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Cross"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This little radical is a {tooltip:Radical:drop} of water or some other liquid. Notice how it's only one drop? Sometimes radicals or kanji will have more than one drop, so it's important that you know how to tell them apart.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Drop"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Watch out! This isn't the person radical, it's something else. What's the difference? The right side is longer than the left side. And on computers, it usually has that extra part sticking out of the top. It kind of looks like a flap opening to a teepee. And what do you do with an opening? You enter it! That's why this radical is {tooltip:Radical:enter}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Enter"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Picture a fish. Now picture the fish a little worse, like a child's drawing of the fish. Now erase the fish's body and... you're left with two {tooltip:Radical:fins}! Do you see these two fins? Yeah, you see them.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Fins"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical consists of a single, horizontal stroke. What's the biggest, single, horizontal stroke? That's the {tooltip:Radical:ground}. Look at the ground, look at this radical, now look at the ground again. Kind of the same, right?",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Ground"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This looks a lot like a long {tooltip:Radical:gun} pointing to the right. You have a shoulder rest on the left, and the barrel on the right. Imagine it hanging in a case on the wall in a museum.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Gun"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's a leaf on top and a stem going down out of it, making it the {tooltip:Radical:leaf} radical. It looks kind of like a lilypad, sitting on top of the water with its stem going down to the bottom where the dirt is.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Leaf"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This looks like something you'd put on a kettle or a pot, right? It even has a little handle to make sure you don't burn yourself. This is some kind of {tooltip:Radical:lid}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Lid"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This is a {tooltip:Radical:mountain}. You'll have to add the lines that make up the top of the mountain if you want it more \"mountain-like.\" Also something that might help: sometimes mountains have a couple of smaller peaks coming out the side of the main peak too. Connect the dots and create the mountain in your mind.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Mountain"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A big, gaping, square hole represents someone opening their giant {tooltip:Radical:mouth} to tell you something stupid.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Mouth"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "If you look at this radical in just the right way, you'll notice that it kind of looks like the letters \"n\" and \"i\" (the \"i\" comes from the little flippy-up stroke on the right side). What do the letters \"n\" and \"i\" start spelling? Why, the word {tooltip:Radical:nine}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Nine"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "If you were to draw a {tooltip:Radical:person} with just strokes like you see in kanji, you'd create a stick figure, maybe something like this: {span:lang=ja:\u5927}. But this is just a person, not someone holding out their arms to show how big they are. So their arms are down, which makes them look like a torso with a pair of legs. Maybe they're walking too, which is why their legs are spread out like that. You can see the person now, can't you?",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Person"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Imagine a guy flexing his arm downward to show off his steroid-pumped arm. It's huge. It's so full of {tooltip:Radical:power}. RARRRWWWRRR!!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Power"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This looks like an enclosure of sorts - that's why it's a {tooltip:Radical:prison}. It wraps itself around things and locks them in there. Prisons are pretty scary, even before you imagine one slowly floating down from the sky to trap you.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Prison"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical already looks like a {tooltip:Radical:river}. See the banks of the river on the side and the flow of the river in the middle? There's even a slight turn in the river too. So when you see this radical, you should think \"river.\"",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "River"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "What does this radical look like? Hmm, nothing much. What about if you turn it upside down? Now does it look like a 7 with a line through it? Close enough to help you to remember that this radical is {tooltip:Radical:seven}, right? Just remember, when you see this radical, flip it over and see what number it looks like. If you can do that, you can remember the meaning!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Seven"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Close your eyes and imagine you're a kid again. Now open them and... look! It's a {tooltip:Radical:slide}! Imagine little you sliding down this slide over and over, having a great time. Imagination + emotion makes for good memorization!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Slide"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This is a {tooltip:Radical:stick}, sticking out of the ground. It even looks like a stick.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Stick"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Since there are no circles in Japanese characters, sometimes rectangles or squares have to make do. In this case, this big rectangle is the {tooltip:Radical:sun}. The middle line going through the center horizontally is the equator. Or maybe it's a cloud, moving across the sun. Picture it, but don't actually look at the sun and burn your eyes!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Sun"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical looks just like the katakana character {span:lang=ja:\u30c8} (to), which sounds like the word {tooltip:Radical:toe}. So when you see {span:lang=ja:\u30c8}, think toe.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Toe"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Do you see the {tooltip:Radical:tree} in here? It has branches, and then it has the foliage coming down diagonally from it, spreading out like a tree would. That's why this radical is a tree.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Tree"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "One line plus another line equals {tooltip:Radical:two} lines. This radical represents the number two. Pretty simple, right? Can you guess what three looks like? I bet you can...",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Two"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical looks like a wide person made up of two stacked Xs. Women generally have two XXs for chromosomes, and back in the day they were praised for their wide, child-bearing hips. We may not think of women this way anymore, but way back when Japanese was made, this made them think of a {tooltip:Radical:woman}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Woman"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You find a {tooltip:Radical:toe} on the {tooltip:Radical:ground}. It's weird, because it's {tooltip:Kanji:above} the ground, not where toes belong.",
            "hints": "Focus your imagination on how body parts should be buried, not above ground like this. Sure, finding a toe is a little strange, but finding a toe above the ground? Downright silly.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Above",
                "Alternatives": "Up, Over"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3058\u3087\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3046\u3048, \u3042, \u306e\u307c, \u3046\u308f, \u304b\u307f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Try to remember Jourm, the local farmhand. He is a recurring character that will come up anytime the reading for a kanji is {span:lang=ja:\u3058\u3087\u3046}.{splitParent}He is a very big man, and he wears farmer's clothes. It's important to imagine your version of Jourm as very big, because it will help us to differentiate him from Jo-Anne, who is the character associated with the similar, but shorter reading (and character) {span:lang=ja:\u3058\u3087}. Although Jourm will develop as you go through all the {span:lang=ja:\u3058\u3087\u3046} reading kanji, for now just imagine him as a big, kinda slow dude who works for a local farmer. Also, he only has nine or fewer toes, apparently.",
                "mnemonic": "Of course when you find a toe {tooltip:Kanji:above} the ground, you want to know where the toe came from. When you examine it, you see a name written on there. This toe belongs to the local clumsy farmhand, {tooltip:Reading:Jou}rm ({span:lang=ja:\u3058\u3087\u3046})."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's a {tooltip:Radical:toe} {tooltip:Kanji:below} {tooltip:Radical:ground}.",
            "hints": "So, where is the {tooltip:Radical:toe} now? It's {tooltip:Kanji:below} ground. Imagine yourself digging it out, and thinking... ugh, why does it have to be {tooltip:Kanji:below} ground like this? So irritating. Will I be able to sew it back on in time to save it?",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Below",
                "Alternatives": "Down, Under, Beneath"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304b, \u3052",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u305f, \u3055, \u304f\u3060, \u304a",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "There's another useful on'yomi reading that just isn't useful enough for you to learn now. That reading is {span:lang=ja:\u3052}, but we're only going to worry about that much, much later when you have to learn one or two vocab words that read {span:lang=ja:\u4e0b} as {span:lang=ja:\u3052}. That's the problem with really common kanji - too many readings.",
                "mnemonic": "You keep digging for the {tooltip:Radical:toe} {tooltip:Kanji:below} the {tooltip:Radical:ground}, and instead of hitting a {tooltip:Radical:toe} you hit something hard and metallic. You keep digging and digging around it, but it's huge. Turns out, you found yourself a {tooltip:Reading:car} ({span:lang=ja:\u304b}). Keep digging until you unearth the car. Inside, you find the toe you were looking for."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:big} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:big} kanji are exactly the same!",
            "hints": "So, if you know the radical, you know the meaning of this kanji as well.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Big",
                "Alternative": "Large"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f\u3044, \u3060\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304a\u304a",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "\u3072\u308d",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "When you look at the big guy in your head, imagine him covered in tie dye head to toe. Think about how ridiculous it looks on him, especially since he's such a big guy. Shirt. Sweatpants. Hat. Shoes... it goes on and on.",
                "mnemonic": "There's this big guy (that's how you remember the radical, remember?), and he's wearing a huge {tooltip:Reading:Tie Dye} ({span:lang=ja:\u305f\u3044}, {span:lang=ja:\u3060\u3044}) shirt. Because he's so huge, the tie dye shirt is also super huge. Everything about him is pretty big, but the tie dye stands out more than anything."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:construction} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:construction} kanji are the same!",
            "hints": "Know your radicals and you'll know this kanji's meaning.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Construction",
                "Alternative": "Industry"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3053\u3046, \u304f",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "If {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061} as a construction worker standing in a construction site some how doesn't make your legs tremble, that's okay and totally normal. Do make sure you take this story to a ridiculous enough level to hook a memory into that brain of yours. Be mad at him for not following construction site regulations (where's his shirt, shoes, and pants!?). Why is he using that bulldozer like that?{splitParent}Alternatively, say you really do like what you see (you're going to have to imagine a much better looking version of {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061}'s actual face, though). You and {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061} make eye contact a little too long. He nods, puts his cigarette out, and takes off his hard hat, letting his long Fabio-esque locks flow. You beckon him over and he comes. What happens next is up to you and your own imagination. My guess is you have a very deep conversation about kanji and the best ways to learn it.{splitParent}Hint: When you see {span:lang=ja:\u5de5} in a kanji it will often take the {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046} reading. Remember that, because it comes up quite a bit!",
                "mnemonic": "For kanji that use the {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046} (there are a lot of them!) we will use the character {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061} every time. He's one of the people who started this website, so we'll do our best in the mnemonics to embarrass him as much as possible. Anyways, remember that whenever you see {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046}, think {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061}."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This isn't exactly the same as the {tooltip:Radical:fins} radical, but it's close. Sometimes this will happen (where we have to use a slightly \"modified radical\" version of the original), so it's good to understand that now, early on. In this case, we have fins. How many fins? {tooltip:Kanji:Eight} fins. How do you know that? Because you caught four fish with two fins each.",
            "hints": "Count the fins out in front of you, all the way from one to eight. It'll help if you actually do this. Now smell each fin, and think \"Wow, I have to smell all eight of these?\" Yes, you do.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Eight"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306f\u3061",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3084, \u3088\u3046",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "You've asked him why you can only put eight fins at a time in the hatch, but he never wants to answer. What could be down in the hole that the hatch covers? Why is there a hatch at all? What's with numbers and hatches being mysterious?",
                "mnemonic": "After you count and smell all {tooltip:Kanji:eight} of your fins, it's time to put them away. You open a large {tooltip:Reading:hatch} ({span:lang=ja:\u306f\u3061}) and put them in. This is where you keep your fins, always putting in eight at a time because that's what the boss wants."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The kanji and the radical are the same, so remembering the meaning of this kanji is as simple as making sure you know the radical for {tooltip:Radical:enter} first! If you know the radical, you know this kanji means {tooltip:Kanji:enter} as well.",
            "hints": "Make sure you know those radicals, yo!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Enter"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306b\u3085\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306f\u3044, \u3044",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine lights turning on and flashing, and an entire audience inside. You're at a gameshow, and the announcer announces the {tooltip:Reading:neeeeew} teepee in his announcey voice. Feel the surprise. Feel the happiness. Feel the excitement. A beautiful new teepee all for you! ",
                "mnemonic": "Turns out, after you {tooltip:Kanji:enter} the teepee (you'll remember that the radical is a teepee with a big entrance to it), you're given a {tooltip:Reading:new} ({span:lang=ja:\u306b\u3085\u3046}) one! Now you have two teepees!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:mountain} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:mountain} kanji are the same.",
            "hints": "Make sure you're studying your radicals. If you are, then you're studying your kanji as well!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Mountain"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3055\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3084\u307e",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Be sure to imagine this vividly. The more absurdly you can imagine it, the more likely you are to remember the reading, {tooltip:Reading:san}.",
                "mnemonic": "Think about {tooltip:Kanji:mountains} talking to each other, calling each other by their names and adding the Japanese name-ender {tooltip:Reading:san} ({span:lang=ja:\u3055\u3093}) to each of their names. \"Hello, Everest-{tooltip:Reading:san}.\" \"Oh hi, Fuji-{tooltip:Reading:san}.\""
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:mouth} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:mouth} kanji are exactly the same!",
            "hints": "If you learned your radicals\u2013which the WaniKani system forces you to do (unless you're cheating)\u2013you'll already know the meaning of the kanji.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Mouth"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3053\u3046, \u304f",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304f\u3061",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "You just can't forget all the cool things about kanji learning he said from that mouth. What a great mouth {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061} has. How are the lips of his mouth both so skinny and fat at the same time?",
                "mnemonic": "Do you remember seeing the construction worker {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061} earlier in the {span:lang=ja:\u5de5} mnemonic? If you haven't yet, not to worry! \n{linebreak}For kanji with {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046} readings (there are a lot of them!) we will use the character {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061} every time. He's one of the people who started this website, so we'll do our best in the mnemonics to embarrass him as much as possible. Anyways, remember that whenever you see {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046}, think {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061}."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Nice! This kanji is the same as the radical that looks just like it (down to the meaning, even!). The radical is {tooltip:Radical:nine} and the kanji is {tooltip:Kanji:nine}, making the meaning of this kanji really easy to remember as long as you know the radical first.",
            "hints": "If you know your radicals well, this kanji will be a breeze!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Nine"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304f, \u304d\u3085\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3053\u3053\u306e",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture nine-year-old you, lining up nine cookies and nine cucumbers. You dip a cookie in milk and bite into it, then dip a cucumber in and take a bite. Nine bites each until they're all gone. Ah, you miss being nine.",
                "mnemonic": "Although this kanji has two on'yomi to learn, the pronunciations of those two on'yomi are very similar, so that should make them easier to remember."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Lying on the {tooltip:Radical:ground} is something that looks just like the ground, the number {tooltip:Kanji:One}. Why is this One lying down? It's been shot by the number two. It's lying there, bleeding out and dying. The number One doesn't have long to live.",
            "hints": "To remember the meaning of {tooltip:Kanji:One}, imagine yourself there at the scene of the crime. You grab {tooltip:Kanji:One} in your arms, trying to prop it up, trying to hear its last words. Instead, it just splatters some blood on your face. \"Who did this to you?\" you ask. The number One points weakly, and you see number Two running off into an alleyway. He's always been jealous of number One and knows he can be number one now that he's taken the real number one out.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "One"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3044\u3061, \u3044\u3064",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3072\u3068",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "\u304b\u305a",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Make sure you feel the ridiculously {tooltip:Reading:itchy} sensation covering your body. It climbs from your hands, where you're holding the number {tooltip:Kanji:One} up, and then goes through your arms, crawls up your neck, goes down your body, and then covers everything. It becomes uncontrollable, and you're scratching everywhere, writhing on the ground. It's so itchy that it's the most painful thing you've ever experienced (you should imagine this vividly, so you remember the reading of this kanji).",
                "mnemonic": "As you're sitting there next to {tooltip:Kanji:One}, holding him up, you start feeling a weird sensation all over your skin. From the wound comes a fine powder (obviously coming from the special bullet used to kill One) that causes the person it touches to get extremely {tooltip:Reading:itchy} ({span:lang=ja:\u3044\u3061})."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Remember how the radical version of this kanji meant {tooltip:Radical:person} because it's a person walking from the side with their arms down? Well this is a {tooltip:Kanji:person} too!",
            "hints": "Know the radicals, and you shall know the kanji.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Person"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306b\u3093, \u3058\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3072\u3068, \u3068",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine how angry you were about the policy. You'll have to come up with something to be angry about yourself (pick something that would actually make you angry, like maybe you're only allowed to wear cut off short-shorts jeans at Nintendo now), but if you pick something good, you'll definitely remember the two readings for this. Just remember you're working at {tooltip:Reading:Nin}tendo, and the policy has to be something about {tooltip:Reading:jean}s. Then, imagine yourself as the only {tooltip:Kanji:person} complaining and everyone else in their dumb jeans (or lack-thereof) shaking their head at you because you're being a fool.",
                "mnemonic": "Only one {tooltip:Kanji:person} at {tooltip:Reading:Nin}tendo ({span:lang=ja:\u306b\u3093}) complained about the new {tooltip:Reading:jean}s ({span:lang=ja:\u3058\u3093}) policy, and that person was you."
            }
        }

{
