{
            "mnemonic": "Lucky for you (as is the case with many of the lower-stroke kanji), this kanji is the same as the radical you've already learned. The radical that makes up this kanji is {tooltip:Radical:seven} and the kanji (which is exactly the same) is also {tooltip:Kanji:seven}. So, if you know the radical, you know the meaning of the kanji as well.",
            "hints": "Make sure you know your radicals well and you'll be A-Okay on this one!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Seven"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3061",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306a\u306a, \u306a\u306e",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "How unbelievable is it that someone got {tooltip:Kanji:seven} percent on a test, especially if you try to cheat? Imagine the girl you loathe the most from school, and imagine her cheating on the test (it's important it's a girl, because of the \"she\" part in {tooltip:Reading:she chea}ted ({span:lang=ja:\u3057\u3061}). Then, imagine your happiness when she's super upset about getting seven percent on it. Serves her right, obviously.",
                "mnemonic": "You know this kanji means {tooltip:Kanji:Seven}, so let's use that to remember the reading too. {tooltip:Kanji:Seven} is a lucky number, usually, but this time it wasn't so good. "
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This {tooltip:Radical:cross} is the symbol for the number {tooltip:Kanji:ten}. A good way to remember this is to think about all the religions that have crosses and also important sets of tens. ",
            "hints": "If you're familiar with Christianity, they have a cross and ten commandments. Cross = ten, you see?",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Ten"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3058\u3085\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3068\u304a",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture the jewels on this cross. Maybe draw it out on a piece of paper so the visual really sticks. Each cross is really a symbol for different sets of ten things, and in this case, it's ten jewels.",
                "mnemonic": "This cross in particular has {tooltip:Kanji:ten} {tooltip:Reading:jew}els ({span:lang=ja:\u3058\u3085\u3046}) on it. Two on each arm and two in the middle. That's ten jewels!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This kanji is made up of the ground radical (which also happens to be the kanji for \"one\") and the radical for two. Combine {tooltip:Radical:one} and {tooltip:Radical:two} together and what do you get? {tooltip:Kanji:Three}!",
            "hints": "This one should be pretty self explanatory! Even easier, you can look at this kanji and see it has three straight lines. You know what one looks like. You know what two looks like. So... this is three!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Three"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3055\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture Santa-san sitting at his desk at the North Pole, eating cookies, and marking one, two, then finally three lines next to names. Once the third line appears, the kid doesn't get presents anymore.",
                "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Kanji:Three} is the number of chances you get with {tooltip:Reading:San}ta-san ({span:lang=ja:\u3055\u3093}). He has a list, and when you get to three \"naughty things\" lines, that's it. You're on Santa-san's three strikes and you're out list."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The kanji {tooltip:Kanji:two} and the radical {tooltip:Radical:two} are the exact same things. Therefore, you know the kanji for two already!",
            "hints": "More simply put, you can just know that this kanji is the double of One. It's two lines, that's not all that hard to remember!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Two"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306b",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3075\u305f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine yourself without two knees, and see what it would be like. What would give you trouble? What couldn't you do? Just imagining this for a minute or two will help you to remember the reading of this kanji more effectively.",
                "mnemonic": "Think of all the things you have {tooltip:Kanji:Two} of. What is the most important? Some would say eyes, some would say ears. I would say the {tooltip:Reading:knee} ({span:lang=ja:\u306b}) because if you didn't have them, it'd be really hard to go up stairs."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The radical for {tooltip:Radical:woman} and the kanji for {tooltip:Kanji:woman} are exactly the same.",
            "hints": "That means if you know your radicals well, you know the meaning of this kanji as well! How swell.",
            "meanings": {
