{
            "mnemonic": "When a vocab is a single kanji it usually steals its meaning from its kanji as well. Same goes for this vocab word. It means {tooltip:Vocabulary:eight}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Eight",
                "Word Type": "numeral"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u306f\u3061"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u306f\u3061",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "2983-subject-2482.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u306f\u3061",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "27955-subject-2482.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}When a vocab is a single kanji and all alone like this, it's usually going to use the kun'yomi. But, because this is a number (exception) it uses the on'yomi instead, meaning you already know the reading from when you learned the kanji!\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u304c\u304f\u305b\u3044\u306f\u3001\u304d\u3087\u3046\u3046\u3061\u3067\u3001\u516b\u30da\u30fc\u30b8\u3092\u3079\u3093\u304d\u3087\u3046\u3057\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "Students will study page eight at home today."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3042\u3081\u3092\u516b\u3053\u30ab\u30d0\u30f3\u306b\u5165\u308c\u307e\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "I put eight candies in my purse."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u516b\u3058\uff1f\u307b\u3093\u3068\u3046\u306b\u3001\u516b\u3058\uff1f",
                    "en": "Eight o\u2019clock? Are you sure it\u2019s eight o\u2019clock?"
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This word follows the \"number of things\" pattern where there's a kanji for a number plus {span:lang=ja:\u3064} on the end. Whenever you see this, you know the word means \"____ things.\" Knowing that, as long as you know the kanji (which you do) you can figure out what number of things it is. For this one, it's the kanji for {tooltip:Kanji:eight} plus {span:lang=ja:\u3064}. So, this one is {tooltip:Vocabulary:eight things}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Eight Things",
                "Word Type": "numeral"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3084\u3063\u3064"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3084\u3063\u3064",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "27980-subject-2483.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3084\u3063\u3064",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "3123-subject-2483.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}Since all of the \"number of things\" words all follow the same pattern (number plus {span:lang=ja:\u3064}), you really just have to remember the part before the {span:lang=ja:\u3064} (and then remember that the {span:lang=ja:\u3064} is in all of these). All of the readings are the kun'yomi reading, which means we'll have to use a mnemonic to remember them (you know the on'yomi reading of the kanji portion).\n{splitParent}So what {tooltip:Vocabulary:eight things} do you have? You have eight {tooltip:Reading:yachts} ({span:lang=ja:\u3084\u3063\u3064}). Go ahead and count them. They're beautiful. Also, be sure not to be confused by this particular reading mnemonic. It includes the {span:lang=ja:\u3064}, even though the {span:lang=ja:\u3064} is outside the reading you need to learn (just makes things easier for you overall). Since you can see the {span:lang=ja:\u3064} outside, it shouldn't be too difficult for you.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u3075\u304f\u308d\u306b\u306f\u3001\u3058\u3083\u304c\u3044\u3082\u304c\u516b\u3064\u5165\u3063\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "There are eight potatoes in a bag."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u516b\u3064\u3057\u304b\u3042\u308a\u307e\u305b\u3093\u3088\u3002",
                    "en": "There are only eight of them."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u516b\u3064\u306e\u304b\u304a\u3068\u516d\u3064\u306e\u3046\u3067\u304c\u3042\u308b\u3082\u306e\u3001\u306a\u30fc\u3093\u3060\u3002",
                    "en": "What has eight faces and six arms?"
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Kanji:mouth} that you {tooltip:Kanji:enter} is an {tooltip:Vocabulary:entrance}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Entrance",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3044\u308a\u3050\u3061"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3044\u308a\u3050\u3061",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "2881-subject-2498.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3044\u308a\u3050\u3061",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "27936-subject-2498.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}This word is really two separate words put together to form one, so let's look at it in two parts:\n{splitParent}The kanji {span:lang=ja:\u5165} has {span:lang=ja:\u308a} attached to it as okurigana (hiragana that comes after a kanji), so you can bet that {span:lang=ja:\u5165\u308a} will use a kun'yomi reading for the kanji. You haven't learned this reading yet, so here's a mnemonic to help you:\n{splitParent}The {tooltip:Vocabulary:entrance} you're going through is really {tooltip:Reading:eerie} ({span:lang=ja:\u3044\u308a}). It has cobwebs and creepy figures carved into it, and it just gives you a really eerie feeling.\n{splitParent}Since {span:lang=ja:\u5165\u308a} uses the kun'yomi, you can guess that {span:lang=ja:\u53e3} will follow suit. You learned this reading with the vocabulary, but the {span:lang=ja:\u304f} in {span:lang=ja:\u304f\u3061} changes to {span:lang=ja:\u3050} due to something called rendaku. Think of it as an eerie entrance into the {tooltip:Reading:Gucci} ({span:lang=ja:\u3050\u3061}) store.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u5165\u308a\u53e3\u306e\u3068\u3053\u308d\u3067\u307e\u3063\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "I will wait for you at the entrance."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u5165\u308a\u53e3\u304c\u4e09\u3064\u3042\u308a\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "There are three entrances."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u5165\u308a\u53e3\u306b\u30b8\u30e7\u30cb\u30fc\u30c7\u30c3\u30d7\u304c\u3044\u308b\u3088\u3002",
                    "en": "Johnny Depp is at the entrance."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "{span:lang=ja:\u3057\u305f} is the past tense form of {span:lang=ja:\u3059\u308b} (which means \"to do\"). So, essentially this word is \"did big.\" What is something that \"did big\"? It's something that's {tooltip:Vocabulary:great} or {tooltip:Vocabulary:considerable}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Great",
                "Alternatives": "Considerable, Important, Big Deal",
                "Word Type": "adjective"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u305f\u3044\u3057\u305f"
