                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3084\u307e",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "27956-subject-2504.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}When a vocab is a single kanji all alone with no okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) it's usually going to be the kun'yomi reading, which you didn't learn when you learned the kanji. To remember this word's reading we'll use a mnemonic.\n{splitParent}What are you doing going up into the {tooltip:Vocabulary:mountain}s? You're hunting for {tooltip:Reading:yam}s ({span:lang=ja:\u3084\u307e}). Go ahead, imagine yourself doing just that, climbing up the mountain (it's hard work!) and then digging in the ground with your hands, then pulling up that sweet, delicious yam! Mmm!\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u3068\u3082\u3060\u3061\u3068\u3044\u3063\u3057\u3087\u306b\u3001\u5c71\u306b\u306e\u307c\u308a\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "I'll climb a mountain with my friends."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u308c\u306f\u305b\u304b\u3044\u4e00\u5927\u304d\u3044\u4eba\u5de5\u306e\u5c71\u306a\u3093\u3067\u3059\u3088\u3002",
                    "en": "This is the world's biggest man-made mountain."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3042\u3055\u516b\u3058\u306b\u3001\u306f\u3060\u304b\u3067\u306f\u3052\u5c71\u306b\u3057\u3085\u3046\u3054\u3046\u3057\u307e\u3057\u3087\u3046\u3002",
                    "en": "At 8 am, let\u2019s meet naked on Bald Mountain."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "When a vocab is made up of a single kanji with no okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) it's generally going to have the same meaning as the kanji it came from. From that, you know this word's meaning is {tooltip:Vocabulary:mouth}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Mouth",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u304f\u3061"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u304f\u3061",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "2744-subject-2497.ogg"
