                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u304f\u3061",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "27915-subject-2497.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}When a vocab is made up of a single kanji with no okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) it's generally going to use the kun'yomi reading, which you did not learn from learning the kanji. To remember this word, you should use a mnemonic. With your {tooltip:Vocabulary:mouth}, make a \"{tooltip:Reading:kuchi} kuchi kou\" ({span:lang=ja:\u304f\u3061}) sound. Go ahead, make it. Focus on your mouth and try to imagine each shape it makes while zooming in on it. You're talking to a baby, which is why you're making all these ridiculous noises. Also, instead of \"coo\" at the end, you're saying \"kou,\" because that's the reading of the kanji ({span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046}).\n{splitParent}{span:lang=ja:\u304f\u3061\u304f\u3061\u3053\u3046\uff01\u304f\u3061\u304f\u3061\u3053\u3046\uff01} Sounds like something from Arrested Development, actually.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u53e3\u306b\u30bd\u30fc\u30b9\u304c\u3064\u3044\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3088\u3002",
                    "en": "There is some sauce on your mouth."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u308c\u3092\u53e3\u306b\u5165\u308c\u3066\u304f\u3060\u3055\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "Please put this in your mouth."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u304d\u306e\u3046\u304b\u3089\u53e3\u304c\u3068\u3058\u3089\u308c\u306a\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "I haven\u2019t been able to close my mouth since yesterday."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Just look at this word. What do you think it means? That's right - it's a name (Fuji) plus the kanji for {tooltip:Kanji:mountain}. Put those together, and you have {tooltip:Vocabulary:Mount Fuji}. In English the \"Mount(ain)\" part comes first. In Japanese, it comes at the end, sort of like the name ender {span:lang=ja:\u3055\u3093}, {span:lang=ja:\u3061\u3083\u3093}, and so on. You can add {span:lang=ja:\u5c71} to the end of any mountain's name too, not just Mount Fuji.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Mt Fuji",
                "Alternatives": "Mount Fuji, Mt. Fuji",
                "Word Type": "proper noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3075\u3058\u3055\u3093"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3075\u3058\u3055\u3093",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "27951-subject-2505.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3075\u3058\u3055\u3093",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "21571-subject-2505.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}Normally {span:lang=ja:\u3075\u3058} would be in kanji, but I wanted you to see this kanji use in action since it's more useful than just being used on Mount Fuji. This is treated like a jukugo word (combo kanji word). In the case of jukugo, usually the on'yomi reading is used, meaning you just have to remember the on'yomi reading of {span:lang=ja:\u5c71}. Luckily, you learned this when you were learning the kanji, so you should already know it! \n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u3075\u3058\u5c71\u306b\u3001\u3054\u305c\u3093\u5341\u4e00\u3058\u3054\u308d\u307e\u3067\u306b\u3064\u304d\u305f\u3044\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "I want to arrive at Mt. Fuji by around 11 a.m."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3075\u3058\u5c71\u306b\u306f\u3044\u3064\u3082\u5927\u4eba\u306e\u30d5\u30e9\u30f3\u30b9\u4eba\u304c\u4e09\u4eba\u3068\u3061\u3044\u3055\u3044\u30a2\u30e1\u30ea\u30ab\u4eba\u304c\u4e8c\u4eba\u3068\u30ab\u30ca\u30c0\u4eba\u304c\u4e00\u4eba\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "There are always three adult French people, two small Americans, and one Canadian on Mt. Fuji."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3075\u3058\u5c71\u306f\u3057\u308d\u3044\u3068\u308a\u306e\u3075\u3093\u3067\u304a\u304a\u308f\u308c\u3066\u3044\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "Mt. Fuji was covered in white bird poop."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

