                        "file": "27942-subject-2469.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}Most single-kanji all-alone vocab words like this use the kun'yomi reading. Numbers are an exception to this rule. That being said, four and seven are exceptions to this exception to the rule (because the on'yomi readings sound too much like the word for \"death\"). You should learn both readings for this word, though we're going to go with {span:lang=ja:\u306a\u306a} for the main one here. You can remember that because you had seven {tooltip:Reading:nana}s after you.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u3042\u3057\u305f\u306e\u3042\u3055\u3001\u4e03\u3058\u306b\u3048\u304d\u3067\u3042\u3044\u307e\u305b\u3093\u304b\u3002",
                    "en": "Why don\u2019t we meet at the station at seven tomorrow morning?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u307f\u304b\u3093\u304c\u4e03\u3053\u3042\u308a\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "There are seven mandarin oranges."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u4e03\u306b\u3093\u306e\u3053\u3073\u3068\u3068\u3068\u3082\u3060\u3061\u306b\u306a\u308a\u305f\u3044\u3002",
