{
            "mnemonic": "This word follows the \"number of things\" pattern where there's a kanji for a number plus {span:lang=ja:\u3064} on the end. Whenever you see this, you know the word means \"____ things.\" Knowing that, as long as you know the kanji (which you do) you can figure out what number of things it is. For this one, it's the kanji for {tooltip:Kanji:three} plus {span:lang=ja:\u3064}. So, this one is {tooltip:Vocabulary:three things}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Three Things",
                "Word Type": "numeral"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u307f\u3063\u3064"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u307f\u3063\u3064",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "3136-subject-2487.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u307f\u3063\u3064",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "27981-subject-2487.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}Since all of the \"number of things\" words all follow the same pattern (number plus {span:lang=ja:\u3064}), you really just have to remember the part before the {span:lang=ja:\u3064} (and then remember that the {span:lang=ja:\u3064} is in all of these). All of the readings are the kun'yomi reading, which means we'll have to use a mnemonic to remember them (you know the on'yomi reading of the kanji portion).\n{splitParent}What thing do you have three of? The {tooltip:Vocabulary:three things} that you have are actually three versions of yourself. \"Who is the real one?\" you ask yourself. \"It's {tooltip:Reading:me}!\" \"Wait, no, it's {tooltip:Reading:me}!\" Just remember that there are three of you, and they're all saying \"me me me me me\" to try and prove that they're the real you.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u4e09\u3064\u306e\u305b\u3093\u305f\u304f\u3057\u304c\u304a\u3093\u305b\u3044\u3067\u304d\u3053\u3048\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "You will hear three choices."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u3053\u306b\u30dc\u30fc\u30eb\u3092\u4e09\u3064\u5165\u308c\u3066\u304f\u3060\u3055\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "Please put three balls in here."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u4e09\u3064\u3082\u304b\u306b\u304b\u307e\u308c\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "I got three mosquito bites."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This vocab word has okurigana, which is the hiragana part that comes after the kanji. When a vocab's okurigana makes an \"{span:lang=ja:\u3046}/oo\" sound (in this case, we have a {span:lang=ja:\u308b}), it's often going to be a verb. So in this case, the kanji {span:lang=ja:\u5165} is {tooltip:Kanji:enter}. The vocab {span:lang=ja:\u5165\u308b} is {tooltip:Vocabulary:to enter}. We're just taking the kanji and using it in a verb! ",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "To Enter",
                "Alternative": "To Go In",
                "Word Type": "intransitive verb, godan verb"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u306f\u3044\u308b"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u306f\u3044\u308b",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "2936-subject-2480.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u306f\u3044\u308b",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "27946-subject-2480.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}When a vocab is a single kanji with okurigana, chances are it's going to be the kun'yomi reading, which you don't usually learn in the kanji (you usually learn the on'yomi reading). So, let's learn the kun'yomi reading for this word using its meaning.\n{splitParent}When you {tooltip:Vocabulary:enter} the room, what do you do? You say \"{tooltip:Reading:Hi}\" ({span:lang=ja:\u306f\u3044}) to everyone to the point of absurdity. \"Hi there. Oh hi Frank. Oh hi there Susan. Oh hi there lamp.\"\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u30d7\u30fc\u30eb\u306b\u5165\u308b\u307e\u3048\u306b\u3001\u3058\u3085\u3093\u3073\u3046\u3093\u3069\u3046\u3092\u3057\u3066\u304f\u3060\u3055\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "Please stretch before getting in the pool."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3061\u3083\u3093\u3068\u5165\u308a\u53e3\u304b\u3089\u5165\u3063\u3066\u304f\u3060\u3055\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "Please enter from the entrance."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3078\u3084\u306b\u5165\u3063\u3066\u3001\u3044\u3063\u3057\u3087\u306b\u307e\u3086\u3052\u3092\u304b\u3053\u3046\u3088\uff01",
                    "en": "Let\u2019s go in the room and draw our eyebrows on!"
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
