                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "27941-subject-2473.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}When a vocab word is a single kanji with no okurigana, it usually uses the kun'yomi reading. Numbers are an exception to this. Numbers use the on'yomi reading, which you've already learned by learning the kanji's reading! How nice!\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u4e8c\u5341\u4e00\u30da\u30fc\u30b8\u306e\u4e8c\u3070\u3093\u306f\u30af\u30e9\u30b9\u3067\u3057\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "We will work on question two on page twenty-one in class."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u308f\u305f\u3057\u306e\u3053\u3069\u3082\u306f\u4e8c\u3055\u3044\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "My child is 2 years old."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u308f\u305f\u3057\u306e\u304a\u3058\u306f\u3001\u306d\u3053\u3092\u4e8c\u3072\u304d\u304b\u3063\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "My uncle has two cats."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "When you have {tooltip:Kanji:two} and {tooltip:Kanji:person} and then put them together, what do you get? You get a {tooltip:Vocabulary:pair} or {tooltip:Vocabulary:two people}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Two People",
                "Alternatives": "Pair, Couple, Two Persons",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3075\u305f\u308a"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3075\u305f\u308a",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "3116-subject-2476.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3075\u305f\u308a",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "27971-subject-2476.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The reading for this word is quite strange. If you know {span:lang=ja:\u4e8c\u3064}'s reading, you can use that to remember the {span:lang=ja:\u4e8c} part ({span:lang=ja:\u3075\u305f}). But, the {span:lang=ja:\u308a} that is the {span:lang=ja:\u4eba} is a total exception, something you won't see too often (though you may have seen it in {span:lang=ja:\u4e00\u4eba}). If you can use the reading of {span:lang=ja:\u4e00\u4eba} (aka if you've learned it already) then definitely use that. If not, do your best to remember the reading on your own. It's a strange one that doesn't connect to much else.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "DVD\u3092\u3001\u3042\u306d\u3068\u4e8c\u4eba\u3067\u307f\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "I am watching a DVD with my older sister."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3042\u306e\u4e8c\u4eba\u306f\u3044\u3064\u3082\u4e8c\u4eba\u3067\u304f\u307f\u305f\u304c\u308b\u3002",
                    "en": "Those two want to be a couple all the time."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u304b\u306e\u3058\u3087\u306f\u4e8c\u4eba\u3054\u308d\u3057\u3067\u305f\u3044\u307b\u3055\u308c\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "She was arrested for double murder."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This word follows the \"number of things\" pattern where there's a kanji for a number plus {span:lang=ja:\u3064} on the end. Whenever you see this, you know the word means \"____ things.\" Knowing that, as long as you know the kanji (which you do) you can figure out what number of things it is. For this one, it's the kanji for {tooltip:Kanji:two} plus {span:lang=ja:\u3064}. So, this one is {tooltip:Vocabulary:two things}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Two Things",
                "Word Type": "numeral"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3075\u305f\u3064"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3075\u305f\u3064",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "27976-subject-2474.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3075\u305f\u3064",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "3099-subject-2474.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}Since all of the \"number of things\" words all follow the same pattern (number plus {span:lang=ja:\u3064}), you really just have to remember the part before the {span:lang=ja:\u3064} (and then remember that the {span:lang=ja:\u3064} is in all of these). All of the readings are the kun'yomi reading, which means we'll have to use a mnemonic to remember them (you know the on'yomi reading of the kanji portion).\n{splitParent}So what thing do you have two of? The {tooltip:Vocabulary:two things} that you have consist of one foot and another {tooltip:Reading:foot} ({span:lang=ja:\u3075\u305f}). Remember from the on'yomi how you had two knees (for the reading {span:lang=ja:\u306b})? Now look further down your leg at one foot then over to the other foot. Two foot!\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u4e8c\u3064\u306e\u304b\u3044\u308f\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "It\u2019s two dialogues."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u30ab\u30b4\u306b\u30ea\u30f3\u30b4\u3092\u4e8c\u3064\u5165\u308c\u3066\u304f\u3060\u3055\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "Please put two apples in this basket."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u304b\u308c\u306e\u4e8c\u3064\u3081\u306e\u3042\u304f\u304e\u3087\u3046\u306f\u3001\u304b\u3048\u308b\u306e\u3042\u3093\u3055\u3064\u3060\u3063\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "His second evil deed was the frog\u2019s assassination."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "When a vocab word is a single kanji and alone, it tends to steal the meaning from the kanji. Same goes for this one too. It means {tooltip:Vocabulary:above} or {tooltip:Vocabulary:up}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Up",
                "Alternatives": "Above, Over",
                "Word Type": "noun, suffix"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3046\u3048"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3046\u3048",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "2779-subject-2489.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3046\u3048",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "27918-subject-2489.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}When a vocab word is a single kanji and doesn't have okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) it usually will use the kun'yomi. Since you learned the on'yomi reading of the kanji, we'll need to use a mnemonic to learn the reading of this vocabulary word. \n{splitParent}{tooltip:Vocabulary:Above} you is a huge weight. You're holding it up and struggling (it's heavy!). You look up and try to crane your neck to see how much it {tooltip:Reading:weigh}s ({span:lang=ja:\u3046\u3048}), but you can't see the numbers on the side of it. How long can you hold it above your head like this?\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u4e0a\u306e\u3072\u3087\u3046\u3092\u307f\u3066\u304f\u3060\u3055\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "Please look at the chart above."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u30d5\u30ed\u30a2\u306e\u4e09\u304b\u3044\u4e0a\u306b\u30c8\u30fc\u30d5\u30b0\u306e\u30aa\u30d5\u30a3\u30b9\u304c\u3042\u308a\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "The Tofugu office is three floors up."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u304f\u3064\u4e0b\u306e\u304b\u305f\u307b\u3046\u306f\u3001\u304b\u3044\u3060\u3093\u306e\u4e0a\u306b\u3042\u3063\u305f\u308f\u3088\u3002",
                    "en": "One of your socks was at the top of the stairs."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You've hopefully already learned that {span:lang=ja:\u529b} means \"power\". You might have also heard the word {span:lang=ja:\u3044\u3063\u3071\u3044}, because it's used a lot in conversation. It has a few meanings (you'll learn more of them later) but the important one here is \"full\" or \"a lot\". Put these two words together and you have {tooltip:Vocabulary:full power}, {tooltip:Vocabulary:with all one\u2019s strength}, and {tooltip:Vocabulary:as hard as one can}. Remember this one with all your strength!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "With All One's Strength",
                "Alternatives": "Full Power, As Hard As One Can",
                "Word Type": "adverb, expression"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3061\u304b\u3089\u3044\u3063\u3071\u3044"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3061\u304b\u3089\u3044\u3063\u3071\u3044",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "44548-subject-8958.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3061\u304b\u3089\u3044\u3063\u3071\u3044",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "44127-subject-8958.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}{span:lang=ja:\u529b} uses the same reading as you learned with the vocabulary. That's because this is basically two separate words stuck together.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u529b\u3044\u3063\u3071\u3044\u304c\u3093\u3070\u308a\u307e\u3057\u305f\uff01",
                    "en": "We did our best!"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30c9\u30a2\u3092\u529b\u3044\u3063\u3071\u3044\u304a\u3057\u3066\u304f\u3060\u3055\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "Please push on the door as hard as you can."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30ea\u30ec\u30fc\u3067\u306f\u529b\u3044\u3063\u3071\u3044\u306f\u3057\u308a\u307e\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "I ran as fast as I could in the relay."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "When a kanji is alone and doesn't have any okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) it's usually going to take the same meaning as the kanji it comes from. So in this case, you know that this word means {tooltip:Vocabulary:woman}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Woman",
                "Alternative": "Female",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u304a\u3093\u306a"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u304a\u3093\u306a",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "3117-subject-2503.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u304a\u3093\u306a",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "27978-subject-2503.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}When a word is a single kanji with no okurigana (hiragana attached to the kanji) it tends to use a kun'yomi reading. You didn't learn the kun'yomi reading when you learned the kanji itself, so let's use a mnemonic to remember the vocab word.\n{splitParent}There's a woman in front of you. Who is she? She's the woman who lives {tooltip:Reading:on Ni}nth ({span:lang=ja:\u304a\u3093\u306a}) Street! \n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u3042\u306e\u5973\u306f\u3060\u308c\uff1f",
                    "en": "Who is that woman?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u308f\u305f\u3057\u306f\u5973\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "I'm a woman."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u304a\u308c\u306f\u3053\u3069\u3082\u3092\u305b\u308f\u3059\u308b\u5973\u304c\u307b\u3057\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "I want a woman who will help look after my children."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's a cut right at the base of this tree. Why would someone cut down a tree? To make a {tooltip:Radical:book} out of it! Imagine living in the old days when you'd have to cut down a tree yourself to write or read a book.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Book"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical looks like a fancy composite {tooltip:Radical:bow} people would use to shoot at targets and definitely not any normal living animals. See how the middle part is being pulled back? Imagine holding this bow and firing it at a target shaped like a giant alligator-crab monster.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Bow"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical is made up of two barbs and one ground radical. If there are barbs on the ground, whom (especially) do you want to avoid that area? You want your {tooltip:Radical:child} to avoid it, since children are so careless and often don't wear shoes. Imagine how bad you'd feel if you let a child walk around on top of these barbs! Pretty bad, I imagine.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Child"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's a cross and a gun, with the gun going through the cross. You're using the cross to stabilize your gun, because you need to shoot a {tooltip:Radical:cow} for dinner. You bring your cross because you need to bless the cow once you've killed it (and it's also a good stand for your gun). Poor cow.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Cow"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's a cross drawn on the ground, making this {tooltip:Radical:dirt}. You can draw anything in the dirt, it just happens to be a cross this time. Imagine drawing a cross in the dirt with your finger. Feel the dirt get stuck under your fingernail and never forget.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Dirt"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Remember the big guy that helped you remember the big radical? Now the big guy has something on his shoulder... what is that? You look and see that he has a {tooltip:Radical:dog} up there, perched as if it were a parrot or a cat or something. Dogs aren't supposed to be up on people's shoulders like that, but for him? It works!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Dog"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Do you see the prison and drop radicals making up this radical? This prison is holding onto a drop because it's the {tooltip:Radical:evening}. Evening air does a better job of holding on to drops of liquid like this, which is why everything is covered in dew in the morning.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Evening"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "See the iris in the middle of the eyeball? Remember that there are no round things in kanji, so square things take their place. That makes this a pretty realistic, square {tooltip:Radical:eye}. You just have to squint with your real eyes a bit.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Eye"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "See the person radical here? It's got things coming off of it... what are those things\u2014OH MY GOD IT'S {tooltip:Radical:FIRE}!!! Help, help! Put the fire out!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Fire"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "If you use your imagination, this radical actually looks like the number \"5.\" You have to remove the vertical line in the bottom left, but once you do that, you have an easy way to remember that this radical means {tooltip:Radical:five}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Five"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "See the {tooltip:Radical:flag} in this radical? There's a pole with a flag on the end of it, blowing in the wind. Basically, this radical looks just like a flag.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Flag"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "These three streaks flowing off to the side are three strands of hair. Pretty easy to see and remember, right?",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Hair"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This one's a little tough to picture, but I believe in you. The barb in the middle is the wrist/arm. Then, there are four regular fingers coming off of it and then one big thumb (the slide radical at the top). So you have four fingers, a thumb, and a wrist. This is what makes up your crazy looking {tooltip:Radical:hand}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Hand"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This is the kind of {tooltip:Radical:head} a child might draw\u2014can you imagine it with eyes, nose, mouth, and ears too? Whoever's head this is, it's really square. Maybe it's the head of a {link#https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SpongeBob_SquarePants_(character)#famous spongy cartoon character}. Hmm.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Head"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There is a two radical and a person radical. The first level of a person's life is on earth. The second level (this level, which is why there's a two radical here) is in heaven. So when you see a person at level 2, you know they're in {tooltip:Radical:heaven}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Heaven"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical is not something anyone can have. Only a {tooltip:Radical:king} can wear this crown upon his head. This radical is the sign of kings. Imagine your favorite king wearing this on their head. It's a pretty distinctive shape\u2014I think you can do it.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "King"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical already looks like a pair of {tooltip:Radical:legs}. There's even feet on these legs (unlike the fins radical, which you learned earlier). That's how you know these are legs and not fins (or something else).",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Legs"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's a stick going right through the {tooltip:Radical:middle} of someone's mouth! Imagine that mouth being yours. Measure the placement of the stick. Perfectly aligned, right in the middle. That's amazing, though to be honest, you probably have bigger problems than measuring the location of this stick in your mouth.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Middle"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:moon} is actually made of the sun, but it has little legs coming off of it leaning towards the left. Why? Because the moon is always running away from the sun as fast as it can. It doesn't want to get caught on fire, so it has to run to stay ahead!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Moon"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical looks like the katakana character {span:lang=ja:\u30ca} (na), which we're going to say stands for {tooltip:Radical:narwhal}. Plus, this radical kind of looks like a narwhal, right? The body and head on the bottom, and the single majestic narwhal horn coming out the top, shooting up out of the water and into the air. Oh, and don't confuse this radical with treasure. The sideways line for narwhal ({span:lang=ja:\u30ca}) is horizontal, the sideways line for treasure is at more of a downward angle ({span:lang=ja:\u30e1}), like an \"X.\"",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Narwhal"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This is a little triangle that's almost completely closed off. When you want to be alone, you hide in a place where you can be in {tooltip:Radical:private}. That's why this is the private radical. You're closing yourself off to the rest of the world, with just a little peephole.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Private"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Rice paddies are made up of fields with irrigation lines going between them. See the four rice paddies with water flowing between and around them? That's why this radical is {tooltip:Radical:rice paddy}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Rice Paddy"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You have fins and you have a barb. A fish that you can stick an entire barb through like this would have to be really {tooltip:Radical:small}, since it's sticking through the whole fish body (which you can imagine by basing the body off the size of the fins compared to the already small barb.)",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Small"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Two sticks are propping up a lid. By doing this, they're making it {tooltip:Radical:stand} up.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Stand"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's a leaf growing out of a square thing (which is normally a mouth, but not this time). The leaf is growing out of a {tooltip:Radical:stone}. I mean, obviously, because leaves don't grow out of mouths. I hope. Anyway, picture a stone in the middle of nature with a leaf growing out of it or just behind it and you'll never forget this radical!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Stone"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This looks just like a fold-out {tooltip:Radical:stool}, amiright?",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Stool"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's a giant toe and a stick in the ground in front of you. You're driving your car toward them, but you don't see them until your lights hit them. What would these things cause you to do? Most likely {tooltip:Radical:stop} your car right away.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Stop"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's a barb under the ground, holding it in place. This is actually a {tooltip:Radical:street}. All streets have to be secured to the ground with barbs so that they don't slide all over the place. If you tried to pick up the asphalt streets we have today, you'd still find barbs under the ground to keep them there. The more you know! \u2b50",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Street"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "If you look at this radical, it looks like the bottom part of a {tooltip:Radical:sword}\u2014the handle, more specifically. This radical also looks a lot like the power radical ({span:lang=ja:\u529b}), so don't get them confused. It's missing the little nubbin at the top, because 90% of the power comes from the sword to cut your enemies down (which is why the sword radical is 90% of the power radical).",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Sword"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You're in a room, surrounded by slides. Down off the slides tumble cross after cross after cross. They're bouncing in and hitting you in the face, the arm, everywhere... With each cross comes a voice yelling, \"ONE! TWO! THREE HUNDRED! FIVE HUNDRED!\" and so on. You are worried it will never end, because you're starting to drown in the crosses. Then the voice yells out ONE {tooltip:Radical:THOUSAND} and they stop. Still, you can't move. Think about all the numbers being called out, and keep wondering if it'll be the last one. Feel the relief you'd feel if it stops on a thousand, because you won't be buried anymore. The thousand crosses are covering you entirely, over your head, but because they're crosses you can still breathe.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Thousand"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical is {tooltip:Radical:treasure} because \"X marks the spot,\" as they say. Doesn't it look like the X on a pirate treasure map? You want to go find that treasure now, right?!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Treasure"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There are drops of liquid coming out of both sides of this barb. You can imagine the barb being stuck into the ground then pulled out. When it is, {tooltip:Radical:water} comes gushing out of both sides! That's why this radical is water.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Water"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Add a drop of the sun to something and it will turn it {tooltip:Radical:white}. The sun is really strong, so even a single drop will burn anything white. Why do you think so many beaches are white? Even the drops of sunblock you use to stop the sun's rays is white. So when you think of sun drops, think white.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "White"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This looks like a stool with a lid on it, but it's actually a {tooltip:Radical:writing} desk. Do you see it? It's a nice, quiet place for writing. You can even see the pen sticking up in the middle. Wouldn't you want to do your writing here?",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Writing"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:king} has something in his hand that he's {tooltip:Radical:drop}ping... but it bounces too! He's bouncing a {tooltip:Kanji:ball}",
            "hints": "This kanji looks just like a king bouncing a {tooltip:Kanji:ball}, no? If not, imagine the kanji itself bouncing the drop (the {tooltip:Kanji:ball}) over and over again, then laying some hoops like a pro.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Ball"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304e\u3087\u304f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f\u307e",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "So, who's in a ball? Which \"Tom\" did you choose?",
                "mnemonic": "Think of someone curled up in a ball. It is someone you know / someone famous named \u201cTom\u201d ({span:lang=ja:\u305f\u307e})."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The kanji and the radical are the same, so remembering the meaning of this kanji is as simple as making sure you know the radical for {tooltip:Radical:book} first! If you know the radical, you know this kanji means {tooltip:Kanji:book} as well.",
            "hints": "Make sure you know those radicals, yo! And good luck with all the meanings (sorry sorry sorry).",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Book",
                "Alternatives": "Origin, Real, Main"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307b\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3082\u3068",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Just think about books and how you use them to hone your skills. Or read a book about honing things. Like swords.",
                "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Kanji:Book}s are one of the best ways to {tooltip:Reading:hone} ({span:lang=ja:\u307b\u3093}) your intellect, you know?"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:child} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:child} kanji are exactly the same.",
            "hints": "So, if you know your radicals, you know your kanji as well! This won't last forever (where radicals are exactly the same as the kanji) so enjoy it while the kanji are still small and simple!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Child",
                "Alternative": "Kid"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057, \u3059",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3053",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "The child runs away and you chase after it. Every time it's about to turn a corner it looks like a sheep in a suit again, but whenever you yell and point, it turns back. You're the only one who can see this sheep suit child!!",
                "mnemonic": "To remember the sounds  {span:lang=ja:\u3057} and {span:lang=ja:\u3059} we'll use these mnemonics:"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "What's something with {tooltip:Radical:nine} sides? Just add a {tooltip:Radical:drop} and you'll have something that's almost a {tooltip:Kanji:circle}.",
            "hints": "It's not quite a {tooltip:Kanji:circle} though. You just need to add a {tooltip:Radical:drop} of liquid to the edges to smooth it out to become a full circle.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Circle",
                "Alternatives": "Circular, Round"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304c\u3093",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307e\u308b",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Obviously if you \"don't know this reading\" well enough, you should watch some more Maru videos. I'll understand... just make sure you come back someday, okay?",
                "mnemonic": "Do you know about the adorable cat Maru? If not, you should {link#http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=z_AbfPXTKms#check it out}. May seem like a distraction, but if you come back knowing the reading for this kanji, then it's totally worth while."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There is a line on the {tooltip:Radical:ground} so you {tooltip:Radical:stop} and a giant sign pops up that says {tooltip:Kanji:CORRECT}! You obeyed the rules of the road and did the correct thing.",
            "hints": "Imagine walking, staring at the ground until you come across a line that you're supposed to stop at. If you stop, the big \"correct\" sign appears, chiming a happy tune.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Correct"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305b\u3044, \u3057\u3087\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f\u3060, \u307e\u3055",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Being correct got the Shougun to notice you AND a new saber. You better make sure to answer this kanji correctly in your reviews too, so you can keep being correct and have more nice things happen.",
                "mnemonic": "You did the {tooltip:Kanji:correct} thing, so the {tooltip:Reading:Shou}gun ({span:lang=ja:\u3057\u3087\u3046}) shows up to reward you! He holds out a shiny new {tooltip:Reading:sa}ber ({span:lang=ja:\u305b\u3044}) in his hand, gently taps it onto each of your shoulders, and then hands it to you. It's big, curved and you love it."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:dirt} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:dirt} kanji are the same!",
            "hints": "As long as you know the radical dirt, you know the kanji dirt.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Dirt",
                "Alternatives": "Soil, Earth, Ground"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3069, \u3068",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3064\u3061",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Dogs and dirt just go together!",
                "mnemonic": "Who spends a lot of time in {tooltip:Kanji:dirt}? {tooltip:Reading:Do}gs ({span:lang=ja:\u3069}) do! Dogs dig in dirt, they roll in dirt, sometimes they try to eat dirt (which isn't so great)."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:dog} radical and {tooltip:Kanji:dog} kanji are exactly the same!",
            "hints": "Soon there will be very few kanji/radical-same combos ... so enjoy it while you can.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Dog"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3051\u3093",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3044\u306c",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "There's a good chance you know the word for dog in Japanese already too. If so, you're ahead of the game. If not, do your best to learn the reading before moving on.",
                "mnemonic": "You see an emu running towards you from off in the distance (strange to see an emu in these parts...). As it gets closer, you realize that it's not an emu at all, it's something that sounds the same, or similar... an {tooltip:Reading:inu}!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:evening} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:evening} kanji are the same. If you know the radical you know the meaning of the kanji.",
            "hints": "Do what you're meant to do. Study those radicals!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Evening"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305b\u304d",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3086\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Young people love staying up the entire evening doing youth-people things, and it's really obnoxious for us curmudgeonly folk.{splitParent}Note: All the {span:lang=ja:\u3086\u3046} readings will use the word \"youths\" for the reading mnemonic. You can think of these \"youths\" as a group of younger people who cause a lot of the problems in this world, just like how people treat millennials nowadays.",
                "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Kanji:evening} is when all the {tooltip:Reading:you}ths ({span:lang=ja:\u3086\u3046}) come outside to play."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There are two {tooltip:Radical:mountains} in front of you... and probably a bunch more around you too. You are in a pass, and don't know how to get out. You are frantically looking for the {tooltip:Kanji:exit} before you freeze to death. The most important thing is to find the exit from this mountain range - your life depends on it.",
            "hints": "In your imagination, really feel like your life depends on getting out. The important part is the feeling of \"needing to get out of the mountain pass.\" If you have this feeling, you'll be more likely to remember that this kanji means exit, because that's what you want to do when you see this kanji.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Exit"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3085\u3064",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3067, \u3060",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Focus on the surprise of realizing you're being hunted while trying to find the exit to the mountain range, then focus on the next part, where you get shot. View it in your head as the hunter shooting you. Powww!",
                "mnemonic": "As you're trying to {tooltip:Kanji:exit} this mountain range, you suddenly realize that someone is actually hunting you. What? Make sure to feel that sudden realization. It's crazy. Then, you find the exit and run towards it. Sadly, the hunter is waiting there, and he {tooltip:Reading:shoots you} ({span:lang=ja:\u3057\u3085\u3064}) through the leg, knocking you down."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:eye} radical and {tooltip:Kanji:eye} kanji are the same as each other, so now you know them both.",
            "hints": "Well... you'll know them both as long as you studied your radicals, young grasshopper. If you didn't, then you're just a young grasshopper who needs to study their radicals.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Eye"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3082\u304f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3081",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Of course, feel the pain of your eye being gone... and struggle to get it back. You eventually give up because the sheep is too fast, but then you come up with an idea. What if I call out back to the sheep in its own language? Go ahead and cover one eye and start making \"MEHHH\" noises to see if you can get the sheep to come back to you and give you your eye. It's up to you whether it works or not, but be sure to feel frustrated by its reaction, or happy by it. Either one works.",
                "mnemonic": "Your {tooltip:Kanji:eyes} are important to you, right? Sadly, one of your eyes is missing right now, because a sheep grabbed it and pulled it out, and is now running away from you, yelling his sheep call.... \"{tooltip:Reading:Mehhhhhh} ({span:lang=ja:\u3081})!\""
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Think about it this way. There is a famous fishing lure inventor, working on his best work ever. He\u2019s old, and he\u2019s been trying to make the best fishing lure for the last 50 years, and knows this will be his last invention. Then he figures it out. He takes one {tooltip:Radical:barb}, and connects the end of it to the top of another {tooltip:Radical:barb}. When he does this, he knows his work is finally {tooltip:Kanji:finish}ed.",
            "hints": "Make sure you look at this amazing fishing lure too - hold it in your hand, see the two barbs fused together. Think about how strange this lure is. Is this really the best in the world? Will this really finish all other fishing lures?",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Finish",
                "Alternatives": "Complete, End"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u308a\u3087\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Make him think that he can\u2019t just use his new lure on the side of the lake / ocean / whatever. He has to get out there, but unfortunately all he has is a crappy row boat. Imagine the row boat being terrible, leaky, and dangerous, which is why you\u2019re surprised when he goes for it. That\u2019s a terrible row boat to use! If you do this, you'll focus your mind on the rowboat part of things, which will help you to remember the reading of this kanji.",
                "mnemonic": "How does the fish lure maker test his newly {tooltip:Kanji:finish}ed lure out? The one he just finished? He gets in a {tooltip:Reading:row} ({span:lang=ja:\u308a\u3087\u3046}) boat and rows out into the sea."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:fire} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:fire} kanji are the same as each other.",
            "hints": "If you know the radicals then this one is EZPZ.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Fire"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304b",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3072, \u307b",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Oh no... Oh no!! If you don't have a {tooltip:Reading:car}, imagine what it'd feel like if your car just exploded and burned down. If you do have a car, imagine your own car in this situation. It should be a terrible feeling, if you imagine it well enough. So, when you think {tooltip:Kanji:fire}, remember the fear you had when your car was given to the flames. Now you have to walk everywhere, sad :(",
                "mnemonic": "Whenever we need to remember {span:lang=ja:\u304b}, we use the word {tooltip:Reading:car}."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:five} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:five} kanji are the same.",
            "hints": "Know your radicals, friend-o.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Five"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3054",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3044\u3064",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "One, two, three, four, five\u2014GODZILLA IS HERE GO GO, RUN!",
                "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Kanji:Five} is the number of buildings {tooltip:Reading:Go}dzilla ({span:lang=ja:\u3054}) will knock down. Oh, look! There he goes now."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Imagine contorting yourself in such a way that your {tooltip:Radical:legs} end up inside your {tooltip:Radical:mouth}. You have bent your body in all the wrong ways in order to achieve this. In fact, if you look at yourself, you look like a square now... You're just a person with {tooltip:Kanji:four} sides.",
            "hints": "If you can get to square, you should be able to get to four as well, since squares always have four sides.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Four"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3088\u3093, \u3088",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "What's better than one four sided sheep?",
                "mnemonic": "This amazing feat, which turned you into a {tooltip:Kanji:four} sided square, has you looking like a four sided animal: the {tooltip:Reading:shee}p ({span:lang=ja:\u3057}). Look at yourself in the mirror and see an adorable four-sided sheep looking back at you. (You can't see the other sides because mirrors aren't 3D and whatnot. So you're a bit cartoonish.)"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "On the {tooltip:Radical:ground} you put {tooltip:Radical:barb}s at the bottom of a {tooltip:Radical:slide} because you're a {tooltip:Kanji:genius} trying to catch another {tooltip:Kanji:genius}.",
            "hints": "Imagine yourself placing the barbs in front of the slide, thinking about how your genius plan will surely catch another genius.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Genius"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3055\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Cyborgs have brain implants, so they're definitely all geniuses.",
                "mnemonic": "You check your {tooltip:Kanji:genius} trap, and there's a genius stuck in the barbs! You know it's a genius because they're actually a {tooltip:Reading:cy}borg ({span:lang=ja:\u3055\u3044})."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:hand} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:hand} kanji are identical in looks and meaning! That should make things easy!",
            "hints": "Make sure you're studying your radicals. It won't be forever that radicals and kanji overlap so nicely like this!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Hand"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3085, \u305a",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3066",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "You try to shake your hands off, but it's no good. The televisions are stuck, and the volume won't go down. You have no idea how you got these televisions on your hands. All you know is that you want them off right this instant.",
                "mnemonic": "To remember the reading {span:lang=ja:\u3066} we will use the word \"Television.\" You have great hands. Go ahead and look at them. OH MY GOD. You have little {tooltip:Reading:te}levisions ({span:lang=ja:\u3066}) on your hands. They are playing the dumbest show you can think of, too, both hands at the same time."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The radical {tooltip:Radical:heaven} is the same as the kanji {tooltip:Kanji:heaven}, making this an easy kanji to memorize.",
            "hints": "If you know your radicals well (and you should!) you know the meaning of this kanji well, as well!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Heaven"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3066\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3042\u307e",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine yourself in line to get into heaven. You're super worried, because you're not sure if you'll get in. You heard there was a one in {tooltip:Reading:ten} chance... those aren't good odds. Feel nervous in your imagination. Feel worried. Feel the pressure as you get closer and closer to the gate... will you make it?? Ten percent!",
                "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Kanji:Heaven}... nowadays, people aren't all that good. Things have gotten bad. That's why the acceptance rate to get into heaven is only {tooltip:Reading:ten} ({span:lang=ja:\u3066\u3093}) percent!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:king} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:king} kanji are the same!",
            "hints": "Hopefully you're a {tooltip:Kanji:king} of radicals... because if you are, this kanji was super easy for you!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "King"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304a\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "You need to be polite when you talk to the king and add \"Oh\" to the beginning, otherwise the king will be pretty angry and bad things might happen.{splitParent}We're going to use the {span:lang=ja:\u738b} kanji for all {span:lang=ja:\u304a\u3046} mnemonics from now on. So make sure you always remember to say {span:lang=ja:\u304a\u3046} King!",
                "mnemonic": "When you see the {tooltip:Kanji:king} you need to say, \"{tooltip:Reading:Oh} ({span:lang=ja:\u304a\u3046}) King! Oh King, oh no, oh why are you looking at me like that? Oh King... you aren't going to chop off my head are you? OH NO!\""
            }
        }

{
