                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3064\u304d",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "This one might not be as memorable at first, but if you think of the moon, and think about what people do when the moon is up, you'll come to sleeping. Then naturally you can think about what happens when the moon goes down... people {tooltip:Reading:gets} up!",
                "mnemonic": "Since {span:lang=ja:\u3052\u3064} and {span:lang=ja:\u304c\u3064} are quite similar, we'll use one mnemonic to cover both of them. I expect your experience learning Japanese throughout the future will help you to memorize and differentiate between the two. When the moon goes down and goes away, everyone in the world {tooltip:Reading:gets} ({span:lang=ja:\u3052\u3064}) up, because that means it's going to be daytime soon!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This is a strange kanji because it doesn't really mean anything at all. In fact, what it does is it repeats the kanji that comes before it. For example: {span:lang=ja:\u4eba\u3005} is actually {span:lang=ja:\u4eba\u4eba}. This kanji shows up whenever there's a repeating kanji, because it's often easier to write than writing a more complicated kanji twice. That's why this kanji is the {tooltip:Kanji:repeater}. It repeats what comes before it.",
            "hints": "Since this kanji doesn't really have a meaning as much as it does a function, try to change the way you think about it when you're memorizing it.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Repeater",
                "Alternatives": "Repetition, Reduplication, Iteration Mark"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306e\u307e",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "If you feel like you're so full you're about to burst, and if you imagine your mom doing this to you (how could you, ma?), you should be able to remember the reading of this kanji. That being said, the reading is mostly only useful in the event that you have to identify a {span:lang=ja:\u3005}\u2014otherwise, you won't see it much.",
                "mnemonic": "In Japanese, this symbol is often called the {span:lang=ja:\u306e\u307e} because it looks like the katakana characters {span:lang=ja:\u30ce} and {span:lang=ja:\u30de} put together. If you know katakana then this will be easy to read. If you don't, think of it this way: "
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:rice paddy} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:rice paddy} kanji are the same, making this one super easy to remember, at least meaning-wise.",
            "hints": "Just know your radicals, and you will know your kanji as well.",
