            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The radical {tooltip:Radical:stand} and the kanji {tooltip:Kanji:stand} are the same thing, so now you know them both.",
            "hints": "If you're keeping up with your radical studies, you're also keeping up with your kanji studies!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Stand"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u308a\u3064, \u308a\u3085\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine the doormen being extra straight\u2026 too straight in their standing. On their uniform is \u201cThe Ritz\u201d written in fine lettering, and as you walk past them, they stand up straight and say \u201cWelcome to the Ritz!\u201d Whatever you do, while you\u2019re thinking about it, make sure you think about how they stand up so straight, and associate it with these doormen, who work, specifically, at the Ritz. If you effectively imagine all this, you should be able to remember this on\u2019yomi. Go through it like it\u2019s a movie in your head!",
                "mnemonic": "Think of someone who has to {tooltip:Kanji:stand} up straight for you. These people are pros in hospitality. These are the doormen at the {tooltip:Reading:Ritz} ({span:lang=ja:\u308a\u3064})."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The kanji and the radical that look like this are exactly the same. They are both {tooltip:Kanji:street}.",
            "hints": "As long as you know the radical street, you know the kanji street.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Street"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3061\u3087\u3046, \u3066\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Mrs. {tooltip:Reading:Chou} is a character you'll see a lot throughout WaniKani, anytime the reading is {span:lang=ja:\u3061\u3087\u3046} like this one. A lore will develop around her, so the more vividly you can imagine your Mrs. Chou right now, the easier things will get later. In general, she's a mean, evil woman, so as long as you imagine her within those parameters, you're good. And, when I say she is a \"street-woman\" in the mnemonic, I don't mean she is homeless. She has a nice, middle-class home that's not outside of her financial means. What I'm saying is she wanders the streets looking for evil things to do. These are HER streets. So whenever you see the street radical, know that Mrs. Chou is probably going to show up!",
                "mnemonic": "So you're walking down this {tooltip:Kanji:street}, and off in the distance you see the silhouette of somebody. A lady. Big, black curly hair and red eyes. It is the {tooltip:Kanji:street}-woman, Mrs. {tooltip:Reading:Chou} ({span:lang=ja:\u3061\u3087\u3046})."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:sun} radical and {tooltip:Kanji:sun} kanji are the same, huzzah! Also, if you want to remember {tooltip:Kanji:day} as well, you can just think about how a day is the time it takes for the sun to cross the sky.",
            "hints": "The radical for this one is easy too, making the meaning for this kanji super easy as well!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Sun",
                "Alternative": "Day"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306b\u3061, \u3058\u3064",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3072, \u304b, \u3073",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "This reading will be easier to remember if you know of Friedrich Nietzsche... so do your research and learn about him if you don't. He's a pretty historical dude. Imagine the sun totally pissed off, too. It makes a grunting noise as it builds up a sun beam to blast him off the face of the earth.{splitParent}Also note that there's another reading worth learning as well: {span:lang=ja:\u3058\u3064}. The reading can be remembered by thinking \"{tooltip:Reading:Gee! Two} readings?\" Because, seriously, two readings? Too many...",
                "mnemonic": "There are so many on'yomi for this kanji, but since this kanji is so important and common, you'll end up learning them all eventually whether you want to or not. For now, you're only going to have to learn one of the readings, and that one is {span:lang=ja:\u306b\u3061}. "
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The radical and the meaning of this kanji are almost the same! The radical is {tooltip:Radical:sword}, but we're learning Japanese, so can you name a Japanese {tooltip:Kanji:sword}? That's right, the most common is a {tooltip:Kanji:katana}, which happens to be the meaning of this kanji as well.",
            "hints": "The progression on this one is important. When you think \"sword\" you have to think, \"What kinds of swords do Japanese people have?\" Then you'll reach the meaning of this kanji on your own.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Sword",
                "Alternative": "Katana"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3068\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304b\u305f\u306a",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine trying to travel to {span:lang=ja:\u3068\u3046\u304d\u3087\u3046} with your katana. Airport security is not happy with you trying to take a katana on the plane, so you get a full cavity search. I hope you don't have any other swords hidden anywhere.",
                "mnemonic": "So now you have this sweet {tooltip:Kanji:katana} in your hands and you're swinging it around like an idiot. Feel free to make whooshing noises as you swing it around. To become a master katana wielder, you decide to travel to {span:lang=ja:\u3068\u3046\u304d\u3087\u3046} (That's Tokyo!). {span:lang=ja:\u3068\u3046\u304d\u3087\u3046} has the best sword and katana schools in the world, so it makes sense to travel to {span:lang=ja:\u3068\u3046\u304d\u3087\u3046}."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This kanji looks like and means the same thing as the radical, which makes it particularly easy to remember.",
            "hints": "As long as you know the radical {tooltip:Radical:thousand} then you know the kanji {tooltip:Kanji:thousand} as well!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Thousand"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305b\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3061",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "But, the problem is even though you have a {tooltip:Kanji:thousand} {tooltip:Reading:cents} in your pocket, you can't get them out. You're stuck under all these crosses. So, your savior just shrugs and runs off, he won't help you anymore. Feel the desperateness of the situation to help yourself remember the kanji better. Hear the coins falling on the ground.",
                "mnemonic": "Remember the thousand crosses you got stuck under while learning the radical {span:lang=ja:\u5343}? You're still under it. Then, suddenly, someone pops his head over you and says \"Hey, I'll save you... but only for a {tooltip:Kanji:thousand} {tooltip:Reading:cents} ({span:lang=ja:\u305b\u3093}). Go ahead and try to calculate what that would come out to. It's not that expensive, actually."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This is another kanji that is the same as the radical. Both {tooltip:Radical:tree} and {tooltip:Kanji:tree} are the same in looks and meaning.",
            "hints": "So, how are those radicals doing? Do you know them well?",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Tree",
                "Alternative": "Wood"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3082\u304f, \u307c\u304f",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304d, \u3053",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Feel the shame, feel the irritation. Make yourself want to run away from those dumb trees that are mocking you.",
                "mnemonic": "You\u2019re walking through the trees, looking at them. They look ominous. Suddenly, you trip and fall. The trees start to {tooltip:Reading:mock} you. Even the mocking birds mock you. It\u2019s terrible. It\u2019s painful. You\u2019re being mocked as you pull yourself up off the ground."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:water} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:water} kanji are the same.",
            "hints": "So as long as you know the radicals, you know the kanji.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Water"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3059\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307f\u305a",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "I know that was a long one, but try to imagine all the different bits. Try to feel the feelings (cold, smell, etc) too. The more you create in this fake memory (which you\u2019re using to remember and associate water with {span:lang=ja:\u3059\u3044} (swing)) the more likely you are to end up remembering enough to make it happen. Especially when it comes to the senses part, the more vividly you imagined it, the more likely you are to remember the on\u2019yomi.",
                "mnemonic": "Imagine a lake (i.e. a whole bunch of {tooltip:Kanji:water}). When you think of water, you should try to think of this beautiful lake. It\u2019s sunny, the trees smell nice (imagine a tree smell if you can \u2013 the more senses you use the better!) and on that tree there is an awesome tire {tooltip:Reading:swi}ng ({span:lang=ja:\u3059\u3044}). You get onto the tire swing and you are swinging back and forth. The water is right in front of you, and you are able to use the swing to throw yourself into the water. It\u2019s a lot colder than you expected, and you come back to the surface, gasping for breath. The water gets into your mouth and you taste it. How strange, it\u2019s very, very {tooltip:Reading:swee}t. Imagine the sweetness of the water and how weird it is (really really sweet, this water is)."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The radical {tooltip:Radical:white} and the kanji {tooltip:Kanji:white} are the same.",
            "hints": "Study your radicals hard! Most important thing!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "White"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306f\u304f",
