{
            "mnemonic": "The mouth you exit out of is just a doorway (I hope), but an easier way to describe this word or concept is to call it an {tooltip:Vocabulary:exit}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Exit",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3067\u3050\u3061"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3067\u3050\u3061",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "21301-subject-2558.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3067\u3050\u3061",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "30479-subject-2558.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}This is a jukugo word, which usually means on'yomi readings from the kanji. {span:lang=ja:\u51fa\u53e3} is an exception to this rule, however, and uses its kun'yomi readings. There's no particular reason why this is, but oftentimes a \"body part\" word (like mouth) changes a word to use kun'yomi readings instead of on'yomi ones. You already know the kun'yomi reading for {span:lang=ja:\u53e3} ({span:lang=ja:\u304f\u3061}) from learning the vocab, but to remember the reading for {span:lang=ja:\u51fa} you can think the following:\n{splitParent}If I exit out this mouth, I'll end up in the ocean (you were swallowed by a whale) and I will surely end up {tooltip:Reading:dea}d ({span:lang=ja:\u3067}).\n{splitParent}Also keep in mind that because of rendaku, {span:lang=ja:\u304f\u3061} becomes {span:lang=ja:\u3050\u3061}.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u305d\u3053\u306f\u51fa\u53e3\u3067\u306f\u3042\u308a\u307e\u305b\u3093\u3002",
                    "en": "That is not an exit."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3075\u3058\u5c71\u306b\u306f\u3072\u307f\u3064\u306e\u5165\u308a\u53e3\u3068\u51fa\u53e3\u304c\u3042\u308b\u3089\u3057\u3044\u3067\u3059\u3088\u3002",
                    "en": "I heard that Mt. Fuji has a secret entrance and exit."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u304d\u3085\u3046\u3058\u3087\u3046\u306e\u51fa\u53e3\u306f\u3042\u3061\u3089\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "This baseball stadium\u2019s exit is over there."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

