{
            "mnemonic": "The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Right Direction",
                "Alternative": "Right",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u307f\u304e"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u307f\u304e",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "30389-subject-2559.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u307f\u304e",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "21235-subject-2559.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it uses the kun'yomi reading. You haven't learned this reading yet, so here's a mnemonic to help you:\n{splitParent}Look to the {tooltip:Vocabulary:right}. There are {tooltip:Reading:me}at {tooltip:Reading:gee}se ({span:lang=ja:\u307f\u304e}) over there. What are meat geese, you ask? They're geese made of meat, that sit to the right of you, trying to get you to do bad things. Like the devil/angel motif you see in cartoons. Well there's nothing on your left. And on your right? Just meat geese. Watching you. Waiting. Geese made of meat. Telling you not to do the right thing.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u53f3\u306e\u3057\u3083\u3057\u3093\u306f\u3001\u3068\u3046\u304d\u3087\u3046\u3067\u3068\u3063\u305f\u3057\u3083\u3057\u3093\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "The photo on the right was taken in Tokyo."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u53f3\u306b\u306f\u738b\u306e\u597d\u304d\u306a\u30e1\u30a4\u30c9\u304c\u4e94\u5341\u4eba\u3001\u3072\u3060\u308a\u306b\u306f\u738b\u5b50\u306e\u3059\u3046\u306f\u3044\u3059\u308b\u5929\u624d\u304b\u304c\u304f\u3057\u3083\u304c\u4e00\u4eba\u3044\u308b\u3002",
                    "en": "On the right are fifty of the king's favorite maids and on the left is a genius scientist who worships the prince."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3046\u307e\u304c\u53f3\u306b\u307e\u304c\u3063\u305f\u3068\u304d\u3001\u30ed\u30d0\u306f\u53f3\u3092\u30c1\u30e9\u30ea\u3068\u307f\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "When the horse turned to the right, the donkey glanced right as well."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This word is made up of a single kanji with hiragana attached to it. That hiragana ends with {span:lang=ja:\u3044} which means it's an adjective. So, what's the adjective form of the kanji that's in this word? {span:lang=ja:\u5186} means both \"yen\" and \"round\" but in this case we're looking at the round one (because something isn't going to be \"yenny\"). The adjective version of round is {tooltip:Vocabulary:round} or {tooltip:Vocabulary:circular}. Just like a yen coin, this \"round\" is a flat round. As in, if you draw a circle on a piece of paper, you could use this word. Same goes with yen, which is why \"yen\" and \"round\" both use the same kanji.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Round",
                "Alternative": "Circular",
                "Word Type": "\u3044 adjective"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u307e\u308b\u3044"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u307e\u308b\u3044",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "30501-subject-2534.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u307e\u308b\u3044",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "20856-subject-2534.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun'yomi reading. You didn't learn that reading with this kanji, so here's a mnemonic to help you: \n{splitParent}The reading for the {span:lang=ja:\u5186} portion of this word is the same as a kanji you're learning in this level: {span:lang=ja:\u4e38}. That kanji means something really similar to this kanji. And guess what? They also share a reading. So, if you know that the reading of {span:lang=ja:\u4e38} is {span:lang=ja:\u307e\u308b} then you'll also know that the reading for this word is {span:lang=ja:\u307e\u308b}!\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u5186\u3044\u304a\u3055\u3089\u3092\u3064\u304f\u3048\u306b\u306a\u3089\u3079\u3066\u304f\u3060\u3055\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "Please line up the round plates on the desk."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u5186\u3044\u307e\u3069\u304c\u3042\u308b\u306e\u304c\u3001\u738b\u5973\u306e\u3078\u3084\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "The room with the round window is the princess's."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u308f\u305f\u3057\u306f\u5186\u3044\u6708\u304c\u3053\u308f\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "I\u2019m afraid of the full moon."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Vocabulary:Second day} or {tooltip:Vocabulary:day two}. No matter how you look at this combination of kanji, the meaning of the word makes a lot of sense!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Second Day",
                "Alternatives": "Day Two, Two Days",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3075\u3064\u304b"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3075\u3064\u304b",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "30438-subject-7515.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3075\u3064\u304b",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "20833-subject-7515.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}Words that count days use an exceptional reading. Even though this is a jukugo word, it uses kun'yomi readings for both the kanji. Think about this word this way:\n{splitParent}The circus is in town this weekend and you can't wait to get there. You want to show off your new {tooltip:Reading:foots car} ({span:lang=ja:\u3075\u3064\u304b}) to all the circus clowns. But you have to use your feet to get it there and it ends up taking you {tooltip:Vocabulary:two days}! Darn this old fashioned foots car!\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u3089\u3044\u6708\u306e\u4e8c\u65e5\u306b\u3001\u3042\u306d\u3068\u3048\u3044\u304c\u3092\u307f\u306b\u3044\u304d\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "I will go see a movie with my older sister on the second day of next month."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u738b\u5b50\u304c\u5b50\u72ac\u3092\u304a\u3063\u3066\u3075\u3058\u5c71\u306e\u3058\u3085\u304b\u3044\u306b\u304d\u3048\u3066\u304b\u3089\u4e8c\u65e5\u304c\u305f\u3061\u307e\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "It's been two days since the prince followed the puppy and disappeared in the woods of Mt. Fuji."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3072\u3089\u304c\u306a\u306a\u3093\u3066\u3001\u3057\u3093\u3051\u3093\u306b\u3079\u3093\u304d\u3087\u3046\u3059\u308c\u3070\u4e8c\u65e5\u3042\u308c\u3070\u5341\u3076\u3093\u3055\u3002",
                    "en": "If you study diligently, two days is enough to learn hiragana."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

