                        "pronunciation": "\u3058\u3085\u3046\u308d\u304f",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "20892-subject-2530.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}This is a jukugo word, which usually means on'yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you'll know how to read this as well!\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u5341\u516d\u3055\u3044\u306e\u305f\u3093\u3058\u3087\u3046\u3073\u306b\u3075\u3058\u5c71\u306b\u306e\u307c\u308a\u307e\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "I climbed Mt. Fuji on my sixteenth birthday."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3082\u3046\u5341\u516d\u306d\u3093\u3082\u3075\u3058\u5c71\u306e\u5165\u308a\u53e3\u304b\u3089\u3046\u3054\u304b\u306a\u3044\u30a2\u30e1\u30ea\u30ab\u4eba\u304c\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "There is an American who hasn't moved away from the entrance of Mt. Fuji for 16 years."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u308f\u305f\u3057\u306f\u3048\u3044\u3048\u3093\u306e\u5341\u516d\u3055\u3044\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "I am forever sixteen."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "It's the kanji for six and day. Put them together and you have the {tooltip:Vocabulary:sixth day} (or {tooltip:Vocabulary:day six}).",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Sixth Day",
                "Alternatives": "Day Six, Six Days",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3080\u3044\u304b"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3080\u3044\u304b",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "30406-subject-2529.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3080\u3044\u304b",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "20832-subject-2529.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}This is a jukugo word, which usually means on'yomi readings from the kanji. This is an exception, though. When you're counting days (at least one through ten), the kun'yomi readings are used. So let's think about that sixth day - what does it entail?\n{splitParent}The cow ({tooltip:Reading:moo} / {span:lang=ja:\u3080\u3044}) is standing on top of your {tooltip:Reading:car} ({span:lang=ja:\u304b}). Why? Because you only feed the cow once a week, and that is on the seventh day. He's standing there on the sixth day because he's super hungry and knows it's almost time.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u4e8c\u4eba\u306f\u3001\u305b\u3093\u6708\u306e\u516d\u65e5\u306b\u3001\u3069\u3053\u3067\u3042\u3044\u307e\u3057\u305f\u304b\uff1f",
                    "en": "Where did you two meet on the sixth of last month?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u516d\u65e5\u3082\u304c\u3063\u3053\u3046\u3092\u3084\u3059\u3093\u3067\u3001\u3044\u3063\u305f\u3044\u306a\u306b\u3092\u3057\u3066\u305f\u3093\u3060\u3044\uff1f",
                    "en": "What were you doing when you took six days off of school?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u6708\u306e\u516d\u65e5\u76ee\u306b\u3001\u304f\u307e\u304c\u30d0\u30fc\u306b\u3084\u3063\u3066\u304d\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "A bear came into the bar on the sixth day of the month."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Any number with a {span:lang=ja:\u3064} attached to it means you are counting that number of things. In this case, it's {tooltip:Vocabulary:six things}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Six Things",
                "Word Type": "numeral"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3080\u3063\u3064"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3080\u3063\u3064",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "30520-subject-2527.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3080\u3063\u3064",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "21227-subject-2527.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can guess that it uses the kun'yomi reading. Here's a mnemonic to help you do that:\n{splitParent}What are the {tooltip:Vocabulary:six things} that you need to count? This time you are counting six cows ({span:lang=ja:\u3080\u3063\u3064}). The reason it's six, you know, is because you wanted a half a dozen cows. No more, no less.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u308f\u305f\u3057\u306e\u3078\u3084\u306b\u306f\u3001\u30c6\u30fc\u30d6\u30eb\u304c\u4e00\u3064\u3068\u3001\u3044\u3059\u304c\u516d\u3064\u3042\u308a\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "In my room, there is one table and six chairs."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u5ddd\u304b\u3089\u77f3\u3092\u516d\u3064\u3082\u3063\u3066\u304b\u3048\u308a\u307e\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "I brought back six stones from the river."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u308f\u305f\u3057\u306f\u3001\u516d\u3064\u306e\u30ab\u30c3\u30d7\u3092\u304b\u3044\u307e\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "I bought six cups."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This kanji has hiragana attached and ends with an {span:lang=ja:\u3044} sound. That means it's probably an adjective (and it is!). So, if you take the kanji {span:lang=ja:\u5c0f} and its meaning (small), you can figure out the adjective version of it. What is the adjective version of \"small?\" Actually, it's the same thing: {tooltip:Vocabulary:small}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Small",
                "Alternative": "Little",
                "Word Type": "\u3044 adjective"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3061\u3044\u3055\u3044"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3061\u3044\u3055\u3044",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "20703-subject-2515.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3061\u3044\u3055\u3044",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "30543-subject-2515.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun'yomi reading. You didn't learn that reading with this kanji, so here's a mnemonic to help you to remember the {span:lang=ja:\u3061\u3044} portion: \n{splitParent}When you think of the word {tooltip:Vocabulary:small}, try to think of this {tooltip:Reading:chee}se ({span:lang=ja:\u3061\u3044}) store. You go inside, and all of their cheeses are so small. They hand you a magnifying glass to see all the cheeses. You look at them and exclaim: \"Why are these cheeses so small???\"\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u3075\u304f\u306f\u3001\u308f\u305f\u3057\u306b\u306f\u5c0f\u3055\u3044\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "These clothes are small on me."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u30af\u30e9\u30b9\u306b\u306f\u3001\u307e\u3060\u5c0f\u3055\u3044\u5b50\u3082\u3044\u308c\u3070\u3001\u5927\u4eba\u306e\u3088\u3046\u306b\u5927\u304d\u3044\u5b50\u3082\u3044\u308b\u3002",
                    "en": "In this class, there are kids who are still small while there are kids who are as tall as adults."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u307c\u304f\u306e\u308a\u305d\u3046\u306e\u30de\u30ea\u30e2\u306f\u3001\u5c0f\u3055\u3044\u7389\u306d\u304e\u306e\u3088\u3046\u306a\u30de\u30ea\u30e2\u3060\u3002",
                    "en": "My ideal marimo would look like a small onion."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

