                        "pronunciation": "\u3078\u305f",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "30511-subject-2538.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3078\u305f",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "20841-subject-2538.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}If you're {tooltip:Vocabulary:bad at} something and you complain about it, then you're just a {tooltip:Reading:hater} ({span:lang=ja:\u3078\u305f}). Don't be a hater.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u30ae\u30bf\u30fc\u306f\u307e\u3060\u4e0b\u624b\u3067\u3059\u304c\u3001\u307e\u3044\u3070\u3093\u308c\u3093\u3057\u3085\u3046\u3057\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "I\u2019m still bad at the guitar, but I practice every night."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30a2\u30e1\u30ea\u30ab\u4eba\u306e\u307e\u306d\u304c\u4e0b\u624b\u306a\u4eba\u306f\u3001\u30d5\u30e9\u30f3\u30b9\u4eba\u306e\u307e\u306d\u304c\u4e0a\u624b\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "The person, who is not good at imitating Americans, is good at imitating French people."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u308f\u305f\u3057\u306f\u306f\u3092\u307f\u304c\u304f\u306e\u304c\u4e0b\u624b\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "I\u2019m bad at brushing my teeth."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {span:lang=ja:\u3005} symbol just repeats the kanji that comes before it. You'll see it from time to time, so take note of it, even though it doesn't really have any meaning of its own. So, essentially {span:lang=ja:\u4e2d\u3005} is the same as {span:lang=ja:\u4e2d\u4e2d}. That being said, you have something that's middle x2. When something is even more middle than middle itself, it is {tooltip:Vocabulary:very} or {tooltip:Vocabulary:considerably} something or another.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Very",
                "Alternatives": "Considerably, Quite",
                "Word Type": "adverb"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u306a\u304b\u306a\u304b"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u306a\u304b\u306a\u304b",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "20654-subject-2521.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u306a\u304b\u306a\u304b",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "30419-subject-2521.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The reading for this word is the kun'yomi reading, since it's like putting two single kanji together rather than a true jukugo word. To learn the kun'yomi reading, take a look at this mnemonic: \n{splitParent}From the middle of the middle comes a sound. It is a knocking sound. Knock. Knock. Knock. You open up the walls around this thing. Then you open up the second set of walls (middle of the middle). Inside is an old man, who identifies himself as \"the knocker.\" You go inside of him and find another knocker as well. That's why it's a {tooltip:Reading:knocker knocker} ({span:lang=ja:\u306a\u304b\u306a\u304b}).\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u4e2d\u3005\u3067\u304d\u307e\u305b\u3093\u3002",
                    "en": "I really can\u2019t do it."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3042\u306e\u738b\u5b50\u3082\u4e2d\u3005\u3084\u308a\u307e\u3059\u306d\u3002",
                    "en": "That prince is quite good, huh?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30de\u30ea\u30aa\u30ab\u30fc\u30c8\u3092\u304d\u308f\u3081\u308b\u3053\u3068\u306f\u4e2d\u3005\u304b\u3093\u305f\u3093\u3067\u306f\u306a\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "It\u2019s not very easy to make oneself a master of Mario Kart."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

