{
            "mnemonic": "This word has a single kanji with hiragana attached, ending in a {span:lang=ja:\u3044}. This means you know it's an adjective. What is the adjective form of {tooltip:Kanji:few}? It's also {tooltip:Vocabulary:few}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "A Few",
                "Alternatives": "Few, Scarce, Not Much, Not Many",
                "Word Type": "\u3044 adjective"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3059\u304f\u306a\u3044"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3059\u304f\u306a\u3044",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "13014-subject-2608.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3059\u304f\u306a\u3044",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "32935-subject-2608.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The reading for this one is fairly difficult, as it's the kun'yomi reading (you can tell it's kun'yomi because of all the hiragana attached). You have to remember the {span:lang=ja:\u3059\u304f} part to remember the reading of this word. Think of it this way: \n{splitParent}You have a {tooltip:Vocabulary:few} of something. This something is {tooltip:Reading:suck}ers ({span:lang=ja:\u3059\u304f}). Imagine putting all (three-ish) of them in your mouth at once. How does it taste? Can you taste the flavors from each of the few?\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u3072\u3083\u304f\u3055\u3044\u307e\u3067\u751f\u304d\u308b\u4eba\u306f\u5c11\u306a\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "There are few people who live to be 100 years old."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u308a\u3087\u3046\u308a\u306e\u30ec\u30d1\u30fc\u30c8\u30ea\u30fc\u304c\u5c11\u306a\u3044\u3093\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "I don't know how to cook many things."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3073\u3088\u3046\u3057\u3055\u3093\u304b\u3089\u3001\u304b\u307f\u306e\u3051\u304c\u5c11\u306a\u3044\u3068\u3088\u304f\u3044\u308f\u308c\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "I\u2019m often told by hairdressers that I don\u2019t have much hair."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This word is a single kanji with hiragana attached, though there is no specific clue as to what type of word it is. That means you can usually guess it's a noun, adverb or na-adjective (in this case it's a noun / adverb). The meaning of this word is pretty much the same as the kanji, though, making it fairly easy. The kanji meaning for {tooltip:Kanji:\u5c11} is {tooltip:Kanji:few}, and the meaning of the vocab form is {tooltip:Vocabulary:a little} or {tooltip:Vocabulary:a few}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "A Little",
                "Alternatives": "A Few, Few",
                "Word Type": "adverb, noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3059\u3053\u3057"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
