{
            "mnemonic": "When you see a katakana word, it's a safe bet that it is a foreign loan word from somewhere else. This one's interesting, because it combines katakana and kanji! The first {tooltip:Vocabulary:marble} came to Japan from Portugal, so the {span:lang=ja:\u30d3\u30fc} part of the word is short for \"vidro,\" which means \"glass\" in Portuguese. Anyway, don't get thrown off by that {span:lang=ja:\u30d3\u30fc}. Maybe just imagine that your favorite marble is black and yellow like a \ud83d\udc1d",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Marble",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3073\u30fc\u3060\u307e"
                    },
                    {
                        "primary": false,
                        "reading": "\u30d3\u30fc\u3060\u307e"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3073\u30fc\u3060\u307e",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "30404-subject-2574.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3073\u30fc\u3060\u307e",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "20831-subject-2574.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}If you know katakana as well as the reading that you learned when you learned the kanji {tooltip:Kanji:\u7389}, you should be able to read this. Just watch out for the rendaku.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u30d3\u30fc\u7389\u3092\u3064\u304b\u3063\u3066\u30b2\u30fc\u30e0\u3092\u3057\u307e\u3057\u3087\u3046\u3002",
                    "en": "Let\u2019s play a game that uses marbles."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30a2\u30e1\u30ea\u30ab\u3067\u306f\u307e\u3044\u3068\u3057\u4e00\u6708\u306b\u30d3\u30fc\u7389\u3092\u3064\u304b\u3063\u305f\u30b2\u30fc\u30e0\u304c\u4e0a\u624b\u306a\u30d5\u30e9\u30f3\u30b9\u4eba\u304c\u3064\u3069\u3046\u30b3\u30f3\u30c6\u30b9\u30c8\u304c\u3042\u308a\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "In America, there is a contest every January where French people who are good at marbles gather."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u304b\u308c\u306f\u3001\u3051\u3063\u3053\u3093\u306e\u3042\u304b\u3057\u306b\u4e00\u3064\u3076\u306e\u30d3\u30fc\u7389\u3092\u304f\u308c\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "He gave me a single marble as proof of our marriage."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well. This is the polite and humble form of {tooltip:Vocabulary:mother}. You use this when you're talking about your own mother to other people, especially if you aren't very close to them.",
