{
            "mnemonic": "The {span:lang=ja:\u306e} particle makes it so the {span:lang=ja:\u4eba} belongs to the {span:lang=ja:\u5973}. Basically, this literally means \"a woman person\" or \"a person of woman.\" It's a more polite way to say plain old woman, though, so all you have to remember is that.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Woman",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u304a\u3093\u306a\u306e\u3072\u3068"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u304a\u3093\u306a\u306e\u3072\u3068",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "12945-subject-8735.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u304a\u3093\u306a\u306e\u3072\u3068",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "32832-subject-8735.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}This word is actually two separate words combined with a particle. So, since you treat them like separate words, you read each of them with the kun'yomi readings (they are both single kanji words all alone). That being said, in level 1 you learned about the vocab readings for these separate words, so use that knowledge to remember the reading for this word as well.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u308f\u305f\u3057\u306f\u5973\u306e\u4eba\u306b\u3044\u3058\u3081\u3089\u308c\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "I was bullied by a woman."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u305d\u306e\u30ab\u30d5\u30a7\u306f\u5973\u306e\u4eba\u3060\u3089\u3051\u3067\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "The cafe was full of women."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u5973\u306e\u4eba\u3068\u3057\u3083\u3079\u308b\u306e\u304c\u82e6\u624b\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "I\u2019m not good at talking to women."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "See the mouth and teeth of this {tooltip:Radical:alligator}, which is facing to the right? You wouldn't want to get your hand stuck in there, that's for sure.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Alligator"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "See how tall the stalks are with little bushy things at the top? It kind of looks like {tooltip:Radical:bamboo}, if you use your imagination.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Bamboo"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You'll have to complete the line on the right side in your mind, but see the rectangle {tooltip:Radical:bed} and the pillows? This is totally a bed. That missing line is where you pulled back the sheets when you got up this morning.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Bed"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Doesn't this look like someone on their knees, holding their hands out (clasped) and begging for something (money, their life, etc.)? That's why this radical is known as the {tooltip:Radical:beggar}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Beggar"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There is a cross growing out of this stool. It's {tooltip:Radical:branch}ing off of it, if you will. The tree that this stool was made of is coming back to life in the form of a branch.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Branch"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Doesn't this look like a top-down view of the frame of a {tooltip:Radical:car}? There are the rods that make up the base of the car and things for the wheels to go on. That's why this radical is the car radical.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Car"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical looks like a person with a long dress or train running behind them. That's because it's a bride or groom going to their wedding {tooltip:Radical:ceremony}! You usually have to wear a long dress or a cape if you're at a ceremony. So when you see this radical, think of all the different ceremonies you might have to go to in your life and what you'll need to wear.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Ceremony"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "See the {tooltip:Radical:cliff}? It's facing towards the west. What ocean is to your west? That's the direction this cliff is facing.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Cliff"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical is a bit more elaborate than the eye, though similar. In fact, if you go blind, your {tooltip:Radical:ear}s become one of your new senses of sight, which is why this is like another, somewhat modified, eye. You'll even notice little lines sticking out of the eye. That's because ears stick out more than eyes ({span:lang=ja:\u76ee}), which is why this radical is the ear radical.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Ear"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical is shaped kind of like a dam. See the wall and the water pushing over the top? If this radical is a dam, then it must create a ton of {tooltip:Radical:energy} as well!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Energy"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "See the little things sticking out of the ground? Those little things are pretty little {tooltip:Radical:flowers}. You know they're flowers and not, say, grass, because they're spaced out. You'll learn grass later, and it will be less spaced out, but somewhat similar to this, so keep that in mind!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Flowers"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Do you see the dude doing a flying dragon kick to the right? What does he hit the target with? Obviously it's his {tooltip:Radical:foot}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Foot"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Sure, this {tooltip:Radical:forehead} is a bit square, but that's because it's Frankenstein's Monster's forehead. Also, did you notice that this is just the top part of the head ({span:lang=ja:\u5182}) radical? This is just the forehead part of that!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Forehead"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Can you see the {tooltip:Radical:horns}? You could put these horns on anything. Donkeys, people, monkeys, and so on. That's exactly what we'll be doing when we learn kanji that have horns too.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Horns"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There is something private in your mouth. You're keeping it private because you don't want other people to judge you. Why? Because it's an {tooltip:Radical:insect}. You like to eat insects, but society seems to think it's wrong. Don't worry, friend. Someday the rest of the world will embrace eating insects like you have.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Insect"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This radical technically means person (like {span:lang=ja:\u4eba}), but we're going to shift the meaning a little bit. It's kind of like the person radical, but it's taller and skinnier, standing above the rest of those peons. That's why this is a {tooltip:Radical:leader}, not a regular old person.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Leader"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The leader ({span:lang=ja:\uff72}) radical has a little something extra on top of him, holding him down, holding him back. It's causing him to wait... it's causing him to {tooltip:Radical:loiter}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Loiter"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This king has a drop of water floating above his head. That's because he's not just a king, he's the {tooltip:Radical:master} of water. Master of elements. Master of ALL THINGS!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Master"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Have you ever used one of those math compasses? This looks just like one of those because this is the {tooltip:Radical:measurement} radical. Compasses are used to measure things, just like this.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Measurement"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "If you see this radical in a kanji, you'll want to think of the opposite of whatever other radicals there are. It looks a lot like \"below,\" but guess what? It's {tooltip:Radical:not} the below radical. It's just not.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Not"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Obviously, this looks just like the good ol' 3.14 something-something all people are supposed to learn about. It's the math symbol that has to do with circles... {tooltip:Radical:pi}!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Pi"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This is a few {tooltip:Radical:plate}s drying on a rack. They're stacked together in a line. Imagine reaching out and grabbing one of those plates for yourself.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Plate"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There are some legs and fins under the dirt. Imagine yourself looking at all of these severed limbs... there's blood everywhere. No matter how deep you dig, everything is stained {tooltip:Radical:red}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Red"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Not to be confused with a rice paddy ({span:lang=ja:\u7530}). This is just raw {tooltip:Radical:rice}, like the grain that grows out of the ground when rice is raw and fresh. When rice is particularly ripe, it'll kind of look like this at the top of the stalks. See the long leaves with the little rice tufts at the top? That's why this radical is \"rice.\"",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Rice"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This is a {tooltip:Radical:roof}. You know it's a roof and not a forehead because there's a little chimney sticking out of the top. And foreheads definitely don't have chimneys.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Roof"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:samurai} radical looks a lot like the dirt radical. The difference is in the top horizontal line. For samurai, the top is longer because he's holding swords. Dirt can't really hold anything because its arms are too short. So when you see this radical, think: long arms for some long swords. That's a samurai!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Samurai"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's an eyeball on top of some legs. When your eyes get taken places (on legs, in this case), your eyes are {tooltip:Radical:see}ing those things. That's why this radical means see.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "See"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's a giant eye on top of some fins. Imagine this giant eye to be on top of a clam or a mussel. It has little fins, which it runs around on, escaping from your every attempt to capture it. That's why this is the {tooltip:Radical:shellfish} radical.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Shellfish"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "On the ground there are a bunch of {tooltip:Radical:spikes} coming out. You wouldn't want to step on those now, would you?",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Spikes"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This looks just like the katakana character for ne. Ne for necromancer! That makes this a {tooltip:Radical:spirit}, brought back from the grave. OooooOOoooOoo!",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Spirit"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There is something small in some poop. You lean in, carefully pull it out, and realize it's a {tooltip:Radical:thread}. Why is there some thread in this small poop? Because it's cat poop. And cats love to eat thread when you aren't looking. And floss and hoodie strings and mouse tails. But this poop just has thread in it.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Thread"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Do you know how the bottom of an {tooltip:Radical:umbrella} is shaped? It has a hook that curls around for the handle. So when you see this radical, you should think of the bottom of an umbrella, which will help you remember that it means umbrella.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Umbrella"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There are no circles in kanji, remember? So imagine that these angles are really circles. A little circle in a big circle, with a line running through them. Why, it's the {tooltip:Radical:world}. The world looks just like this, with continents on the globe and a line is the equator. Can you see it?",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "World"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:bamboo} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:bamboo} kanji are exactly the same!",
            "hints": "Just make sure you know your radicals!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Bamboo"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f\u3051",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Of course, focus on the absurdity of a bamboo shrub that can cry and eat a lollipop. Also, think about how mean you are to {tooltip:Reading:take} the lollipop away from a defenseless bamboo shrub in the first place!",
                "mnemonic": "There's a little {tooltip:Kanji:bamboo} shrub sitting there, holding onto a lollipop. Because you're bigger than the bamboo shrub, you just {tooltip:Reading:take} ({span:lang=ja:\u305f\u3051}) it from the bamboo, causing it to cry."
            }
        }

