        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's a {tooltip:Radical:mouth} in the {tooltip:Radical:ground} (in our case, it's a hatch), and inside is some kind of {tooltip:Radical:prison} you can't quite get to. If you've ever seen Lost, this will sound familiar... on the hatch, there's actually a series of {tooltip:Kanji:number}s that you don't understand.",
            "hints": "What are these numbers? What do they mean? Think over it for a while, get frustrated, and try to figure it out. You can't, though, so you probably won't ever get inside.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Number"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3054\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "{span:lang=ja:\u3054\u3046\u3044\u3061} keeps saying numbers as you free him from the prison. Just one after another. Numbers numbers numbers. Hopefully old man {span:lang=ja:\u3054\u3046\u3044\u3061} isn't crazy.",
                "mnemonic": "\"You want to know the {tooltip:Kanji:number} to the hatch?\" yells an old voice from inside. You say \"yes,\" and the voice gives you the numbers and you're in! You go down into the prison, where there is one prisoner: Grandpa Koichi ({span:lang=ja:\u3054\u3046\u3044\u3061})."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:leader} of {tooltip:Radical:alligators}... she's no ordinary leader, that's for sure. She's not a politician.. she's not a queen... She's one of the {tooltip:Kanji:Other} Ones, a group of strange, eccentric people who become leaders of animals.",
            "hints": "Imagine different groups of leaders - they're all dressed nice and normal. Then, off to the side are the leaders who are leaders of bears, cats, alligators, and narwhals... they're the {tooltip:Kanji:other} ones, no matter how you look at it. They are in their own group, and they are considered \"others\" because society won't accept them :(",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Other"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307b\u304b",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Just imagine the \"others\" shaking their heads at these taco worshipers. Seriously, what the heck?",
                "mnemonic": "To remember the reading {span:lang=ja:\u305f}, we use the word \"Taco\""
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "If someone tries to dig up {tooltip:Radical:private} {tooltip:Radical:dirt} on you, tell them no thanks, that stuff is in the {tooltip:Kanji:past}. Some things should stay in the past. Not everyone needs to know everything about your life and your past.",
            "hints": "Keep your private dirt in the past. It's better for everyone.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Past"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304d\u3087, \u3053",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3055",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine physically, literally burying your past {span:lang=ja:\u5b50} self. If you hide it in Little Kyoto, no one will ever be able to dig it up.",
                "mnemonic": "To remember the reading {span:lang=ja:\u304d\u3087}, we'll use \"Little Kyoto.\" This will differentiate {span:lang=ja:\u304d\u3087} from {span:lang=ja:\u304d\u3087\u3046}, which is regular-sized Kyoto. Get it? You decide to bury all this private dirt from your {tooltip:Kanji:past} in Little {tooltip:Reading:Kyo}to ({span:lang=ja:\u304d\u3087}). That stuff is from when you were just a little {span:lang=ja:\u5b50} ({span:lang=ja:\u3053}) and you don't like to think about it."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Kanji:plate} kanji and the {tooltip:Radical:plate} radical are exactly the same, like two matching pieces of china.",
            "hints": "If you know your radicals, you know your kanji too.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Plate",
                "Alternative": "Dish"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3055\u3089",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine someone with a huge head, washing dishes. When I say huge, I mean huge. She turns towards you with her big head and says \u201cHi, I\u2019m Sarah\u201d and you see her name tag, which also says Sarah. She is Sarah, the dish washer. The plates she holds up next to her head pale in comparison, even though they're the biggest pieces.",
                "mnemonic": "The person washing all the {tooltip:Kanji:dish}es was named {tooltip:Reading:Sarah} ({span:lang=ja:\u3055\u3089}). She was named that because of her large head, which was shaped like Sarah Jessica Parker\u2019s head (very big)."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Radical:Slide} some {tooltip:Radical:dirt} on your {tooltip:Radical:legs} so you can feel like you're from a {tooltip:Kanji:previous} time. Everything from the past is in the dirt. Dead people, dinosaur bones, your mom's secrets in a box from her past life. All these things come back to you as you slide the dirt all over your legs.",
            "hints": "Imagine the feeling of dirt on your legs. Now think back to the previous day. The previous year. The previous century.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Previous",
                "Alternatives": "Ahead, Past, Former"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305b\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3055\u304d, \u307e\u305a",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Centaurs were definitely real in the past. Don't let anyone tell you otherwise. They're just afraid of the truth.",
                "mnemonic": "As you sit there, pretending you're in a {tooltip:Kanji:previous} time you see a {tooltip:Reading:cen}taur ({span:lang=ja:\u305b\u3093})! That's right! Centaurs are from wayyyy in the {tooltip:Kanji:past}."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The radical for {tooltip:Radical:red} and the kanji for {tooltip:Kanji:red} are the same thing!",
            "hints": "What a lucky ducky - as long as you know your radicals, you'll know this kanji's meaning as well!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Red"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305b\u304d",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3042\u304b",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "You'll have to think of him as really, really red, though. Like, a glowing, searing kind of red. Red enough for you to associate red with this evil Fozzie Bear. If you do, then you'll be good to go (as long as you know his catch phrase as well).",
                "mnemonic": "Think of Fozzie Bear. He says \"Wakka wakka wakka\" a lot. Now, think of an evil, {tooltip:Kanji:red} (and I mean totally red) Fozzie Bear. He drops the \"w\" and just says \"{tooltip:Reading:Aka} Aka Aka,\" because he's totally evil."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:leader} is next to the {tooltip:Radical:tree}. What is he doing? He's leaning against it, {tooltip:Kanji:rest}ing.",
            "hints": "The best thing to do with a tree is rest under it. Picture the leader resting under the tree with his eyes half closed.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Rest"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304d\u3085\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3084\u3059",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Don't email me saying cucumbers don't grow on trees. Cucumber trees exist. Look them up and then rest easy knowing I'd never lie to you.",
                "mnemonic": "As he {tooltip:Kanji:rest}s, he suddenly gets hungry and pulls a {tooltip:Reading:cu}cumber ({span:lang=ja:\u304d\u3085\u3046}) off the tree and bites into it. A good rest isn't complete without some crunchy tree cucumbers."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There\u2019s a {tooltip:Radical:cross} in your {tooltip:Radical:mouth}, so the things you say are very pious. Everything you say you {tooltip:Kanji:say humbly}.",
            "hints": "Imagine yourself with a cross in your mouth, saying all kinds of humble things. \"Oh no, I'm not that good.\" \"Oh no, my mustache doesn't look that great.\"",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Say Humbly",
                "Alternatives": "Say, Humbly Say"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3093",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3082\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine this character in your head. If it helps, you can take a Moe you already know of (Simpsons, perhaps?) and pretend he's extremely humble - ridiculously so. Just remember to make it crazy so you can remember it better.",
                "mnemonic": "When you think of saying humble things, think of the humblest man in the world. His name is {tooltip:Reading:Moe} ({span:lang=ja:\u3082\u3046})."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The radical {tooltip:Radical:see} and the kanji {tooltip:Kanji:see} are exactly the same!",
            "hints": "Make sure you're studying your radicals. If you do, you'll know a lot of kanji as well, almost magically!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "See"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3051\u3093",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307f",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "The struggling to see bit is pretty important - make sure that you slowly see a little better and better as the person comes closer, but it's difficult the whole time (struggles are good for memory). Then, it starts to become more and more clear, and you start saying \"that can't be {tooltip:Reading:me}. No way it's me...\" When the person comes up to you (the person, being you) you learn about parallel universes and other fabulous things. Still, the whole time you can't stop thinking about how \"this person looks just like me... no, this person is me!\"",
                "mnemonic": "You see something. It's walking towards you, but you need to see better. When the person comes close, you realize \"That person is {tooltip:Reading:me} ({span:lang=ja:\u307f})!\""
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:shellfish} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:shellfish} kanji are the same! It can be just the {tooltip:Kanji:shell} part too.",
            "hints": "Shellfish are shellfish, you know?",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Shellfish",
                "Alternative": "Shell"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3070\u3044",
