                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Whoops, maybe you shouldn't throw stones through windows or you'll see people's sexy times.",
                "mnemonic": "You throw a {tooltip:Kanji:stone} through a window... and, now you can see something very {tooltip:Reading:sexy} ({span:lang=ja:\u305b\u304d})."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:leader} for the {tooltip:Radical:ceremony} can't come so you need a {tooltip:Kanji:substitute} to {tooltip:Kanji:replace} them ASAP!",
            "hints": "Picture the leader of the ceremony out back, unsure of what period or age they're in, while the substitute leader walks in and stares at them in shock.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Substitute",
                "Alternatives": "Replace, Period, Age"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3060\u3044, \u305f\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304b, \u304b\u308f, \u3057\u308d",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Now you're out back with the original leader and the substitute leader, trying to dye the color of this stupid shirt to match that one.",
                "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Kanji:substitute} leader is wearing the wrong color clothes, so you have to {tooltip:Reading:dye} ({span:lang=ja:\u3060\u3044}) them, like they did in the olden {tooltip:Kanji:age}s."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Always remember to give {tooltip:Radical:umbrella}s to the {tooltip:Radical:spirit}s to show your {tooltip:Kanji:thanks}. The next time you're walking by a shrine and you see a statue dedicated to a spirit, leave your umbrella open to protect it. This is the best way you can show your thanks for life, food, shelter, and all that good, good stuff.",
            "hints": "Picture a spirit shrine with an umbrella protecting it from the heavy rain. \"Thanks for everything,\" you say softly.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Thanks"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u308c\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Thanks for the new ray gun, spirit!",
                "mnemonic": "After you say {tooltip:Kanji:thanks} to a spirit, sometimes you'll get a gift back. You look down. There is a {tooltip:Reading:ray} ({span:lang=ja:\u308c\u3044}) gun by your feet. Huh. Maybe you should show your thanks to the ray gun spirit more often."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:thread} radical and {tooltip:Kanji:thread} are the same in looks as well as in meaning.",
            "hints": "This is why it's good to make sure you know your radicals.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Thread"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3044\u3068",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Think about how it tastes. Think about how it feels in your throat. Let it get caught on your teeth and around your tongue. It's a weird feeling having thread in your mouth, and you want to use that weird feeling to remember you're going to {tooltip:Reading:eat} it.",
                "mnemonic": "You have some {tooltip:Kanji:thread}... what's the first thing you do with it? You take it and put it in your mouth, and start sucking it down like spaghetti. You have decided to {tooltip:Reading:eat} ({span:lang=ja:\u3044\u3068}) the thread."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "When you see both a {tooltip:Radical:rice paddy} and a {tooltip:Radical:street} in the same place, you have the makings of a whole {tooltip:Kanji:town}!",
            "hints": "Think about it, a single farm wouldn't need a whole street. Just paths would do. There is a street next to this rice paddy, so there must be other people, which makes this a {tooltip:Kanji:town}.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Town"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3061\u3087\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307e\u3061",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Remember imagining Mrs. Chou in great detail in level 2? Time for some of that work to come to fruition. Imagine Mrs. Chou walking down the street of the town towards you, ready to make your day bad. She breaks things as she walks towards you, then knocks you down for walking around in her town. She says, \"Wherever there's a street, you'll find Mrs. Chou.\" Then spits on your back as she walks away.",
                "mnemonic": "You go to visit this nice little {tooltip:Kanji:town}. But then, there she is... Every time you walk down the street, the street-woman Mrs. {tooltip:Reading:Chou} ({span:lang=ja:\u3061\u3087\u3046}) arrives to ruin your day."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Under his {tooltip:Radical:roof}, the {tooltip:Radical:king} stares into a {tooltip:Radical:drop} of water containing his greatest {tooltip:Kanji:treasure}. Just kidding, the king has way more treasure than that. The drop of water is just reflecting what's around him: mounds and mounds of treasure, stacked all the way up to the roof.",
            "hints": "Picture the treasure reflected in the drop of water. It's marvelous!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Treasure"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307b\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f\u304b\u3089",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture his home from the outside. It looks like a normal, small castle. But then you walk in and it's a treasure trove of... well, treasure!",
                "mnemonic": "All of this {tooltip:Kanji:treasure} is kept in the king's {tooltip:Reading:ho}me ({span:lang=ja:\u307b\u3046}). He can't let other people see how much treasure he has, or they'll get mad, or worse(!) ask him to share some with his people. Yuck. The treasure stays at home."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Someone\u2019s taking {tooltip:Radical:measurements} of the {tooltip:Radical:trees}\u2026 Why is someone taking measurements of trees? These people are planning to take the trees and cut them down to make their {tooltip:Kanji:village}.",
            "hints": "There is a whole group of people out there who are measuring these trees. Imagine them asking each other \u201cdo you think this will be a good tree for our village center?\u201d and other people nodding in agreement or disagreement as they measure.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Village"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305d\u3093",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3080\u3089",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Look at the mural on the side of this newly constructed village center. So many trees! Visualize the whole process as the tree becomes a building as you gaze and reflect on the message of the mural.",
                "mnemonic": "They take all the wood back to where they're building their {tooltip:Kanji:village} and once they construct the village center they paint a {tooltip:Reading:mura}l ({span:lang=ja:\u3080\u3089}) on the side of it. The mural is of the forest where they got the tree from so they remember the building's origins."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:world} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:world} kanji are the same, but there is another really important meaning you need to know: {tooltip:Kanji:generation}. ",
            "hints": "It's all these generational differences that make the world go 'round!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "World",
                "Alternative": "Generation"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305b\u3044, \u305b",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3088",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Hold the saber up high and pledge to protect the world for all of the generations to come! Then swish it around and make buzzing sounds with your mouth.",
                "mnemonic": "It's your job to protect the {tooltip:Kanji:world} and every {tooltip:Kanji:generation} with your special {tooltip:Reading:sa}ber ({span:lang=ja:\u305b\u3044}). It was given to you to uphold correctness, and you'll pass it on to the next generation when it's time. Then they'll watch out for the world when you're gone."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:cow} part of this kanji is slightly altered, but I think you can see where it is. The {tooltip:Radical:gun} looks just fine, though. Think back to a fake memory you have living on the farm. You have your gun, and you gotta use it on a cow (ruh roh). Once a {tooltip:Kanji:year} on New Year's, you have to go out and shoot one of the cows. It's only a yearly shooting, though, so at least there's that respite.",
            "hints": "You'll have to imagine yourself doing this to remember the meaning of the kanji. Focus on the thought that \"okay... it's only once a year... I only have to do this once a {tooltip:Kanji:year}...\"",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Year"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306d\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3068\u3057",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "How funny that you use dead cow parts to make a miniature cow holding a gun much like the one you murdered earlier. Imagine yourself playing with this nendoroid as you think about how weird it was to make a cow nendoroid out of a dead cow.",
                "mnemonic": "It is New {tooltip:Kanji:Years}, though, so when you bring the dead cow back home, it\u2019s time to celebrate. Using the cow\u2019s organs and bones, you and your siblings construct a {tooltip:Reading:nen}doroid ({span:lang=ja:\u306d\u3093}). Nendoroids are Japanese toys - little plastic figurines (Google it if need be). You make a little cow nendoroid holding a gun. How cute."
            }
