{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Kanji:name} written in {tooltip:Kanji:letter}s is the {tooltip:Vocabulary:surname}. Back in the day your surname was the important name (it's your family name after all, and family was king back in the day). This is the name you'd write out in kanji... aka in letters. So, that's why this combination of kanji means \"surname.\"",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Surname",
                "Alternatives": "Last Name, Family Name",
                "Word Type": "noun, \u306e adjective"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u307f\u3087\u3046\u3058"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u307f\u3087\u3046\u3058",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "35179-subject-2656.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u307f\u3087\u3046\u3058",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "15286-subject-2656.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}This is a jukugo word, which usually means on'yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you'll know how to read this as well. \n{splitParent}Note that the {span:lang=ja:\u540d} portion is the alternate reading, so adjust your thinking accordingly so you can get to {span:lang=ja:\u307f\u3087\u3046}. Just imagine someone saying \"more\" with a funny accent. They say \"myou\" instead of \"more\". Imagine them saying they have myou people in their family than yours. Their surname is spread amongst myou people than yours.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u3055\u304f\u3089\u3055\u3093\u306e\u540d\u5b57\u306f\u4f55\u3068\u3044\u3044\u307e\u3059\u304b\u3002",
                    "en": "What is Sakura\u2019s last name?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30b3\u30a6\u30a4\u30c1\u306e\u540d\u5b57\u3092\u304a\u3057\u3048\u3066\u304f\u308c\u305f\u3089\u3001\u304a\u793c\u306b\u3066\u3064\u4eba\u4e8c\u5341\u516b\u53f7\u306e\u30d5\u30a3\u30ae\u30e5\u30a2\u3092\u3042\u3052\u308b\u3088\u3002",
                    "en": "If you tell me Koichi's surname, I will give you a figure of Tetsujin 28 in return."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u79c1\u306e\u540d\u5b57\u306f\u3042\u306a\u305f\u306b\u306f\u7d76\u5bfe\u6559\u3048\u307e\u305b\u3093\u3002",
                    "en": "I will never tell you my family name."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

