{
            "mnemonic": "When you ask {tooltip:Kanji:what} {tooltip:Kanji:people} you're not actually asking \"what people?\" You're asking {tooltip:Vocabulary:how many people}. One thing you'll notice with Japanese and kanji is that {span:lang=ja:\u4f55}, when added to other things, often means \"how many _____\" rather than \"what ____.\" Just something you have to know and experience a bit to catch on to, but you will, I believe in you.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "How Many People",
                "Word Type": "noun, expression"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u306a\u3093\u306b\u3093"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u306a\u3093\u306b\u3093",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "37528-subject-2782.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u306a\u3093\u306b\u3093",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "17608-subject-2782.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}This word uses the kun'yomi reading for {span:lang=ja:\u4f55} and the on'yomi reading for {span:lang=ja:\u4eba}. If you know the readings of your kanji you'll know how to read this as well. Take note that {span:lang=ja:\u4eba} uses its {span:lang=ja:\u306b\u3093} reading here.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u305d\u306e\u30d1\u30fc\u30c6\u30a3\u30fc\u306b\u306f\u3001\u4f55\u4eba\u306e\u4eba\u304c\u3055\u3093\u304b\u3057\u307e\u3057\u305f\u304b\u3002",
                    "en": "How many people participated in the party?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u4eca\u307e\u3067\u3053\u306e\u6c60\u3067\u4f55\u4eba\u306e\u4eba\u3005\u304c\u3055\u3064\u4eba\u304e\u3087\u306b\u3053\u308d\u3055\u308c\u307e\u3057\u305f\u304b\u3002",
                    "en": "How many people have been killed by the killer fish in this pond?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u5915\u98df\u306b\u306f\u3044\u3063\u305f\u3044\u4f55\u4eba\u306e\u4eba\u304c\u3084\u3063\u3066\u6765\u308b\u3093\u3067\u3059\u304b\uff1f",
                    "en": "How many people will be joining us for dinner?"
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Kanji:What} {tooltip:Kanji:times}? This doesn't make any sense. One thing you'll notice with Japanese and kanji is that {span:lang=ja:\u4f55}, when added to other things, often means \"how many _____\" rather than \"what ____.\" Just something you have to know and experience a bit to catch on to, but you will, I believe in you. So, for this one it's {tooltip:Vocabulary:how many times}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "How Many Times",
                "Word Type": "noun, expression"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
