{
            "mnemonic": "The {span:lang=ja:\u306e} particle makes it so the {span:lang=ja:\u4eba} belongs to the {span:lang=ja:\u7537}. Basically, this literally means \"a man person\" or \"a person of man.\" It's a more polite way to say plain old {tooltip:Vocabulary:man}, though, so all you have to remember is that.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Man",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u304a\u3068\u3053\u306e\u3072\u3068"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u304a\u3068\u3053\u306e\u3072\u3068",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "17598-subject-3412.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u304a\u3068\u3053\u306e\u3072\u3068",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "37504-subject-3412.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}This word is actually two separate words combined with a particle. So, since you treat them like separate words, you read each of them with the kun'yomi readings (they are both single kanji words all alone). That being said, in level 4 you learned about the vocab readings for these separate words, so use that knowledge to remember the reading for this word as well.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u3042\u3042\uff01\u3053\u306e\u7537\u306e\u4eba\u304c\u3001\u3059\u305a\u6728\u3055\u3093\u3067\u3059\u304b\u3002",
                    "en": "Ah! Is that guy Suzuki?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3042\u305d\u3053\u306b\u7acb\u3063\u3066\u3044\u308b\u5916\u4eba\u306e\u7537\u306e\u4eba\u306e\u3068\u306a\u308a\u306b\u3044\u308b\u5973\u306e\u4eba\u304c\u3042\u306e\u3086\u3046\u540d\u306a\u307e\u3064\u3044\u4e00\u4ee3\u3055\u3093\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "The lady next to the foreign guy standing over there is THE famous Kazuyo Matsui."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u80cc\u306e\u9ad8\u3044\u7537\u306e\u4eba\u306fT\u30b7\u30e3\u30c4\u3092\u7740\u3066\u3044\u3066\u3001\u80cc\u306e\u4f4e\u3044\u7537\u306e\u4eba\u306f\u30bb\u30fc\u30bf\u30fc\u3092\u7740\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "The tall guy is wearing a T-shirt and the short guy is wearing a sweater."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This is a single kanji with an {span:lang=ja:\u3044} on the end, meaning you know it's probably an adjective. What's the adjective form of {tooltip:Kanji:many}? It is also {tooltip:Vocabulary:many} (or you could say {tooltip:Vocabulary:much}). Speaking of which, there are so many kanji to learn, ammiright?",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Many",
                "Alternatives": "Much, Lots Of",
                "Word Type": "\u3044 adjective"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u304a\u304a\u3044"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u304a\u304a\u3044",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
