                        "pronunciation": "\u3044\u304f",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "37376-subject-2775.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun'yomi reading. You didn't learn that reading with this kanji, so here's a mnemonic to help you: \n{splitParent}You have to remember the {span:lang=ja:\u3044} portion. To remember {span:lang=ja:\u3044} we use the word \"eagle.\" \n{splitParent}You want {tooltip:Vocabulary:to go} somewhere (anywhere!) but you can't. An {tooltip:Reading:ea}gle ({span:lang=ja:\u3044}) blocks your path. You try to go forward and it opens its wings and bites at you. You try to go back and it jumps at you. There's nowhere for you to go with this eagle standing there.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u4eca\u304b\u3089\u3001\u304b\u3044\u3082\u306e\u306b\u884c\u304d\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "I'm about to go shopping."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3088\u3046\u3058\u304c\u3067\u304d\u3066\u3001\u884c\u3051\u306a\u304f\u306a\u3063\u3066\u3057\u307e\u3044\u307e\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "Something came up, so I'm not able to make it."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30bf\u30b3\u30d9\u30eb\u304c\u7121\u3044\u306e\u3067\u706b\u661f\u306b\u306f\u884c\u304d\u305f\u304f\u306a\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "I wouldn\u2019t want to go to Mars because they don\u2019t have Taco Bell there."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an \u3046 sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji {span:lang=ja:\u5165} means \"enter,\" but this word does not mean \"to enter\" (which you already learned as {span:lang=ja:\u5165\u308b}). Still, the meaning of {span:lang=ja:\u5165\u308c\u308b} is still related to the kanji's meaning: it means {tooltip:Vocabulary:to insert} or {tooltip:Vocabulary:to put in}. It's something you do to another object, like inserting a key into a lock, or you hand into a glove. ",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "To Insert",
                "Alternative": "To Put In",
                "Word Type": "transitive verb, ichidan verb"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3044\u308c\u308b"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3044\u308c\u308b",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "27921-subject-2481.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3044\u308c\u308b",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "2768-subject-2481.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}When a vocab is a single kanji plus some okurigana (hiragana stuck on the end of kanji) it's almost always going to be the kun'yomi reading. This is one of two kun'yomi readings you'll be learning for the kanji {span:lang=ja:\u5165}. As long as you know that this vocab means {tooltip:Vocabulary:to insert}, you can use that to remember the reading as well. \n{splitParent}Imagine inserting your hand into a hole and an {tooltip:Reading:ea}gle ({span:lang=ja:\u3044}) flies out, screaming: \"{span:lang=ja:\u3044}!\" This is something that happens every time you insert your hand into something: an eagle comes out. You don't know why.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u3057\u304b\u304f\u306b\u306a\u306b\u3092\u5165\u308c\u307e\u3059\u304b\u3002",
                    "en": "What will you put in the square?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u3053\u306b\u304a\u308a\u304c\u307f\u3092\u5341\u4e8c\u307e\u3044\u5165\u308c\u3066\u304f\u3060\u3055\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "Please insert twelve pieces of origami paper here."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u308f\u305f\u3057\u306f\u30cf\u30f3\u30d0\u30fc\u30ac\u30fc\u3092\u3072\u3064\u304e\u306b\u5165\u308c\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "I put a hamburger into the coffin."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an {span:lang=ja:\u3046} sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means {tooltip:Kanji:study} or {tooltip:Kanji:learning}. When you do these things, what ends up happening? You learn. That's why this word means {tooltip:Vocabulary:to learn}. ",
