{
            "mnemonic": "The kanji part of this word is like saying \"the {tooltip:Kanji:doing} {tooltip:Kanji:way}\" ... aka the way you do something. So, when you don't have a way to do something, there's nothing you can do. Thus is born the popular Japanese phrase: {span:lang=ja:\u4ed5\u65b9\u304c\u306a\u3044}, which means {tooltip:Vocabulary:can't be helped}, {tooltip:Vocabulary:it's no use}, or {tooltip:Vocabulary:there's no other way}. This is a very good expression to know if you're in Japan.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Can't Be Helped",
                "Alternatives": "It's No Use, There's No Other Way",
                "Word Type": "expression"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3057\u304b\u305f\u304c\u306a\u3044"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
