                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "39519-subject-2863.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3057\u3085\u3046",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "20544-subject-2863.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The reading is the on'yomi reading that you learned with the kanji, probably because it normally gets added on to other things, like the names of the state. Examples: {span:lang=ja:\u30ab\u30ea\u30d5\u30a9\u30eb\u30cb\u30a2\u5dde}, {span:lang=ja:\u30cb\u30e5\u30fc\u30e8\u30fc\u30af\u5dde}, etc.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u30cb\u30e5\u30fc\u30e8\u30fc\u30af\u5dde\u306e\u51fa\u3057\u3093\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "I\u2019m from New York state."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30a2\u30e1\u30ea\u30ab\u304c\u3063\u3057\u3085\u3046\u56fd\u306b\u306f\u3001\u4e94\u5341\u306e\u5dde\u304c\u3042\u308a\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "There are fifty states in the United States."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30aa\u30ec\u30b4\u30f3\u5dde\u30dd\u30fc\u30c8\u30e9\u30f3\u30c9\u5e02\u306b\u306f\u3001Tofugu\u306e\u672c\u793e\u304c\u3042\u308b\u3002",
                    "en": "The headquarters of Tofugu is located in Portland, Oregon."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You've seen that {span:lang=ja:\u4ee3\u308f\u308b} means \"to substitute for\" or \"to replace.\" This is the noun version of that (no {span:lang=ja:\u3046} sound on the end). So, what is the noun version of those verb words? It would be {tooltip:Vocabulary:substitute} or {tooltip:Vocabulary:replacement}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Substitute",
                "Alternative": "Replacement",
                "Word Type": "noun, \u306e adjective"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u304b\u308f\u308a"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u304b\u308f\u308a",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "39426-subject-3443.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u304b\u308f\u308a",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "19513-subject-3443.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}You should be able to figure out the reading from learning {span:lang=ja:\u4ee3\u308f\u308b} in the previous level.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u4ee3\u308f\u308a\u306e\u5148\u751f\u3092\u3055\u304c\u3057\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "We\u2019re looking for a substitute teacher."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u4e16\u306e\u3069\u3053\u306b\u3082\u3001\u304b\u308c\u306e\u4ee3\u308f\u308a\u306f\u3044\u306a\u3044\u3093\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "There is no replacement for him anywhere in the world."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u65e5\u672c\u8a9e\u3092\u6559\u308f\u308b\u4ee3\u308f\u308a\u306b\u3001\u82f1\u8a9e\u3092\u6559\u3048\u308b\u3053\u3068\u304c\u3067\u304d\u307e\u3059\u3088\u3002",
                    "en": "I can teach you English and in exchange you can teach me Japanese."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Your {tooltip:Kanji:eye} is on what's {tooltip:Kanji:next}. How is this possible to see what's coming up? That's because you're looking at a {tooltip:Vocabulary:table of contents}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Table Of Contents",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3082\u304f\u3058"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3082\u304f\u3058",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "39445-subject-2872.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3082\u304f\u3058",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "19527-subject-2872.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The readings are the on'yomi readings, but you learned the kun'yomi reading for {span:lang=ja:\u76ee} before. So, you have to remember that this word uses {span:lang=ja:\u3082\u304f} instead of {span:lang=ja:\u3081}. To do that, imagine yourself looking at a {tooltip:Vocabulary:table of contents}. Instead of being a normal table of contents there, it's just line after line of text trying to {tooltip:Reading:mock} ({span:lang=ja:\u3082\u304f}) you. Come up with what those lines are and think about how you're being mocked.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u76ee\u6b21\u306e\u30da\u30fc\u30b8\u304c\u3084\u3076\u308c\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "The table of contents page was torn out."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u5148\u305a\u306f\u3058\u3081\u306b\u3001\u76ee\u6b21\u3092\u3054\u305b\u3064\u660e\u3044\u305f\u3057\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "First, I would like to explain the table of contents."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u672c\u5168\u90e8\u8aad\u307e\u306a\u3044\u3068\u3060\u3081\uff1f\u76ee\u6b21\u3060\u3051\u8aad\u3080\u3063\u3066\u3053\u3068\u306f\u7121\u7406\u304b\u306a\uff1f",
                    "en": "Do I have to read this whole book? Can\u2019t I just read the table of contents?"
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
