{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Kanji:now} {tooltip:Kanji:year} is which year? It is {tooltip:Vocabulary:this year}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "This Year",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3053\u3068\u3057"
                    },
                    {
                        "primary": false,
                        "reading": "\u3053\u3093\u306d\u3093"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3053\u3093\u306d\u3093",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "41137-subject-3444.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3053\u3093\u306d\u3093",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "41142-subject-3444.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3053\u3068\u3057",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "39448-subject-3444.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3053\u3068\u3057",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "19528-subject-3444.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}With the first reading (the more common one), the {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3093} of {span:lang=ja:\u4eca} is actually just {span:lang=ja:\u3053}. This gets shortened sometimes, so look out for it. Also, the {span:lang=ja:\u5e74} is the kun'yomi reading for some reason. It's a total mystery why this is. Perhaps the ancient aliens know, but I do not. You're going to have to remember this exception with that brain of yours and rely on the SRS to bestow this knowledge upon you.\n{splitParent}The second reading, which is less common but still common enough, makes more sense. It is {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3093\u306d\u3093} and just follows the jukugo patterns.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u5c71\u4e0b\u3055\u3093\u306e\u304b\u305e\u304f\u306f\u3001\u4eca\u5e74\u306e\u51ac\u4f11\u307f\u306f\u3069\u3053\u306b\u884c\u304d\u307e\u3059\u304b\uff1f",
                    "en": "Where is your family going to go on winter vacation this year, Yamashita?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u304a\u304d\u306a\u308f\u306e\u305b\u3044\u4eba\u3057\u304d\u306f\u4eca\u5e74\u3082\u5927\u3042\u308c\u3060\u3063\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "The coming-of-age ceremony in Okinawa went wild again this year."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3088\u3046\u3084\u304f\u4eca\u5e74\u306e\u5e74\u30922013\u5e74\u3068\u66f8\u304f\u3053\u3068\u3092\u899a\u3048\u305f\u3068\u3053\u308d\u3067\u3001\u5e74\u304c2014\u5e74\u306b\u5909\u308f\u3063\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "As soon as I got used to writing 2013 as the date for this year, 2014 arrived."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You have {tooltip:Kanji:three} {tooltip:Kanji:hundred}s. What does that come out to? {tooltip:Vocabulary:Three hundred}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Three Hundred",
                "Word Type": "numeral"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3055\u3093\u3073\u3083\u304f"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3055\u3093\u3073\u3083\u304f",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "39572-subject-7456.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3055\u3093\u3073\u3083\u304f",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "19661-subject-7456.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The reading is a little bit weird, so be careful. You should already know the on'yomi readings, but the {span:lang=ja:\u3072\u3083\u304f} rendakus to {span:lang=ja:\u3073\u3083\u304f}. This pattern will repeat itself with {span:lang=ja:\u4e09} + many counter kanjis that can rendaku, so pay attention for it as you read Japanese.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u307e\u3093\u304c\u3092\u4e09\u767e\u3055\u3064\u3082\u3063\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "I have three hundred manga."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3072\u308b\u306d\u3092\u3057\u3066\u3044\u305f\u6642\u306b\u3001\u3060\u308c\u304b\u304c\u30a4\u30f3\u30bf\u30fc\u30db\u30f3\u3092\u4e09\u767e\u56de\u3082\u306a\u3089\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "When I was taking a nap, somebody rang the doorbell 300 times."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u4e00\u5e74\u306f\u4e09\u767e\u516d\u5341\u4e94\u65e5\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "One year is three hundred and sixty-five days."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "When you {tooltip:Kanji:pull} something and {tooltip:Kanji:separate} it, you are making everything even. In a game, this would be a {tooltip:Vocabulary:tie} or a {tooltip:Vocabulary:draw}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Tie",
                "Alternative": "Draw",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3072\u304d\u308f\u3051"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3072\u304d\u308f\u3051",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "35138-subject-3405.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3072\u304d\u308f\u3051",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "15235-subject-3405.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}See the hiragana stuck in there? That's hinting that these use the kun'yomi readings. You learned the kun'yomi readings of these two kanji separately in the previous level, so if you know those you should know this word's reading as well.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u30d0\u30b9\u30b1\u306e\u3057\u3042\u3044\u306f\u3001\u5f15\u304d\u5206\u3051\u3067\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "The basketball game was a tie."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30a2\u30e1\u30ea\u30ab\u306e\u30d9\u30b9\u30c8\u30d4\u30c3\u30af\u30a2\u30c3\u30d7\u30c8\u30e9\u30c3\u30af\u3092\u304d\u3081\u308b\u305f\u305f\u304b\u3044\u306f\u3001\u4eca\u306e\u3068\u3053\u308d\u30c8\u30e8\u30bf\u3068\u30d5\u30a9\u30fc\u30c9\u306e\u5f15\u304d\u5206\u3051\u306e\u3058\u3087\u3046\u305f\u3044\u304c\u3064\u3065\u3044\u3066\u3044\u308b\u3002",
                    "en": "The battle to determine the best pickup truck in America is currently a draw between Toyota and Ford."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u79c1\u306f\u3001\u3058\u3083\u3093\u3051\u3093\u304c\u4e09\u5bfe\u4e09\u306e\u5f15\u304d\u5206\u3051\u306b\u7d42\u308f\u3063\u305f\u3053\u3068\u306b\u3068\u3066\u3082\u8179\u3092\u7acb\u3066\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "I was so upset that our rock-paper-scissors game ended in a three way tie."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Remember {span:lang=ja:\u8db3\u308a\u308b}? That is \"to be enough.\" How can you make something be enough? You keep adding things to the pile until you reach that point where there are enough! That's why this verb is {tooltip:Vocabulary:to add}, because you keep adding until you {span:lang=ja:\u8db3\u308a\u308b}. Maybe you add so much that someone tries to {tooltip:Reading:sue} ({span:lang=ja:\u3059}) you for that nasty pile of squirrels you keep outside.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "To Add",
                "Word Type": "transitive verb, godan verb"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u305f\u3059"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u305f\u3059",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "39404-subject-7477.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u305f\u3059",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "19498-subject-7477.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The reading is the same as {span:lang=ja:\u8db3\u308a\u308b}, so if you know that you can add things up to this.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u4e09\u8db3\u3059\u4e8c\u306f\u4e94\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "Three plus two is five."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u30b9\u30fc\u30d7\u3001\u3082\u3046\u5c11\u3057\u6c34\u3092\u8db3\u3057\u305f\u65b9\u304c\u3044\u3044\u3093\u3058\u3083\u306a\u3044\u304b\u306a\u3002",
                    "en": "Maybe it would be better to add more water to this soup."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u3053\u306b\u3082\u3046\u5c11\u3057\u6587\u7ae0\u3092\u66f8\u304d\u8db3\u3059\u3053\u3068\u306f\u3067\u304d\u307e\u3059\u304b\uff1f",
                    "en": "Can you add a little more to this sentence here?"
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "When you {tooltip:Kanji:exit} and {tooltip:Kanji:come}, apparently it means {tooltip:Vocabulary:to be able to do}. I think of it like you're leaving a location ({span:lang=ja:\u51fa}) to do a task and then you come ({span:lang=ja:\u6765\u308b}) back after you did it. Perhaps you were waiting in a room training like someone from Dragonball. When you leave the room, you are ready to fight the enemy and win. You train in that room {tooltip:Vocabulary:to be able to do} whatever you need to do, and only leave and come back alive because you were able to do it.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "To Be Able To Do",
                "Alternatives": "To Be Ready, To Be Made",
                "Word Type": "intransitive verb, ichidan verb"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3067\u304d\u308b"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3067\u304d\u308b",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "39599-subject-3418.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3067\u304d\u308b",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "19670-subject-3418.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The readings are a bit strange. Both kun'yomi, where {span:lang=ja:\u51fa} is {span:lang=ja:\u3067} and {span:lang=ja:\u6765\u308b} is {span:lang=ja:\u304d\u308b}. This is different from the reading you learned with {span:lang=ja:\u6765\u308b} ({span:lang=ja:\u304f\u308b}), though, so be careful. If you can remember that and remember the kun'yomi readings from the previous vocab, you should be able to learn this one's reading as well.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u3084\u3063\u3068JLPTN1\u306b\u3054\u3046\u304b\u304f\u3059\u308b\u3053\u3068\u304c\u51fa\u6765\u307e\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "I was finally able to pass JLPT N1."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30c7\u30a3\u30ca\u30fc\u304c\u51fa\u6765\u305f\u3088\u3002",
                    "en": "Dinner's ready!"
                },
