{
            "mnemonic": "The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Bird",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3068\u308a"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3068\u308a",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "39751-subject-2985.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3068\u308a",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "19847-subject-2985.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}Since this word is made up of a single kanji, it should use the kun'yomi reading. When learning the kanji, you didn't learn that reading, so here's a mnemonic to help you with this word: \n{splitParent}When you think about birds, what is the most common bird you can think of? What bird do you hate the most or like eating the most? Either one will work. Now, imagine yourself going up to that bird. You did something terrible. You {tooltip:Reading:tore} ({span:lang=ja:\u3068\u308a}) off its wing and started eating it (pretend it's cooked first, if that makes you less nauseous).\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u516c\u3048\u3093\u306b\u306f\u3001\u304d\u308c\u3044\u306a\u9ce5\u304c\u305f\u304f\u3055\u3093\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "There are a lot of beautiful birds in this park."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3051\u3055\u30ab\u30fc\u30c6\u30f3\u3092\u3042\u3051\u305f\u3089\u3001\u4f55\u3082\u304b\u3082\u304c\u9ce5\u306e\u3075\u3093\u306b\u304a\u304a\u308f\u308c\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "When I opened my curtain this morning, everything was covered with bird poop."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u81ea\u5206\u304c\u9ce5\u3060\u3063\u305f\u3089\u3044\u3044\u306e\u306b\u306a\u3068\u601d\u3046\u3088\u3002\u3060\u3063\u3066\u3001\u305d\u3046\u3059\u308c\u3070\u3082\u3046\u9ce5\u307f\u305f\u3044\u306b\u6b69\u3044\u3066\u3044\u308b\u3068\u304b\u3089\u304b\u308f\u308c\u306a\u3044\u3060\u308d\u3046\u304b\u3089\u306d\u3002",
                    "en": "I wish I was a bird because then I wouldn\u2019t get made fun of for walking like one."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This is a single kanji with an {span:lang=ja:\u3044} on the end, meaning you know it's probably an adjective. What's the adjective form of {tooltip:Kanji:black}? It's also {tooltip:Vocabulary:black}. Special Note: The noun version of black also exists in Japanese. It is just {span:lang=ja:\u9ed2} with the same pronunciation, but minus the {span:lang=ja:\u3044} which makes it adjective-tastic.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Black",
                "Word Type": "\u3044 adjective"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u304f\u308d\u3044"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u304f\u308d\u3044",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "19991-subject-2988.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u304f\u308d\u3044",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "39893-subject-2988.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun'yomi reading. You didn't learn that reading with this kanji, so here's a mnemonic to help you:\n{splitParent}Think of black things. One very common black thing is the common {tooltip:Reading:crow} ({span:lang=ja:\u304f\u308d}). Associate those two together.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u305d\u306e\u4e0a\u306e\u3001\u9ed2\u3044\u307c\u3046\u5b50\u3092\u3068\u3063\u3066\u304f\u3060\u3055\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "Can you get me the black hat up there?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3042\u306e\u9ed2\u3072\u3052\u306e\u7537\u306f\u306f\u3089\u9ed2\u3044\u3053\u3068\u3067\u6709\u540d\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "That man with the black goatee is notorious for being black-hearted."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u79c1\u306e\u4e00\u756a\u597d\u304d\u306a\u30c6\u30ec\u30d3\u756a\u7d44\u306f\u3001\u300c\u9ed2\u3044\u753b\u9762\u300d\u3067\u3059\u3002\u3053\u306e\u756a\u7d44\u306f\u3001\u505c\u96fb\u306b\u306a\u3063\u305f\u6642\u3060\u3051\u653e\u9001\u3055\u308c\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "My favorite channel on TV is called \"Black Screen.\" It only airs when the power goes off."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
