{
            "mnemonic": "There's a {tooltip:Radical:cross} sticking out the top of the {tooltip:Radical:sun} and another {tooltip:Radical:cross} sticking out the bottom. The {tooltip:Radical:moon} is off to the right. That's because it's the {tooltip:Kanji:morning}, which is when the sun and moon switch over. Those crosses are making sure the sun stays high in the sky for the rest of the day.",
            "hints": "Imagine a sun attached up in the sky with two crosses, which act as scaffolding, and the moon gradually fading off to the right as the morning wears on.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Morning"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
