            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You {tooltip:Radical:loiter} around with a {tooltip:Radical:weapon} because you're currently doing your {tooltip:Kanji:service} and {tooltip:Kanji:duty} to your country. You're standing guard outside, waiting just in case something will happen.",
            "hints": "Imagine standing around with a weapon in your hands. It feels more like you're doing nothing, loitering around the wall, but this is how you offer your service to your country, so you have to do it.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Service",
                "Alternative": "Duty"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3084\u304f, \u3048\u304d",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Somehow your duty has become pushing this stubborn, hairy yak away from the entrance to your country. You poke it in the butt with your weapon, but it doesn't seem to care.",
                "mnemonic": "You're about halfway through your {tooltip:Kanji:service}, when a {tooltip:Reading:yak} ({span:lang=ja:\u3084\u304f}) walks by. It stops directly in front of you and grunts. \"Excuse me, Mr. Yak, but it's my {tooltip:Kanji:duty} to escort you away from my country.\""
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:stone} {tooltip:Radical:lantern} is an excellent way to {tooltip:Kanji:sharpen} anything, even in the dark. Your knives, your forks, your spoons, and even your sword.",
            "hints": "Imagine walking around at night and you need to sharpen your sword. You walk up to a stone lantern and rub your sword all over it to sharpen it. Touch the blade. Indeed, it's been sharpened!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Sharpen"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3051\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3068",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "You'll want to remember this character, because he will come up many times throughout your WaniKani journey. Ken the samurai used to work for WaniKani, but then he went rogue because he thought WaniKani wasn't being radical enough. So now he wanders the website carrying his squid friend (who you'll meet later) in a bucket.{splitParent}To help you visualize him, imagine a samurai with armor made from crab shells, a helmet that is just an alligator head, and swords with crab claw hand guards. His great beard and mustache are styled in the shape of whatever kanji is giving you the most trouble at the time.",
                "mnemonic": "Why did you {tooltip:Kanji:sharpen} your blade? Because you are about to do battle with {tooltip:Reading:Ken} ({span:lang=ja:\u3051\u3093}), the famous samurai warrior."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The self radical is a bit modified, with things sticking out, but that\u2019s because it\u2019s falling down a slide.",
            "hints": "Apparently, you\u2019re having some kind of internal crisis. Come up with a rough one and you\u2019ll remember this kanji better.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Somebody",
                "Alternatives": "Someone, Body"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Just make the shin hit as painful as possible in your imagination and you\u2019ll surely remember this kanji\u2019s on\u2019yomi.",
                "mnemonic": "You\u2019re falling down this metaphorical slide\u2026 And then you hit {tooltip:Kanji:somebody}\u2026 and you hit them really hard. On your metaphorical {tooltip:Reading:shin} ({span:lang=ja:\u3057\u3093}). You split it in half and it\u2019s at a right angle. The pain is incredible (shins have a lot of nerves!). It may have been broken, and you may not be able to walk correctly again\u2026 metaphorically speaking, that is."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:coffin} has been left out in the {tooltip:Radical:sun} for too long. When dead bodies are out in the sun like this, they really begin to decompose. You are wondering who this person is, though. You can't tell because it's fallen apart too much. You look on the coffin. There's a label, but it only says \"{tooltip:Kanji:someone}.\"",
            "hints": "Smell the stench of the decomposing body. Also, feel really curious as to who this person is. It came from somewhere that gives you a reason to believe it's someone you know. Then, feel the disappointment as you read the label that says \"someone\" instead of an actual name. \"Someone,\" you repeat. \"Someone.\"",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Someone",
                "Alternative": "Somebody"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3083",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3082\u306e",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "How strange that a shaman was delivered to you this way! Come up with a story explaining why this shaman's body is here at your door, rotting in the sun. Make it ridiculous and interesting so you remember it.",
                "mnemonic": "To remember the sound {span:lang=ja:\u3057\u3083} we use \"shaman.\""
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:branch} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:branch} kanji are the same! This kanji also means {tooltip:Kanji:support}. To remember it, think about a tree with lots of branches. The branches support each other and the trunk of the tree. If you were to climb the tree, the branches would be able to support your weight too!",
            "hints": "Picture a branch supporting the weight of you, your entire family, and maybe a few friends.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Support",
                "Alternative": "Branch"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3055\u3055",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "The big sheep walks up the side of the tree with its weird hooves and starts to put its weight on the branch. \"No no no!\" you and your whole family scream. The branch can't support the weight of the sheep. It snaps! You all tumble to the ground.{splitParent}The end.",
                "mnemonic": "You're happily sitting on this {tooltip:Kanji:branch}, letting it {tooltip:Kanji:support} all this weight, when a {tooltip:Reading:shee}p ({span:lang=ja:\u3057}) starts to climb the tree. A very, very big sheep. Uh oh... no no, sheep. If you get on this branch it won't be able to support all this weight!!!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Radical:Say} it with your {tooltip:Radical:tongue}. If you do, you'll be able to {tooltip:Kanji:talk}. You'll be able to {tooltip:Kanji:speak}.",
            "hints": "Your problem is you weren't saying it with your tongue before. Once you start using your tongue, you're able to speak suddenly!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Talk",
                "Alternative": "Speak"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u308f",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306f\u306a, \u306f\u306a\u3057",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "You decided to teach a walrus how to speak because it seemed like the hardest thing to teach, with its tusks and all that. It turned out to be really easy. Although, you may have picked the Justin Long walrus, by accident. Either way, this walrus can speak! And you definitely (probably) helped.",
                "mnemonic": "If something uses their tongue, it will be able to {tooltip:Kanji:talk} and {tooltip:Kanji:speak}. To prove this, you teach a {tooltip:Reading:wa}lrus ({span:lang=ja:\u308f}) to use its tongue, and lo and behold, it speaks to you!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "In your {tooltip:Radical:fingers} is a {tooltip:Radical:weapon}, so you {tooltip:Kanji:throw} it. Maybe you're angry and you threw the weapon at a person you don't like. Maybe you're a pacifist and you threw it away so no one else could use it. Either way, when you have a weapon in your fingers you have to throw it.",
            "hints": "Imagine a weapon in between your fingers. It doesn't matter what kind. Now feel the weight as you throw it.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Throw"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3068\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306a",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine throwing the weapon in your fingers just before you walk into {span:lang=ja:\u3068\u3046\u304d\u3087\u3046}. You can see the lights, smell the smells! Throw your weapons and your cares away!",
                "mnemonic": "You {tooltip:Kanji:throw} your weapon away because you're about to enter {span:lang=ja:\u3068\u3046\u304d\u3087\u3046} and there are no weapons allowed there. There is, however, a big pile of thrown away weapons by the border, which is where you're throwing yours right now."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You want to take a {tooltip:Radical:measurement} of what you're {tooltip:Radical:writing} to make sure it's long enough to hand in to your professor, when you realize it's the {tooltip:Kanji:opposite} of what you should be doing. You don't measure writing, you check things like word count and page numbers. Now it's going to be you {tooltip:Kanji:versus} the teacher on who determines how long this thing you're writing should be.",
            "hints": "Okay. It's going to be you versus the teacher. Subject? Measuring physical length versus checking word count.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Versus",
                "Alternative": "Opposite"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Next to your nicely measured writing, tape a brand new tie with a sticky note that says, \"I hope this isn't the opposite of what you want, because I really need this A.\"",
                "mnemonic": "You let your nerves get the better of you and right when it's about to be you {tooltip:Kanji:versus} your professor, you hand him a new {tooltip:Reading:tie} ({span:lang=ja:\u305f\u3044}). Is this also the {tooltip:Kanji:opposite} of what you should be doing? Probably, since it's bribery. But no one can say no to a tie, right?"
            }
        }

