                        "pronunciation": "\u306a\u3093\u3079\u3044",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "20144-subject-3430.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}This is a jukugo word, which usually means on'yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you'll know how to read this as well.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u5b66\u751f\u6642\u4ee3\u306b\u3001\u30d0\u30c3\u30af\u30d1\u30c3\u30ab\u30fc\u3068\u3057\u3066\u5357\u7c73\u3092\u308a\u3087\u884c\u3057\u307e\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "In my school days, I took a backpacking trip through South America."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u30db\u30c6\u30eb\u306e\u6c34\u9053\u6c34\u306f\u3001\u5357\u7c73\u3067\u4eca\u307e\u3067\u306e\u3093\u3060\u6c34\u306e\u4e2d\u3067\u4e00\u756a\u304a\u3044\u3057\u3044\u3002",
                    "en": "This hotel's tap water is the best water I\u2019ve ever had in South America."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3054\u3081\u3093\u306a\u3055\u3044\u3002\u5357\u7c73\u306f\u30a2\u30e1\u30ea\u30ab\u306e\u4e00\u90e8\u3060\u3068\u601d\u3063\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "I\u2019m sorry. I thought South America was a part of the United States of America."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Kanji:South} {tooltip:Kanji:mouth} isn't actually a mouth you find in the south pole or anything, it's an entrance or exit. This is kind of like how {span:lang=ja:\u5165\u308a\u53e3} means entrance (the {span:lang=ja:\u53e3} represents a doorway). You'll see this word a lot in places like train stations or airports. It means both {tooltip:Vocabulary:South exit} and {tooltip:Vocabulary:South entrance}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "South Exit",
                "Alternatives": "South Entrance, Southern Exit, Southern Entrance, South Gate",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u307f\u306a\u307f\u3050\u3061"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u307f\u306a\u307f\u3050\u3061",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "40074-subject-3432.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u307f\u306a\u307f\u3050\u3061",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "20166-subject-3432.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The reading for these kanji are both kun'yomi. Probably because it's two words put together (South + Exit) rather than a single word. Also, body parts tend to change words into kun'yomi readings as well. So, you've seen vocab words that give you the kun'yomi reading of both {span:lang=ja:\u5357} and {span:lang=ja:\u53e3}, right? Now you just need to combine the two vocab readings into one to be able to read this word. Just watch out for the rendaku that changes {span:lang=ja:\u304f\u3061} to {span:lang=ja:\u3050\u3061}.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u305d\u3053\u306b\u884c\u304f\u306a\u3089\u3001\u5357\u53e3\u304b\u3089\u51fa\u308b\u306e\u304c\u4e00\u756a\u8fd1\u3044\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "If you\u2019re going there, leaving from the south exit is the fastest."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u30e2\u30fc\u30eb\u306e\u5357\u53e3\u3067\u4ecaUFO\u304c\u898b\u3089\u308c\u308b\u3088\uff01",
                    "en": "You can see a UFO at the southern entrance of this mall right now!"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u65b0\u5bbf\u5357\u53e3\u3067\u30ca\u30f3\u30d1\u3055\u308c\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "I was hit on at the south exit of Shinjuku station."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Kanji:sky} {tooltip:Kanji:interval} is an area of the sky. In places that are just empty sky, there is only {tooltip:Vocabulary:space}. Of course, not technically... there's something there, but back in the day when kanji was being made, nobody knew that. This word can mean both space in regards to outer space as well as the space around you in your room.",
            "hints": "",
