                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "20288-subject-3457.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3053\u306e\u307e\u3048",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "40196-subject-3457.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The reading for {span:lang=ja:\u524d} is the kun'yomi, like the reading for the vocabulary word {span:lang=ja:\u524d}. So long as you know that word, you'll be able to read this one too. The reason it's not on'yomi is because it's like two words put together. The word {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u306e} and the word {span:lang=ja:\u524d}.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u305d\u3046\u3044\u3048\u3070\u3001\u3053\u306e\u524d\u3001\u3057\u3087\u3046\u3066\u3093\u304c\u3044\u3067\u3055\u304f\u3089\u5b50\u3061\u3083\u3093\u3068\u30d0\u30c3\u30bf\u30ea\u4f1a\u3063\u305f\u3088\u3002",
                    "en": "That reminds me, last time I was at the shopping district I ran into Sakurako."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u524d\u3001\u65e5\u672c\u3054\u306e\u66f2\u306eCD\u3092\u8cb7\u3063\u305f\u3093\u3060\u3088\u306d\u3002",
                    "en": "I recently bought a Japanese music CD."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u524d\u306b\u8d64\u8272\u306e\u82b1\u3092\u7f6e\u304f\u306e\u306f\u3069\u3046\uff1f",
                    "en": "What about putting red flowers in front of this?"
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Kanji:time} {tooltip:Kanji:interval of time} is just the {tooltip:Vocabulary:time}. As is often the case, if two similar meaning kanji are combined into one word, they tend to produce a word with a similar meaning. Excuse me, friend, what's the {span:lang=ja:\u6642\u9593}?",
