                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}This is a jukugo word, which usually means on'yomi readings from the kanji. If you know the readings of your kanji you'll know how to read this as well.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u548c\u5ba4\u3067\u306d\u308b\u306e\u306f\u3001\u3053\u308c\u304c\u306f\u3058\u3081\u3066\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "This is my first time sleeping in a Japanese-style room."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u548c\u5ba4\u3001\u30c9\u30c3\u30b0\u30d5\u30fc\u30c9\u3068\u30b3\u30a6\u30a4\u30c1\u306e\u8db3\u306e\u306b\u304a\u3044\u304c\u307e\u3056\u3063\u305f\u3088\u3046\u306a\u306b\u304a\u3044\u304c\u3057\u306a\u3044\uff1f",
                    "en": "Don't you think this Japanese-style room smells like a mix of dog food and your feet, Koichi?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u79c1\u306e\u304a\u5144\u3061\u3083\u3093\u306f\u3001\u548c\u5ba4\u3067\u5927\u304d\u306a\u8718\u86db\u3092\u98fc\u3063\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "My older brother has a huge pet spider in his Japanese-style room."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Kanji:temporary} {tooltip:Kanji:name}s. These are the {tooltip:Vocabulary:kana} (that's hiragana and katakana). Why are the \"temporary names\" kana, though? Perhaps they were never meant to be a main way to write the names of the kanji... They are just a temporary way to write the name of kanji you don't know how to write the \"correct\" way (aka kanji)? That's just a guess, but it should help you to remember that this word means \"kana,\" at least.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Kana",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u304b\u306a"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u304b\u306a",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "20317-subject-3157.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u304b\u306a",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "40225-subject-3157.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The reading for {span:lang=ja:\u4eee} is the one you learned with the kanji. The {span:lang=ja:\u540d} portion is not, however, and uses its kun'yomi reading ({span:lang=ja:\u306a}), which you saw with the kanji {span:lang=ja:\u540d} ({span:lang=ja:\u306a}). How to remember all this, though? Think of it this way: \n{splitParent}The people who made kanji really don't want you to know {tooltip:Vocabulary:kana}. It's bad for business. They want anyone who uses the word \"kana\" to suffer, so they decided to make one part on'yomi and another part kun'yomi. Don't let them get you!\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u307e\u305a\u306f\u3001\u4eee\u540d\u3092\u304b\u304f\u308c\u3093\u3057\u3085\u3046\u304b\u3089\u59cb\u3081\u307e\u3057\u3087\u3046\u3002",
                    "en": "First, let\u2019s begin by practicing writing kana."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30ef\u30cb\u30ab\u30cb\u3067\u304b\u3093\u5b57\u3092\u3079\u3093\u5f37\u3059\u308b\u306e\u306b\u3001\u307e\u305a\u306f\u4eee\u540d\u3092\u5168\u90e8\u77e5\u3063\u3066\u3044\u308b\u5fc5\u8981\u304c\u3042\u308b\u3002",
                    "en": "In order to study kanji on WaniKani, you must first know all of the kana."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u66f8\u985e\u306e\u6f22\u5b57\u5168\u90e8\u306b\u3001\u632f\u308a\u4eee\u540d\u3092\u632f\u3063\u3066\u3082\u3089\u3048\u307e\u305b\u3093\u304b\uff1f",
                    "en": "Could you add kana to all the kanji in this document, please?"
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Kanji:Life} {tooltip:Kanji:guarantee} is something you get on someone else. If their life ends, it's guaranteed with money. That's a {tooltip:Vocabulary:life insurance} policy, for yah! (A.K.A. {tooltip:Vocabulary:life assurance} in some parts of the world!)",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Life Insurance",
                "Word Type": "noun"
