{
            "mnemonic": "You know that the word {span:lang=ja:\u4ed8\u3051\u308b} means \"to attach\" and you know that the kanji {span:lang=ja:\u4ed8} means {tooltip:Kanji:attach}. So, this word probably has something to do with attaching. This is the intransitive version, though, where something is attached (and you're not doing the attaching). You can use the {span:lang=ja:\u304f} as a hint. You see something attached on the wall. You say \"Coo'\"",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "To Be Attached",
                "Word Type": "intransitive verb, godan verb"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3064\u304f"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3064\u304f",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "20441-subject-3475.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3064\u304f",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "40352-subject-3475.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The reading can be derived from {span:lang=ja:\u4ed8\u3051\u308b}, a word you should have seen a few levels ago. If you know that word's reading you should be able to figure out this word's reading as well (and vice versa).\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u4eca\u3053\u306e\u30d1\u30bd\u30b3\u30f3\u3092\u8cb7\u3046\u3068\u3001\u30aa\u30de\u30b1\u304c\u4ed8\u304f\u306e\u3067\u3001\u304a\u3068\u304f\u3067\u3059\u3088\u3002",
                    "en": "If you buy this computer now, you\u2019ll get something thrown in, so it\u2019s a deal."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30de\u30a4\u30b1\u30eb\u306eT\u30b7\u30e3\u30c4\u306b\u306f\u30c9\u30ec\u30c3\u30b7\u30f3\u30b0\u306e\u3057\u307f\u304c\u305f\u304f\u3055\u3093\u4ed8\u3044\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "Michael's t-shirts have a lot of salad dressing stains on them."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3042\u306a\u305f\u306e\u8033\u305f\u3076\u306b\u4ed8\u3044\u3066\u308b\u305d\u308c\u3001\u4f55\uff1f",
                    "en": "What\u2019s that thing attached to your earlobe?"
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an {span:lang=ja:\u3046} sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means both {tooltip:Kanji:music} and {tooltip:Kanji:bend}. In the case of this word, we're looking at \"bend.\" So, the verb form of bend is {tooltip:Vocabulary:to be bent}. This is when something bends on its own, not when you're doing the bending. It's just bending.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "To Be Bent",
                "Alternatives": "To Bend, To Curve, To Turn",
                "Word Type": "intransitive verb, godan verb"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u307e\u304c\u308b"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
