                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "40349-subject-3130.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}If you try {tooltip:Vocabulary:to hold} something in your hand long enough it will {tooltip:Reading:mo}ld ({span:lang=ja:\u3082}). And then that mold will cover your hand, then your arm, then your entire body. Basically, you should never hold anything for too long or you'll become a mold person.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u3042\u3089\u304d\u3055\u3093\u306f\u3001\u3051\u3057\u30b4\u30e0\u3092\u6301\u3063\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3059\u304b\uff1f",
                    "en": "Do you have an eraser, Araki?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30b3\u30a6\u30a4\u30c1\u306f\u3001\u5b9f\u306f\u306a\u3093\u3057\u304d\u3084\u304d\u3085\u3046\u3057\u3093\u3071\u3093\u54e1\u306e\u3057\u304b\u304f\u3092\u6301\u3063\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3059...\u3068\u3044\u3046\u306e\u306f\u5b9f\u306f\u3046\u305d\u3067\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "Actually, Koichi has a license to be a soft baseball referee. ...actually, that's a lie."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u4e00\u7b49\u306b\u5f53\u9078\u3057\u305f\u3053\u3068\u3092\u77e5\u3063\u305f\u6642\u3001\u5b9d\u304f\u3058\u3092\u6301\u3064\u624b\u306e\u9707\u3048\u304c\u6b62\u307e\u3089\u306a\u304b\u3063\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "When I realized I won the grand prize, the hand I was holding the lottery ticket with couldn\u2019t help but shake."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You know that the kanji means {tooltip:Kanji:mix} and the word {span:lang=ja:\u4ea4\u305c\u308b} means \"to mix,\" so it probably has something to do with that. This word means {tooltip:Vocabulary:to intersect}, which is kind of like mixing. When two things intersect with each other, they're getting mixed up, so that's where this word gets its meaning. ",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "To Intersect",
                "Alternatives": "To Cross, To Mingle",
                "Word Type": "intransitive verb, godan verb"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u307e\u3058\u308f\u308b"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u307e\u3058\u308f\u308b",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "20285-subject-3466.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u307e\u3058\u308f\u308b",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "40191-subject-3466.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The reading is almost the same as {span:lang=ja:\u4ea4\u305c\u308b} which you should be familiar with by now. It has the {span:lang=ja:\u307e} but it also has an extra {span:lang=ja:\u3058} in there. That {span:lang=ja:\u3058} is there because you intersect with a group of {tooltip:Reading:mage}s ({span:lang=ja:\u307e\u3058}) that catch you on fire as you walk past.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u30aa\u30ec\u30f3\u30b8\u3068\u3042\u3044\u8272\u304c\u4ea4\u308f\u308b\u3001\u5915\u3050\u308c\u306e\u7a7a\u304c\u5927\u3059\u304d\u306a\u306e\u3002",
                    "en": "I love the evening sky when orange meets indigo blue."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u30aa\u30d5\u30a3\u30b9\u3067\u306f\u3001\u6642\u3005\u3001\u65e5\u672c\u3068\u30a2\u30e1\u30ea\u30ab\u306e\u6587\u5316\u304c\u4ea4\u308f\u308a\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "In this office, Japanese and American culture sometimes intersect."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u5a46\u3061\u3083\u3093\u306e\u4e73\u306f\u3068\u3066\u3082\u9577\u3044\u306e\u3067\u3001\u305f\u307e\u306b\u76f8\u4e92\u306b\u4ea4\u308f\u308b\u3053\u3068\u3059\u3089\u3067\u304d\u308b\u3002",
                    "en": "My grandma\u2019s boobs are so long that they sometimes cross over each other."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an {span:lang=ja:\u3046} sound, you know this word is a verb. You already learned that {span:lang=ja:\u901a\u308b} means to pass, but this is a little different. You aren't the one passing, Sue ({span:lang=ja:\u3059}) is. So you have to let Sue pass. In other words, this verb is transitive, meaning it has an object. That's why it means {tooltip:Vocabulary:to let something pass} or {tooltip:Vocabulary:to put something through}.",
