{
            "mnemonic": "I bet you can guess that {span:lang=ja:\u30d5\u30e9\u30f3\u30b9} means \"France.\" So what happens when you add {tooltip:Kanji:language} to it? You get the \"France Language\" aka the {tooltip:Vocabulary:French Language}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "French Language",
                "Alternative": "French",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3075\u3089\u3093\u3059\u3054"
                    },
                    {
                        "primary": false,
                        "reading": "\u30d5\u30e9\u30f3\u30b9\u3054"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3075\u3089\u3093\u3059\u3054",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "3477-subject-3211.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3075\u3089\u3093\u3059\u3054",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "28103-subject-3211.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The reading is just the on'yomi reading for {span:lang=ja:\u8a9e} which is the reading you learned with the kanji. This is probably because it gets added onto a lot of other words so it's treated like a jukugo. For example: {span:lang=ja:\u65e5\u672c\u8a9e} = \"Japanese Language.\" {span:lang=ja:\u4e2d\u56fd\u8a9e} = \"Chinese Language.\" {span:lang=ja:\u30b9\u30da\u30a4\u30f3\u8a9e} = \"Spanish Language.\"\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u308f\u305f\u3057\u306e\u3080\u3059\u3081\u306f\u3001\u30d5\u30e9\u30f3\u30b9\u8a9e\u3082\u3048\u3044\u8a9e\u3082\u8a71\u305b\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "My daughter can speak both French and English."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30a2\u30d5\u30ea\u30ab\u306e\u591a\u304f\u306e\u56fd\u3067\u306f\u516c\u7528\u8a9e\u306b\u3048\u3044\u8a9e\u3068\u30d5\u30e9\u30f3\u30b9\u8a9e\u304c\u4f7f\u7528\u3055\u308c\u3066\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "In many African countries, both English and French are used as their official languages."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30d5\u30e9\u30f3\u30b9\u8a9e\u306e\u5b66\u7fd2\u306f\u50d5\u306b\u306f\u96e3\u3057\u3059\u304e\u308b\u3088\u3002\u3082\u3046\u305f\u304f\u5c71\u3060\uff01\u52d8\u5f01\u3057\u3066\u304f\u308c\u3088\uff01",
                    "en": "Learning French is too difficult! I can\u2019t take it anymore. Give me a break!"
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "So there's this weird thing with Japanese people. Many things that most people would consider \"green\" are actually \"blue\" in their eyes. So, things like plants and street lights are \"blue\" even though you may think of them as green. Remember that for this word, where we talk about a {tooltip:Kanji:blue} {tooltip:Kanji:leaf}. In your head, though, think \"green leaf.\" Now think of all things that have green leaves. This is {tooltip:Vocabulary:greenery}.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Fresh Leaves",
                "Alternatives": "Greenery, Young Leaves",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3042\u304a\u3070"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3042\u304a\u3070",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "3578-subject-3181.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3042\u304a\u3070",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "28141-subject-3181.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The readings for the kanji in this word are the kun'yomi readings. Since you've already seen the word {span:lang=ja:\u9752}, hopefully you can just combine it with {span:lang=ja:\u8449} (you learned the reading for this part with the kanji). Be sure to pay attention to the rendaku, though. {span:lang=ja:\u306f} goes to {span:lang=ja:\u3070} in this word, making it {span:lang=ja:\u3042\u304a\u3070}.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u9752\u8449\u304c\u76ee\u306b\u3057\u307f\u308b\u304d\u305b\u3064\u3068\u306a\u308a\u307e\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "It's that time of year again when the leaves make my eyes burn."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u5bb6\u306e\u306b\u308f\u306e\u9752\u8449\u306b\u866b\u304c\u3044\u3063\u3071\u3044\u4ed8\u3044\u3066\u308b\uff01",
                    "en": "There are a lot of bugs on the young leaves in our garden!"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u304a\u524d\u306e\u982d\u3001\u3061\u3087\u3063\u3068\u5909\u306a\u306b\u304a\u3044\u304c\u3059\u308b\u305e\u3002 \u4e7e\u71e5\u3057\u305f\u9752\u8449\u304b\u4f55\u304b\u307f\u305f\u3044\u306a\u3002",
                    "en": "Your head smells a little weird. It\u2019s like dried up leaves or something."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

