                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture this building and each of its floors. There should be one floor in particular with a waterfall coming out of it that you can climb with your kayak.",
                "mnemonic": "You need to use your ability to tell where {tooltip:Kanji:floor}s are from the outside of the building to find the {tooltip:Kanji:story} that lets {tooltip:Reading:kay}aks ({span:lang=ja:\u304b\u3044}) in. You're coming from the side of the building surrounded by water, so you need to find the kayak floor to get in."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's a {tooltip:Radical:canopy} and a man called the {tooltip:Radical:Yoga} {tooltip:Radical:King} standing in the middle of your {tooltip:Kanji:garden}.",
            "hints": "Somehow your little garden is now being used by the Yoga King to teach yoga to a whole group of random people under this canopy... you have no idea how they got back here.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Garden"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3066\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306b\u308f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "You're not sure what else to do, so you end up serving tater tots in your garden all day to stretchy strangers.",
                "mnemonic": "Not only is this Yoga King taking over your {tooltip:Kanji:garden}, but he's ordering you to serve food to his class. \"You there, peasant, fetch these people your freshest {tooltip:Reading:ta}ter ({span:lang=ja:\u3066\u3044}) tots from this puny garden to give them strength.\""
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's a {tooltip:Radical:tsunami} coming and you want to be {tooltip:Radical:together} with it by {tooltip:Radical:yourself} (oneself) so you go to the {tooltip:Kanji:harbor}.",
            "hints": "Picture the harbor. You're the only one there. The wind is whipping violently and all of the boats docked around you are being bounced around. You can see the tsunami rising out of the ocean and coming for you and the harbor.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Harbor",
                "Alternatives": "Port, Harbour"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3053\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307f\u306a\u3068",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Well this sucks. You wanted to enjoy being alone together with the tsunami and the harbor, but now {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061} is here ruining your peace. He's floating around in the harbor like everything isn't about to be destroyed, sipping on a fancy drink in his inner tube.",
                "mnemonic": "As you look around at the {tooltip:Kanji:harbor} in your last moments you suddenly see someone in the water. It's {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061}! He's sitting in an inner tube in the harbor waiting for the tsunami too."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "When {tooltip:Radical:someone} goes out in the {tooltip:Radical:sun}, they get {tooltip:Kanji:hot}. That\u2019s just how it is. Someone standing out in the sun for a long time will inevitably get hot. The sun is hot, and it makes you get hot too.",
            "hints": "Imagine you are this someone, standing out in the hot sun. Feel the sun\u2019s hot rays beating down on you as you stand outside. The hot sun makes you start to sweat and you get all sweaty and sticky from the heat. Note that this usually refers to the hot weather or the heat of an area you're in and doesn't refer to a hot \"thing.\"",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Hot",
                "Alternative": "Hot Weather"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3087",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3042\u3064",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine that great feeling when you go from hot and sticky outside to nice and air conditioned inside. Feel the heat transition on your skin as you step into an air conditioned room at Suzie\u2019s. It gives you goose bumps.",
                "mnemonic": "You need to get out of this {tooltip:Kanji:hot} sun and into some air conditioning. Then you remember there\u2019s a great air conditioning unit {tooltip:Reading:at Su}zie\u2019s ({span:lang=ja:\u3042\u3064}) house. Ah, Suzie \u2013 our savior! If you need me, I\u2019ll be at Suzie\u2019s place."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "If there's a {tooltip:Radical:tsunami} it's {tooltip:Radical:easy} to get {tooltip:Kanji:hot water}. Just stick your hand out the window as the tsunami goes by and poof! That water's HOT.",
            "hints": "Picture the boiling hot tsunami water as it comes for you. Psh, this will be easy.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Hot Water"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3068\u3046",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3086",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "You decided to live in a yurt because of the easy access it provides to hot water. They're so mobile!",
                "mnemonic": "As the {tooltip:Kanji:hot water} comes, you just lift up the bottom of the {tooltip:Reading:yu}rt ({span:lang=ja:\u3086}) you live in, and it comes flowing in. All of the cups, pots, and barrels you laid on your floor are now full of hot water."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:leader} of {tooltip:Radical:business}? Ha! That's {tooltip:Kanji:me}! That's {tooltip:Kanji:I}!",
            "hints": "This version of {tooltip:Kanji:I} is more boyish. It's a sort of young man/boy way of saying \"I,\" though you'll hear women use it too sometimes (it's a teensy bit tomboyish though).",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "I",
                "Alternative": "Me"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307c\u304f",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "I am beaucoup I am. (Is that a pun? Did I do it right?) I dunno. Just say {span:lang=ja:\u307c\u304f} a lot and try to connect all of your approximate knowledges of languages together. Merci {span:lang=ja:\u307c\u304f} from {span:lang=ja:\u307c\u304f}.",
                "mnemonic": "You use the word {tooltip:Kanji:I} a lot. You say it so much, you just start referring to yourself with the french {tooltip:Reading:beaucoup} ({span:lang=ja:\u307c\u304f}) for a lot. Because you love yourself a lot."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:bird} sits upon a {tooltip:Radical:mountain}. If you look at the radicals, the bird is quite large and the mountain quite small. Think of this in the mnemonic as well. The reason the mountain is small is because you can only see the top of it, sticking out of the water. On it is the bird. This small mountain with the bird on it is an {tooltip:Kanji:island}.",
            "hints": "Imagine the mountain as small as you'd like. Maybe it's only barely sticking out of the sea, and the bird is having trouble standing on it. Whatever you do, make sure you think of it as an island, possibly owned by the bird.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Island"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3068\u3046",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u307e",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "The Sheep Mama is a huge, majestic sheep with magical powers. She kicks the bird off the island with her hooves, and the bird squawks and flies away as fast as it can.",
                "mnemonic": "But this {tooltip:Kanji:island} doesn't only belong to the bird. In fact, the bird shouldn't be on this island, because it belongs to the {tooltip:Reading:She}ep {tooltip:Reading:Ma}ma ({span:lang=ja:\u3057\u307e}), who is like Mother Nature but for sheep. The all-powerful Sheep Mama comes to kick the bird off the island, because this place is only for sheep! All hail the Sheep Mama."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The people who {tooltip:Radical:stand} in your {tooltip:Radical:village} are {tooltip:Kanji:juvenile}s. They aren't babies, who crawl, and they aren't adults who sit in front of their computers all day. They're juveniles who stand.",
            "hints": "Picture all of the juveniles standing in your village. You used to stand out there too and it was really boring, but at least kids these days have phones to keep them company.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Juvenile"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3069\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Look in disdain at the juveniles standing around with their delicious doughnuts. You're really jealous, I mean, you're really... mad! Those lazy... um... ah man. You really want one of those doughnuts. You miss being young.",
                "mnemonic": "Right now all of the {tooltip:Kanji:juvenile}s are standing in line to get {tooltip:Reading:dou}ghnuts ({span:lang=ja:\u3069\u3046}). Free doughnuts, actually. Psh. Just like juveniles to get handouts, amirite?"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:shellfish} with a {tooltip:Radical:mouth} is a {tooltip:Kanji:member} of the invertebrate family. Yes, shellfish do have mouths, look it up!",
            "hints": "Imagine a shellfish opening and closing its mouth, talking to you and telling you what a proud member of its family it is. It\u2019s a strange sight, but picture it in your mind anyway.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Member"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3044\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine trying to go to the shellfish inn. Imagine all the members-only signs telling you that you can't come in. Womp womp.",
                "mnemonic": "If you were a {tooltip:Kanji:member} of the same shellfish family, you'd be able to go to the special shellfish {tooltip:Reading:inn} ({span:lang=ja:\u3044\u3093}). Every species has a members-only inn."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:stand}ing {tooltip:Radical:mustache} with {tooltip:Radical:legs} is able to sell {tooltip:Kanji:merchandise}. Most mustaches can\u2019t stand because they don\u2019t have legs. This mustache, however, does. It also has a keen business sense, which is why it\u2019s selling merchandise.",
            "hints": "Imagine this mustache entrepreneur, standing at his merchandise stand, selling his wares. He looks quite dignified, legs and all.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Merchandise"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3087\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3042\u304d\u306a",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Now all of the merchandise belongs to the Shougun. The mustache has nothing left to sell, but at least he's rich!",
                "mnemonic": "The mustache is selling his {tooltip:Kanji:merchandise} so well that even the {tooltip:Reading:Shou}gun ({span:lang=ja:\u3057\u3087\u3046}) notices. He leans down really close to the standing mustache man's merchandise and stays very still. Then he snaps his fingers and his servants come rushing out to buy it all!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Radical:Someone} decided to build a bunch of {tooltip:Radical:building}s and turn them into a {tooltip:Kanji:metropolis}. You don't know who they are, but now an absolute ton of other someones you don't know also live here in these buildings and work in this busy metropolis.",
            "hints": "Imagine just how many different someones live in this metropolis. There are so many people, you'll never meet them all!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Metropolis"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3068, \u3064",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307f\u3084\u3053",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "If you think about it, every metropolis is completely jam-packed-full of toes. Just picture it. Toes. Everywhere. In every building. Filling up the streets.",
                "mnemonic": "You try to picture the faces of all the people who live in this {tooltip:Kanji:metropolis}, but for some reason you can only picture their {tooltip:Reading:toe}s ({span:lang=ja:\u3068}). And there are ten times as many toes in this city than someones."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "When something is {tooltip:Radical:heavy}, you need a lot of {tooltip:Radical:power} to {tooltip:Kanji:move} it. It\u2019s not going to move unless you use a lot of power. That thing is heavy and unless you use all the power you got, it\u2019s not moving.",
            "hints": "Picture a massive 1 ton weight. It\u2019s heavy. You can\u2019t even begin to move it without some sort of powerful machine to help you. Imagine yourself trying to move it with just your bare hands.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Move"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3069\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3046\u3054",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Okay, so you don\u2019t have enough power to move this doughnut as is. In order to move it, you\u2019re going to have to use your superhuman power of eating. Once you eat enough of it, the remainder will be easy to move. Imagine yourself digging into this massive doughnut and taste its fresh baked goodness on your tongue. It\u2019s mighty tasty.",
                "mnemonic": "Remember that 1 ton weight? Well, actually it\u2019s a {tooltip:Reading:dou}ghnut ({span:lang=ja:\u3069\u3046}). A huge, massive, 1 ton doughnut. It\u2019s super heavy and hard to move, but it\u2019s delicious."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Radical:Bamboo}'s {tooltip:Radical:younger brother} is counted with an {tooltip:Kanji:ordinal number prefix}. An ordinal number is one that represents a position or rank in sequential order (like first, second, third, etc.). We use this ordinal number prefix before counting these things in Japanese sometimes. Like with bamboo, going down in order toward the youngest.",
            "hints": "Imagine a line of bamboo shoots arranged from oldest to youngest. They look like pipe organ pipes, or a xylophone, and now you need to count them.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Ordinal Number Prefix",
                "Alternatives": "Ordinal Prefix, Number"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3060\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Now they're in descending order already, and you can count them nice and easy thanks to your dye method.",
                "mnemonic": "To remember you need to use the {tooltip:Kanji:ordinal number prefix} before you count these bamboo, you {tooltip:Reading:dye} ({span:lang=ja:\u3060\u3044}) them."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:crab} left on the {tooltip:Radical:moon} for a {tooltip:Kanji:period of time} will surely die. Crabs aren\u2019t meant for the moon, let alone outer space. Why would you take a crab to the moon? You take it outside the spaceship for any period of time and it will surely die.",
            "hints": "I\u2019m sure you\u2019ve seen movies where people go out in space with no spacesuit. Now imagine this happening to the poor crab. It only takes a small period of time for the dark reaches of space to kill him.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Period Of Time",
                "Alternative": "Time Period"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304d",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine this crab floating around in an aquarium half filled with water in little to no gravity. The water is floating all about and the crab is confused as can be. He just wants the key so he can get out of this crazy moon box and back to the real ocean where he belongs.",
                "mnemonic": "In order for this crab to live on the moon for any {tooltip:Kanji:period of time}, you\u2019re going to need to put him in a special moon aquarium and lock the lid with a {tooltip:Reading:key} ({span:lang=ja:\u304d}). This crab is crafty, so after you lock it, take the key with you and don\u2019t let it out of your sight for any period of time. This crab knows how to use a key."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "If you want to {tooltip:Radical:fix} a {tooltip:Radical:tree} you have to {tooltip:Kanji:plant} it. It's pretty obvious that digging up the tree broke it. So fix it by planting it again. That is how plants work.",
            "hints": "Is your tree broken? Fix it up by planting it somewhere else. Somewhere with the right amount of light and no biting bugs.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Plant"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3087\u304f",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3046",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Have you ever seen a tree in shock? Imagine it now. It's shaking and dying and sad. Make sure you properly plant all your trees from now on.",
                "mnemonic": "If you don't {tooltip:Kanji:plant} a tree it goes into {tooltip:Reading:shock} ({span:lang=ja:\u3057\u3087\u304f}). Which, honestly, shouldn't be very shocking to you, since it's a tree."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:tree} {tooltip:Radical:root} is still just a {tooltip:Kanji:root}!",
            "hints": "Roots are roots, {span:lang=ja:\u306d}.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Root"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3053\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306d",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture a family of condors nestled at the bottom of a tree in its roots. Then a huge machine comes over and mows them all down as it cuts all the trees off their roots. Darn you humans! Do you have to ruin everything?!",
                "mnemonic": "Tree {tooltip:Kanji:root}s are the perfect roosting place for {tooltip:Reading:con}dors ({span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3093}). Unfortunately, living on roots is also why they're always endangered. Condors can be killed pretty easily living on the ground on roots like this."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "If you shoot a tall stalk of {tooltip:Radical:beans} with an {tooltip:Radical:arrow}, the bean plant will go from tall to {tooltip:Kanji:short}. When the beans are hit with that arrow, it severs the plant in two, and you are left with a stalk of beans that is much shorter than before.",
            "hints": "Picture yourself shooting this arrow. Imagine yourself nocking the arrow to the bow, and pulling the string back. That bean plant is not {tooltip:Kanji:short} enough, and you\u2019re going to do something about it.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Short"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307f\u3058\u304b",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine yourself being out in the sun for so long that you get an amazing tan. Your skin feels warm all over from all the sun you were getting throughout the day. Feel the warmth coming off of your tanned skin.",
                "mnemonic": "All this shooting of arrows to make the bean plants more short is taking a long time. Since you\u2019re out in the sun, you\u2019re starting to get a {tooltip:Reading:tan} ({span:lang=ja:\u305f\u3093}). After the day is finally over, your tan is really, really dark."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:king} {tooltip:Radical:request}s a {tooltip:Kanji:sphere}! He didn't say what kind though, so you're going to have to figure it out and bring him the right kind of sphere.",
            "hints": "Imagine all the different spheres you know and bring them to the king one by one. He doesn't seem to like any of them... think of more spheres!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Sphere",
                "Alternative": "Ball"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304d\u3085\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f\u307e",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Cucumbers aren't really spheres... they're circles at best if you cut them, but you can't tell the king that. Just let him eat your cucumbers and think they're sphere-shaped. Maybe next time use a melon-baller and make your cucumbers into spheres for him.",
                "mnemonic": "You can't seem to find the {tooltip:Kanji:sphere} the king requested, so you take a break, sit down, and take out your lunch. \"THAT!\" the king screams, \"THAT IS THE SPHERE I REQUESTED!\" You look down and he's pointing to the cut up {tooltip:Reading:cu}cumbers ({span:lang=ja:\u304d\u3085\u3046}) in your salad."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Radical:White} {tooltip:Radical:water} shoots out from a {tooltip:Kanji:spring} in the ground. Due to the force the water shoots from the spring, the water looks white with all the air bubbles.",
            "hints": "Imagine these geysers and the white water shooting from the springs under the ground. Now imagine yourself standing over one when the water shoots out. You\u2019d get super wet and probably knocked over due to the force of the spring water. Imagine this happening to yourself.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Spring",
                "Alternative": "Fountain"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305b\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3044\u305a\u307f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine this centaur standing over the white water. It\u2019s just like a shower for him! Hear him laughing with mirth as the whoosh of the spring water washes him down.",
                "mnemonic": "The only creature powerful enough to withstand the force of these white water {tooltip:Kanji:spring}s is the mythical {tooltip:Reading:centaur} ({span:lang=ja:\u305b\u3093}). His horse body allows him to be stable and withstand the white water shooting up from the spring. Centaurs are pretty impressive creatures."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:tsunami} lifts all the {tooltip:Radical:trash} out of the {tooltip:Radical:river} and it {tooltip:Kanji:stream}s out onto the shores.",
            "hints": "As the tsunami recedes, watch as all of the trash comes streaming out of the river and crowds the bank.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Stream"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
