{
            "mnemonic": "The {span:lang=ja:\u3054} just makes this word more polite and fancy. When it comes to food, it's nice to be those things. This has the same meaning as the kanji ({tooltip:Kanji:meal}) making it {tooltip:Vocabulary:meal}, but it also refers to {tooltip:Vocabulary:cooked rice} as well. So, \"Come eat your {span:lang=ja:\u3054\u98ef}\" can be both \"come eat your rice\" and \"come eat your meal.\" Really, since most Japanese meals come with rice, though, you can see how the overlap happens.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Meal",
                "Alternative": "Cooked Rice",
                "Word Type": "noun"
