                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The reading is the same as the one you learned with the kanji. You can laugh freely, now!\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u305d\u306e\u5192\u967a\u5bb6\u306f\u3001\u5f8c\u4e16\u306b\u540d\u3092\u6b8b\u3057\u305f\u3044\u3093\u3060\u3068\u7b11\u3063\u3066\u8a00\u3063\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "That adventurer laughed and said he wants to leave his name to posterity."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30af\u30ea\u30b9\u30c6\u30f3\u306f\u3001\u7b11\u3044\u306a\u304c\u3089\u30b3\u30a6\u30a4\u30c1\u306b\u8b1d\u3056\u3044\u3092\u8981\u6c42\u3057\u307e\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "Kristen demanded an apology from Koichi while she laughed."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u300c\u6628\u591c\u3001\u30d5\u30b0\u3068\u30c7\u30fc\u30c8\u3092\u3057\u305f\u306e\u3002\u300d\u300c\u3069\u3046\u3060\u3063\u305f\uff1f\u300d\u300c\u697d\u3057\u304b\u3063\u305f\u3002\u3081\u3061\u3083\u304f\u3061\u3083\u7b11\u3063\u305f\u308f\u3002\u300d",
                    "en": "\"I had a date with Fugu last night.\" \"How did it go?\" \"It was fun. I laughed a lot.\""
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an {span:lang=ja:\u3046} sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means {tooltip:Kanji:remainder}, but you can't really \"to remainder.\" What is something that's similar that would make sense? How about {tooltip:Vocabulary:to leave behind}. This is when you're actually doing the leaving behind too. For example, you eat some food and you leave behind your rice because you're a bad child. You can remember this because of {span:lang=ja:\u3059}. Every time you leave some food behind your mom would {tooltip:Reading:sue} you, because she was a terrible mom. Imagine this.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "To Leave Behind",
                "Alternative": "To Leave Over",
                "Word Type": "transitive verb, godan verb"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u306e\u3053\u3059"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u306e\u3053\u3059",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "6526-subject-3930.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u306e\u3053\u3059",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "29599-subject-3930.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}The reading for this word can come from level 14 item {span:lang=ja:\u6b8b\u308b}. Associate the two in your mind and you'll be able to read both.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u3053\u306e\u30af\u30ea\u30fc\u30df\u30fc\u30c1\u30ad\u30f3\u30dd\u30c3\u30c8\u30d1\u30a4\u3001\u660e\u65e5\u306b\u6b8b\u3057\u3066\u304a\u304b\u306a\u3044\uff1f",
                    "en": "Why don't we leave this creamy chicken pot pie for tomorrow?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30b3\u30a6\u30a4\u30c1\u306f\u3001\u30d3\u30a8\u30c8\u306b\u591a\u304c\u304f\u306e\u3057\u3083\u3063\u91d1\u3092\u6b8b\u3057\u3066\u6b7b\u3093\u3067\u3057\u307e\u3044\u307e\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "Koichi passed away and left a huge amount of debt behind for Viet."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u6628\u591cTofugu\u30aa\u30d5\u30a3\u30b9\u306b\u6ce5\u68d2\u304c\u5165\u308a\u3001Tofugu\u30b9\u30c6\u30c3\u30ab\u30fc\u3092\u4f55\u679a\u304b\u76d7\u3093\u3067\u884c\u3063\u305f\u306e\u3067\u3059\u304c\u3001\u99ac\u9e7f\u306a\u6ce5\u68d2\u305f\u3061\u306f\u3001\u6307\u7d0b\u3092\u6b8b\u3059\u3068\u3044\u3046\u521d\u6b69\u7684\u306a\u904e\u3061\u3082\u72af\u3057\u307e\u3057\u305f\u3002",
                    "en": "Thieves broke into the Tofugu office and stole some of our stickers last night, but they were so stupid that they made the elementary mistake of leaving behind their fingerprints."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an {span:lang=ja:\u3046} sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means {tooltip:Kanji:hard} so the verb vocab version? {tooltip:Vocabulary:To make hard}. You need to differentiate this from {span:lang=ja:\u56fa\u307e\u308b}, though, which means \"to harden\" (the hardening happens on its own). This word means \"to make hard,\" because you're doing the hardening. You can remember this because you want to make something hard. And to do that, you send special \"{tooltip:Reading:mail}\" ({span:lang=ja:\u3081\u308b}) to this thing you want to make hard, and this mail does that for you.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "To Make Hard",
                "Alternative": "To Harden",
                "Word Type": "transitive verb, ichidan verb"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
