                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "29172-subject-3797.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}If you're going {tooltip:Vocabulary:to be sad} about something right now, it will be {tooltip:Reading:kana} ({span:lang=ja:\u304b\u306a}). Don't use kana when you're trying to learn kanji! You'll be sad forever if you're stuck using kana your whole life, so don't let that habit start now, friend.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u304b\u306e\u5973\u306f\u5931\u308c\u3093\u3057\u3066\u3068\u3066\u3082\u60b2\u3057\u3093\u3067\u3044\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "She got dumped and she's very sad."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u4eba\u9593\u3060\u3051\u3067\u306f\u306a\u304f\u3001\u52d5\u7269\u3082\u4ef2\u9593\u306e\u6b7b\u3092\u60b2\u3057\u307f\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "Animals, as well as humans, are saddened by the deaths of their friends."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u305d\u3046\u60b2\u3057\u3080\u306a\u3063\u3066\u3002\u304a\u524d\u306e\u65e5\u672c\u8a9e\u306f\u4eca\u306f\u305d\u308c\u3067\u5341\u5206\u3060\u3088\u3002\u305f\u3060\u3001\u52c9\u5f37\u3059\u308b\u3053\u3068\u3092\u6295\u3052\u51fa\u3055\u305a\u306b\u3001\u5c11\u3057\u305a\u3064\u4e0a\u624b\u304f\u306a\u308c\u3070\u3044\u3044\u3055\u3002",
                    "en": "Don\u2019t be sad. Your Japanese is good enough for now. Just don\u2019t stop learning and you\u2019ll improve little by little."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an {span:lang=ja:\u3046} sound, you know this word is a verb. ",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "To Cure",
                "Alternatives": "To Heal, To Ease",
                "Word Type": "transitive verb, godan verb"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u306a\u304a\u3059"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u306a\u304a\u3059",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "6538-subject-3845.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u306a\u304a\u3059",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "29586-subject-3845.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}Since this word consists of a kanji with hiragana attached, you can bet that it will use the kun'yomi reading. You didn't learn that reading with this kanji, so here's a mnemonic to help you:\n{splitParent}I need you {tooltip:Vocabulary:to heal} him, and I need you to heal him right {tooltip:Reading:now} ({span:lang=ja:\u306a\u304a}). NOW NOW NOW! \n{splitParent}This also shares the reading for {span:lang=ja:\u76f4\u3059}, a very similar word you learned previously, which means \"to fix.\" I'm guessing that the Japanese word {span:lang=ja:\u306a\u304a\u3059} was only one word (\"to fix\" and \"to cure\" are pretty similar, right?). Then, there were two Chinese words for it (as you can see with the kanji), so the one Japanese word got split into two very similar words.\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u751f\u7406\u3064\u3046\u3092\u6cbb\u3059\u306b\u306f\u30a2\u30b9\u30d4\u30ea\u30f3\u3092\u98f2\u3080\u306e\u304c\u3044\u3044\u3088\u3002",
                    "en": "You should take some aspirin to ease your period pain."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30cf\u30fc\u30d6\u308a\u3087\u3046\u6cd5\u3067\u304c\u3093\u3092\u6cbb\u3059\u3053\u3068\u306b\u3064\u3044\u3066\u3001\u3069\u3046\u601d\u308f\u308c\u307e\u3059\u304b\uff1f",
                    "en": "What do you think of curing cancer by using an herbal remedy?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u30d3\u30a8\u30c8\u3060\u3051\u304c\u3001\u30b3\u30a6\u30a4\u30c1\u304c\u602a\u6211\u3084\u75c5\u6c17\u3092\u6cbb\u3059\u3053\u3068\u304c\u3067\u304d\u308b\u4e0d\u601d\u8b70\u306a\u529b\u3092\u6301\u3063\u3066\u3044\u308b\u3053\u3068\u3092\u77e5\u3063\u3066\u3044\u308b\u3002",
                    "en": "Only Viet knows that Koichi has strange powers that can heal injuries and sicknesses."
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This word consists of kanji with hiragana attached. Because the hiragana ends with an {span:lang=ja:\u3046} sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means {tooltip:Kanji:dry} so the verb vocab version is {tooltip:Vocabulary:to dry}.",
