                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3044\u3058, \u3044\u3089, \u3055\u3044\u306a",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine yourself dressed as an ancient Egyptian pharaoh, wearing your newly-crafted flower lip ring. Think how classy (or strange) you feel.",
                "mnemonic": "After some {tooltip:Kanji:frustration}, you made your flower lip ring. Now you look like an {tooltip:Reading:Egy}ptian  ({span:lang=ja:\u3044\u3058}) pharaoh. Because all ancient Egyptian monarchs wore flower lip rings (historical fact)!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You {tooltip:Radical:say} stuff to a {tooltip:Radical:river}, giving it {tooltip:Kanji:instruction}. This river is a new river so it needs you to say things, giving it instruction on what to do. Otherwise the river wouldn\u2019t know how to behave.",
            "hints": "Imagine saying things to this river. What would you say? Flow harder! Babble better! Picture yourself shouting at the river as the water moves along.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Instruction"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304f\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3088",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture this raccoon captain and his raccoon crew coming down the river. The raccoons are all chittering about as they paddle the boat down the river.",
                "mnemonic": "While giving {tooltip:Kanji:instruction} to the river, you see a small boat coming down it. Inside the miniature boat is a rac{tooltip:Reading:coon} ({span:lang=ja:\u304f\u3093}) captain and his raccoon crew. The raccoon captain is instructing them, \u201cStroke! Stroke!\u201d"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "If a {tooltip:Radical:grain} seems extra {tooltip:Radical:heavy}, you know it's a different {tooltip:Kanji:kind}. Not all {tooltip:Kanji:type}s of grains are the same. So when you feel the heavy type, you know it isn't the same as the light kind.",
            "hints": "Imagine sorting all the different types of grains by their weight.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Kind",
                "Alternative": "Type"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3085",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f\u306d, \u3050\u3055",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture the two chutes you use to separate the different types of grains. One has a sign that says \"Light Chute\" and the other says \"Heavy Chute.\"",
                "mnemonic": "You sort all of the different {tooltip:Kanji:kind}s and {tooltip:Kanji:type}s by putting them in the right {tooltip:Reading:chu}tes ({span:lang=ja:\u3057\u3085}). They slide away, probably to some big type-based piles you can't see."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You want to share {tooltip:Radical:happiness} with people so you {tooltip:Radical:stamp} {tooltip:Radical:stool}s with the {tooltip:Kanji:news}. Now when people want a {tooltip:Kanji:report} of all the happy things going on, they can just look down before they sit.",
            "hints": "Stand up and look at the happy news today! It definitely isn't sad or depressing.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "News",
                "Alternative": "Report"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307b\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3080\u304f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Because your reporting is done from home, most of the news is about how your plants are growing. What the cat did this morning. And it certainly isn't the news without the weather!",
                "mnemonic": "You research and {tooltip:Kanji:report} the {tooltip:Kanji:news} from {tooltip:Reading:ho}me ({span:lang=ja:\u307b\u3046}). Only after, once all of the news is stamped onto the stools, do you leave your home."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:paragraph} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:paragraph} kanji are exactly the same!",
            "hints": "Know your radicals, yo!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Paragraph"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304f",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine actually getting an assignment to write a paragraph all about cookies. Would you write about your favorite cookies? Your eating methods?",
                "mnemonic": "You have to write an entire {tooltip:Kanji:paragraph} about a topic, so you choose {tooltip:Reading:coo}kies ({span:lang=ja:\u304f})."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Radical:Say} it at {tooltip:Radical:noon}. That's the only time I will {tooltip:Kanji:allow} you to say it. That's the only time I will {tooltip:Kanji:permit} you to say it. Other than that, no saying anything. Noon is your window.",
            "hints": "Imagine a world where you're not allowed to say anything except for at noon. At that moment, after you've been staring at the clock, waiting for that opportunity, you burst forth with things you want to say. But, once it hits 12:01, you are grabbed and your mouth is covered. You are not permitted to say anything outside of that time.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Permit",
                "Alternative": "Allow"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304d\u3087",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3086\u308b",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine going to big Kyoto where everything is fine. Then, go to little Kyoto where it's not. Little Kyoto has the short {span:lang=ja:\u304d\u3087} (instead of the long one), and this is how you can remember the difference.",
                "mnemonic": "Where do these archaic horrible rules exist? The smaller version of Kyoto, Little {tooltip:Reading:Kyo}to ({span:lang=ja:\u304d\u3087}). Normal Kyoto is just fine. This smaller one is where a group of people went to create their own little town with their own dumb rules."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "With a {tooltip:Radical:lip ring}, anything is {tooltip:Kanji:possible}. Having a lip ring grants you the power to conquer any obstacle, achieve any goal. As long as it's a {tooltip:Kanji:passable} lip ring, that is. If it isn't good enough, it isn't possible to be passable.",
            "hints": "Imagine getting a magical lip ring. First, you have to find a passable one. Then, you have to go through the pain of having your lip pierced. Is it even possible to imagine how painful that would be?!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Possible",
                "Alternative": "Passable"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304b",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "It was possible to get this passable car thanks to your lip ring. Hooray!",
                "mnemonic": "Since anything and everything is {tooltip:Kanji:possible} with your new {tooltip:Kanji:passable} lip ring, you use it to get a new {tooltip:Reading:ca}r ({span:lang=ja:\u304b}). You go to the car dealership and ask for a free (passable) car. The manager is impressed with your lip ring and gladly hands over the keys to the latest model of your favorite car. Not bad."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "If you see a {tooltip:Radical:spirit} with an {tooltip:Radical:axe}, you better {tooltip:Kanji:pray} for your life. Spirits only hold axes if they're thinking about murder, so pray as hard as you can to try and get it to spare you.",
            "hints": "Imagine trying to pray as hard as you can as a spirit with an axe closes in on you. Can you hold your prayer concentration long enough?",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Pray"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304d",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3044\u306e",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "I guess the key was prayer all along. ( \u0361\u00b0 \u035c\u0296 \u0361\u00b0)",
                "mnemonic": "You {tooltip:Kanji:pray} and pray and pray and nothing happens. You stop praying and dare to open your eyes. There is a {tooltip:Reading:key} ({span:lang=ja:\u304d}) in front of you. Grab the key and hold it before you and pray for the spirit to take it. Moments later, both the key and the spirit are gone."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A religious {tooltip:Radical:leader} wearing a {tooltip:Radical:mask} could be either a {tooltip:Kanji:monk} or a {tooltip:Kanji:priest}. You don\u2019t know, their face is covered by a mask!",
            "hints": "Picture this leader in their mask. Listen to them chant prayers that reverberate throughout the building.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Priest",
                "Alternative": "Monk"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305d\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine the monk/priest's bright, pure soul shining behind the mask. You'll never actually see it, unless you're becoming a monk/priest yourself, and then your soul will need protecting too.",
                "mnemonic": "This {tooltip:Kanji:monk} or {tooltip:Kanji:priest} is wearing a mask to protect their {tooltip:Reading:sou}l ({span:lang=ja:\u305d\u3046}). You aren't a monk or a priest, so they don't want your not-religious-enough eyes to dirty their soul."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Killing two {tooltip:Radical:tree}s with a {tooltip:Radical:jackhammer} is expressly {tooltip:Kanji:prohibit}ed. Don't get me wrong, you can rip up one tree with your jackhammer, but not two.",
            "hints": "Imagine standing in front of a forest with your jackhammer, staring at a sign with just this kanji on it. Tree plus tree with jackhammer equals prohibited. Hmm.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Prohibit",
                "Alternative": "Prohibition"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304d\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture the King angrily covering his ears as his subjects jackhammer trees. He screams and shouts, his crown bouncing on his head, and points at his new sign that prohibits the jackhammering of trees.",
                "mnemonic": "The person who decided to {tooltip:Kanji:prohibit} jackhammering two trees is the {tooltip:Reading:Kin}g ({span:lang=ja:\u304d\u3093}) of England. He's the only person who's able to prohibit certain things, so it had to be him."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Radical:Conflict} in {tooltip:Radical:blue} water is made {tooltip:Kanji:quiet} by the muffling power of water. Under this blue water is a great conflict between two sea monsters, but on the surface it appears peaceful and quiet as the water prevents sound from rising above the surface.",
            "hints": "Imagine watching this conflict from above the blue water on a boat. You can kind of see the action, but you can't hear a thing.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Quiet"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305b\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u305a",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Sabers are sharp, curved, and totally quiet. Great for underwater blue conflicts, and sneaky assassinations. ",
                "mnemonic": "As you watch below the {tooltip:Kanji:quiet} water, every once in a while a {tooltip:Reading:sa}ber ({span:lang=ja:\u305b\u3044}) breaches the surface and quietly slices back down."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There are two {tooltip:Radical:people} under a {tooltip:Radical:canopy} on the {tooltip:Radical:dirt}. What are they doing? It's a thing called {tooltip:Kanji:sit}ting. They're using the dirt as a {tooltip:Kanji:seat} for their butts.",
            "hints": "Did you know anyone can sit? It doesn't matter if there aren't chairs there.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Sit",
                "Alternative": "Seat"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3056",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3059\u308f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Wizards like to watch people sit. So they'll try lots of tricks to make people sit down near them. Don't let a wizard trick you. Don't ever sit down again.",
                "mnemonic": "You shake your head. People can't just {tooltip:Kanji:sit} on dirt, you think to yourself. There must be a wi{tooltip:Reading:za}rd ({span:lang=ja:\u3056}) around here trying to trick you. You certainly aren't going to be tricked into taking a seat by this wizard."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You have a {tooltip:Radical:fire} going on some {tooltip:Radical:dirt} when a {tooltip:Radical:helicopter} flies overhead. This causes {tooltip:Kanji:smoke} from the fire and the dirt to spread everywhere! It's in your eyes, your mouth, your ears! The smoke gets in your lungs and you can't breathe. Why helicopter, why!",
            "hints": "Imagine the smoke stopping up your lungs and getting in your eyes. All you know other than the smoke is the sound of the helicopter that's causing it to happen.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Smoke"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3048\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3051\u3080, \u3051\u3080\u308a",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine choking on the smoke and accepting that this is the end. Boohoo, if only you'd learned more kanji before the end!",
                "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Kanji:smoke} is too much and you realize this is the {tooltip:Reading:en}d ({span:lang=ja:\u3048\u3093}) for you. You're gonna die here!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The water from a {tooltip:Radical:tsunami} can create a lot of {tooltip:Radical:energy} when made into {tooltip:Kanji:steam}. Steam power used to be all the rage back in the day. Steam comes from water, water comes from tsunamis, so logically, tsunamis are a great source of energy.",
            "hints": "Imagine how much steam a tsunami could create. Hear the steam hissing off of the water and feel the moisture from the steam washing over your body.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Steam"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304d",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Steam is very light, so carrying this box of steam energy will be no problem. Picture yourself wandering about with a box of steam in one hand and a key in the other.",
                "mnemonic": "In order to contain this {tooltip:Kanji:steam} energy and store it for later, you lock it up in a box with a {tooltip:Reading:key} ({span:lang=ja:\u304d}). That way the energy can be used at a later time. Provided you don\u2019t lose the key, that is."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "On your {tooltip:Radical:mouth} is a {tooltip:Radical:stamp} that you {tooltip:Kanji:strike} and {tooltip:Kanji:beat} to create a beat! The stamp looks like a little drum, but you're really just hitting your mouth for fun (the stamp was an excuse to make it seem normal).",
            "hints": "Put a stamp on your mouth and look in your bathroom mirror. Now strike that stamp and make a fresh new beat the young kids can dance to!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Strike",
                "Alternative": "Beat"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3053\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f\u305f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "{span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061} doesn't wait for you to remove the stamp to answer, and instead starts striking your mouth to make his own beats. Rude! He should have asked for permission before beating on you, even if you would have said yes.",
                "mnemonic": "As you {tooltip:Kanji:strike} and {tooltip:Kanji:beat} your own mouth, {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061} comes dancing into your bathroom. \"Hey now, this looks fun, can I beat your mouth up a bit too?\" he asks."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Into your {tooltip:Radical:mouth} the {tooltip:Radical:leader} shot an {tooltip:Radical:arrow} with an attached {tooltip:Radical:hook} so that they'd be able to see into your {tooltip:Kanji:throat}.",
            "hints": "Imagine the arrow with its hook shooting down into your mouth and hooking into your throat. Ouch! Now the leader is peering down in there, but you don't know why.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Throat"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3053\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306e\u3069",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "The leader starts to tug {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061} out of your throat, but he resists. \"No, I like it down here, it's dark and damp.\" The leader gets angry and pulls harder, which hurts! This is your throat, after all, don't you get a say?!",
                "mnemonic": "From your {tooltip:Kanji:throat} you hear a voice. Not yours, but one you recognize. It's {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061}! What's that rascal doing down in your throat?"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:big} {tooltip:Radical:geoduck} loves to eat {tooltip:Radical:rice} of every {tooltip:Kanji:type}, {tooltip:Kanji:kind}, and {tooltip:Kanji:category}. It doesn\u2019t matter what kind of rice it is, this big geoduck will eat it.",
            "hints": "Picture this big geoduck shoveling rice down in a flurry of geoduckness. Hear the sloshing and goopy glugging as the geoduck gorges itself on every type of rice.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Type",
                "Alternatives": "Category, Kind"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u308b\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f\u3050",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture this big, fat geoduck, too big to move. It\u2019s now in a wheel chair and on a rice IV. Imagine its wheelchair squeaking by as it wheezes and groans.",
                "mnemonic": "While eating every {tooltip:Kanji:type} and {tooltip:Kanji:kind} of rice from every {tooltip:Kanji:category}, the big geoduck has become even bigger. Its body is {tooltip:Reading:rui}ned ({span:lang=ja:\u308b\u3044}). It\u2019s painfully obese. This geoduck has eaten itself into ruin."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:previous} {tooltip:Radical:tsunami} gave everything in this town a good {tooltip:Kanji:wash}. There are many Laundromats in this town so when the previous tsunami hit, all of the detergent mixed with the water and gave everything a good wash.",
            "hints": "Imagine this soapy tsunami, washing all the things in town. Hear the soapy waves bubbling with cleanliness as they crash down around you.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Wash"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305b\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3042\u3089",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine these centaurs frolicking around in the aftermath of the previous tsunami. It\u2019s just like a big bathtub for them. Hear them splashing and neighing in the water.",
                "mnemonic": "All the Laundromats in town are owned by {tooltip:Reading:cen}taurs ({span:lang=ja:\u305b\u3093}), and they got a good {tooltip:Kanji:wash} as well. Being half horse, centaurs have a lot of hair that needs washing. Luckily, the soap tsunami gave them a good wash too."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You want your {tooltip:Radical:spirit} to be {tooltip:Radical:simple} so you practice {tooltip:Kanji:Zen Buddhism}. Zen is all about meditating and letting earthly things go. There's nothing more simple for your spirit than being {tooltip:Kanji:Zen}.",
            "hints": "Try out Zen meditation for a little bit. Look away from your computer or phone or tablet and let your life be simple for a second. Just let it all go and free your spirit from all the noise.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Zen",
                "Alternative": "Zen Buddhism"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305c\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Without a new reading to learn, you should have time to close your eyes and clear your mind. Zen it out, friend.",
                "mnemonic": "The reading for this kanji is {tooltip:Reading:Zen} ({span:lang=ja:\u305c\u3093}), just like the {tooltip:Kanji:Zen} in {tooltip:Kanji:Zen Buddhism}. Pretty easy to remember, no? Just remember that the reading is the same as the meaning. It\u2019s easy!"
            }
        }

{
