        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The kanji and the word are exactly the same. That means they share meanings as well.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Throat",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u306e\u3069"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u306e\u3069",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kenichi",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "40626-subject-8849.ogg"
                    },
                    {
                        "gender": "female",
                        "pronunciation": "\u306e\u3069",
                        "voiceActorId": 1,
                        "voiceActorName": "Kyoko",
                        "voiceDescription": "Tokyo accent",
                        "file": "40800-subject-8849.ogg"
                    }
                ],
                "mnemonic": "\n{splitParent}Since this word is made up of a single kanji (and is a body part) it uses the kun'yomi reading. You did not learn this reading, so here's a mnemonic to help you:\n{splitParent}Remember the mnemonic from when you first learned this kanji? Well {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061} is still in your {tooltip:Kanji:throat} and he doesn't want to get out. In fact, every time you tug on him, he shouts, \"{tooltip:Reading:No Do}- ({span:lang=ja:\u306e\u3069})\" but you gag and can't hear the rest. People around you think you're saying \"No Do-\" but it's really just the weirdo living in your throat now. What a pain...\n"
            },
            "contextSentences": [
                {
                    "ja": "\u732b\u306f\u3001\u5589\u3067\u30b4\u30ed\u30b4\u30ed\u3068\u3044\u3046\u97f3\u3092\u51fa\u3057\u307e\u3059\u3002",
                    "en": "Cats purr with their throats."
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u6700\u8fd1\u3001\u5589\u304c\u3084\u305b\u305f\u3093\u3058\u3083\u306a\u3044\uff1f",
                    "en": "Your throat's gotten slimmer lately, hasn't it?"
                },
                {
                    "ja": "\u5589\u304c\u75db\u3044\u306e\u3067\u3001\u79c1\u306e\u4ee3\u308f\u308a\u306b\u6b4c\u3063\u3066\u304f\u308c\u307e\u305b\u3093\u304b\uff1f",
                    "en": "I have a sore throat. Can you sing on my behalf?"
                }
            ],
            "collocations": []
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This is a day of the week. You know this because it has {span:lang=ja:\u66dc\u65e5} in it. But what day of the week is it? The {tooltip:Kanji:tree} day of the week is {tooltip:Kanji:Thursday}. Tree day comes after water day (which is Wednesday, for Water-Wednesday!). Trees need water to grow, which is why the tree comes right after the water.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Thursday",
                "Word Type": "noun"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "primary": true,
                        "reading": "\u3082\u304f\u3088\u3046\u3073"
                    }
                ],
                "pronunciationAudios": [
                    {
                        "gender": "male",
                        "pronunciation": "\u3082\u304f\u3088\u3046\u3073",
                        "voiceActorId": 2,
