            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Standard"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304d",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture this woman actually sticking a physical key into the husband\u2019s chest and turning it. Now this is anything but standard, but it\u2019ll help you remember this kanji and its reading.",
                "mnemonic": "It\u2019s pretty {tooltip:Kanji:standard} for a husband and wife to exchange vows when getting married, and the husband said this girl held the {tooltip:Reading:key} ({span:lang=ja:\u304d}) to his heart. She unlocked the husband\u2019s heart with the key and now he\u2019s a man made whole."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You have a {tooltip:Radical:bow} and you want to make it {tooltip:Radical:long}. To do that, you'd naturally just {tooltip:Kanji:stretch} it.",
            "hints": "Imagine holding this bow in your hands, and then stretching it into a longer bow. Feel the strain on your arms as you try to stretch wood. You can feel it stretching, but it's strenuous.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Stretch"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3061\u3087\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Now Mrs. Chou is hitting you with the stretched out bow. You cry and apologize, but she won't listen. \"I'm going to stretch you, like you stretched my bow,\" she says. Then she does it.",
                "mnemonic": "As you {tooltip:Kanji:stretch} the bow, you hear someone running at you. \"That's my bow you're stretching!\" yells Mrs. {tooltip:Reading:Chou} ({span:lang=ja:\u3061\u3087\u3046}). She is not happy that you're stretching her things. You should probably try to un-stretch it, put it down, and run before she beats you."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:leader} of a {tooltip:Radical:hundred} {tooltip:Radical:forehead}s in the {tooltip:Radical:heart} of {tooltip:Radical:winter} is {tooltip:Kanji:superior} at being {tooltip:Kanji:gentle}. These hundred foreheads are sensitive, especially in the heart of winter, so the leader must be very gentle. Not just any kind of gentle though\u2014the superior kind of gentle that will keep a hundred foreheads following.",
            "hints": "As a leader, you are superior. You\u2019re so gentle to these foreheads. See yourself caring for them in the winter. Feel the chill in the air as you wrap the foreheads in blankets and prepare heating pads for them.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Superior",
                "Alternative": "Gentle"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3086\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3084\u3055, \u3059\u3050",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture the youths trying to show you that they are the superior ones, then they sneeze, their eyes fill with tears, and you have to gently tuck them back into bed. Silly youths.",
                "mnemonic": "You need to be {tooltip:Kanji:superior} because all of these foreheads belong to {tooltip:Reading:you}ths ({span:lang=ja:\u3086\u3046}). They don't know how to take care of themselves, though they try to convince you they can. They're too young to know any better."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:tsunami} of {tooltip:Radical:shrimp} crashes into a {tooltip:Kanji:swamp}. Now the swamp is filled with shrimp. They used to live in the ocean, but the tsunami has carried them to their new home in the swamp.",
            "hints": "Imagine yourself in the swamp when the tsunami hits. Feel the water crash into you along with the shrimp. You\u2019re all wet and shrimpy now.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Swamp"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f\u304f",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3055\u308f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Look at the side of your house. It\u2019s covered with shrimp that have been impaled on the tacks. Perfect for dinner.",
                "mnemonic": "Your house was right next to the {tooltip:Kanji:swamp} and on this side of your house you had many a {tooltip:Reading:tack} ({span:lang=ja:\u305f\u304f}) there to hang things on. Now, on every tack there is skewered a shrimp. The force of the tsunami drove them into the tacks on the side of your house."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:drop} of water falls from a {tooltip:Radical:bear} onto the {tooltip:Radical:ground} and needs to be wiped up with a {tooltip:Radical:towel}. Only a {tooltip:Kanji:teacher} or an {tooltip:Kanji:expert} could get away with getting close to this wet bear to wipe up after it.",
            "hints": "The expert is also your teacher, and she's showing you how to clean up after this bear.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Teacher",
                "Alternatives": "Expert, Master"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Your teacher assures you this wet sheep is far less dangerous to clean up after than a wet bear. They're the expert, so you should listen and wipe up after that sheep.",
                "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Kanji:teacher} tells you to start with something smaller, after all, you're not an {tooltip:Kanji:expert} yet. You have to practice on a wet {tooltip:Reading:shee}p ({span:lang=ja:\u3057}). \"I became a wet, dangerous animal expert by starting with sheep, you will too!\" your teacher says."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "In the {tooltip:Radical:morning}, if you want your {tooltip:Radical:hat} to {tooltip:Radical:dry}, you need to hang it on a {tooltip:Kanji:tree trunk}. In the morning, everything is covered in dew. The grass is wet, the leaves are wet, but the tree trunk isn't wet! It was protected by the rest of the tree, so you can hang your hat there to dry.",
            "hints": "Picture everything early in the morning covered in damp dew. Even your hat is uncomfortably wet. Now walk up to a large tree trunk and touch it\u2014it's dry!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Tree Trunk"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304b\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307f\u304d",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Now that you think about it, Genghis Khan was the one who taught people that tree trunks stay dry in the morning!",
                "mnemonic": "You lean against the {tooltip:Kanji:tree trunk}, waiting for your hat to dry, and suddenly you realize Genghis {tooltip:Reading:Khan} ({span:lang=ja:\u304b\u3093}) is on the other side of it. You look around and see that all of the tree trunks around you have Mongols drying their hats under them."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Put your {tooltip:Radical:hand} over your {tooltip:Radical:eye} to {tooltip:Kanji:watch over} others.",
            "hints": "You know that way cartoon characters and pirates watch out below them, with their hand shading their eye? That! Now imagine doing that right now.",
