                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine getting your fortune told by a centaur fortune teller. Do you trust this mythical beast to know your future? Watch him manipulate his crystal ball as his hooves stomp the ground beneath him.",
                "mnemonic": "To {tooltip:Kanji:occupy} some free time you have, you go and get your {tooltip:Kanji:fortune} told at a fortune teller. Your fortune is that you\u2019ll have an encounter with a {tooltip:Reading:cen}taur ({span:lang=ja:\u305b\u3093}) in the near future. Just then you realize that the fortune teller is actually a centaur! The fortune has come true!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's a {tooltip:Radical:building} standing in the way of the next {tooltip:Radical:chapter} in your life, and it's {tooltip:Kanji:hinder}ing you. It's not like you couldn't find another way around, but it's definitely inconvenient.",
            "hints": "Picture this huge, tall building standing directly in the way. You peer around the other side and there is a book floating in the air that says \"Chapter 5: Success and Happiness.\" Dang, you really want to get over there to that part of your life.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Hinder"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3087\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3055\u308f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "The Shougun wants to hinder you and your life. He wants you to have to work harder so you become better, faster, and stronger than anyone else. Hindering you is his way of making sure you don't take the easy way out in life.",
                "mnemonic": "Someone put this building here specifically to {tooltip:Kanji:hinder} you. They knew you were close to the next chapter of your life, and they wanted to hinder you really good. And there they are, sitting on a throne smirking. It's the {tooltip:Reading:Shou}gun ({span:lang=ja:\u3057\u3087\u3046})."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Your {tooltip:Radical:ego}... it's totally out of control. {tooltip:Kanji:I} mean, my ego isn't out of control. I'm the greatest. That's {tooltip:Kanji:me}. But your ego? Ugh, so out of control unlike my amazing ego.",
            "hints": "Just keep going with this sentence about the ego. You can't stop, because really it's your ego that's out of control. That's {tooltip:Kanji:I} when you're saying it.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "I",
                "Alternative": "Me"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304c",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u308f\u308c, \u308f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "You're just destroying the ego of that other guy. \"Why?\" you ask? \"Because {tooltip:Kanji:I} am the boss here. And you are the baby. Ego things.\"",
                "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Kanji:I} am the greatest in the world! I am no baby like you, that's why. A baby who says \"{tooltip:Reading:ga} ({span:lang=ja:\u304c}) ga ga ga!\""
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:loiter}ing {tooltip:Radical:mountain} {tooltip:Radical:king} in the {tooltip:Radical:winter} is an {tooltip:Kanji:indication} or {tooltip:Kanji:sign} of something to come.",
            "hints": "We don't want to focus too much on what the indication / sign actually is, though, because it will take away from the {tooltip:Kanji:indication} part (which we want to remember). So, focus on the fact that this is some kind of indication that's important, but you don't know what that indication means or is... because we'll use that with the reading.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Indication",
                "Alternative": "Sign"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3061\u3087\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine an announcer watching the loitering mountain king, \"There he is... and it's still winter. We all know what that means. It's a sign that Mrs. Chou will be coming out of hibernation soon.\" Then, you can imagine her bursting forth from the mountain, running down her street.",
                "mnemonic": "When you spot a loitering mountain king in the winter, you know it's an {tooltip:Kanji:indication} that soon Mrs. {tooltip:Reading:Chou} ({span:lang=ja:\u3061\u3087\u3046}) will appear. It's like a Groundhog's Day kind of situation, but instead it means Mrs. Chou is about to come out, perhaps early for the season."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There are {tooltip:Radical:fingers} reaching towards you asking you to {tooltip:Radical:accept} to {tooltip:Kanji:instruct} them. They're the fingers of students. And you're the teacher. See them reach out for knowledge. They want to accept this knowledge into their hearts and brains. Instruct them!",
            "hints": "Picture yourself instructing a class full of knowledge hungry little kids. They reach out with their fingers to accept the truth you bring to them.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Instruct"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3058\u3085",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3055\u305a",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture yourself instructing little kids how to punch the straw into small juice boxes. Hold it right-side-up! Don't squeeze too hard! Don't punch it through the other side! No! Don't bite the box! Instructing is hard!",
                "mnemonic": "\"Yes! I will {tooltip:Kanji:instruct} you, my dear students!\" you cry, \"I will instruct you on how to open a small {tooltip:Reading:ju}ice ({span:lang=ja:\u3058\u3085}) box!\" That's right. These little kids need help learning how to open small juice boxes (small because kids are small)."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You hold {tooltip:Radical:gold} {tooltip:Radical:fins} in your {tooltip:Radical:mouth} while shopping for pencil {tooltip:Kanji:lead} at the store. This pencil lead store only accepts gold fins as payment, but your fins don\u2019t fit in your pockets and you need your hands to carry the lead so the gold fins go in your mouth.",
