{
            "mnemonic": "Because the hiragana ends with an {span:lang=ja:\u3046} sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means {tooltip:Kanji:visit} and the verb vocab version is also {tooltip:Vocabulary:to visit}. What makes it different from {span:lang=ja:\u8a2a\u306d\u308b}, though? With {span:lang=ja:\u8a2a\u308c\u308b}, you tend to be doing something for fun but with a purpose. So, if you were visiting Tokyo for sightseeing, you could say {span:lang=ja:\u8a2a\u308c\u308b}. If you were going to visit Tokyo for no real reason, you could say {span:lang=ja:\u8a2a\u306d\u308b}. That being said, they are almost exactly the same, just the nuance is different.",
