{
            "mnemonic": "Because the hiragana ends with an {span:lang=ja:\u3046} sound, you know this word is a verb. The kanji itself means {tooltip:Kanji:detach}, but what happens when something detaches? It causes it {tooltip:Vocabulary:to separate} and {tooltip:Vocabulary:to leave}. This refers to when something separates on its own. You aren't doing the separating. You know this because it's {tooltip:Reading:rare} ({span:lang=ja:\u308c\u308b}) to see things separate, but sometimes you do see them. Also, whenever you see {span:lang=ja:\u308c\u308b} usually it's a safe bet that you're not the one doing the action, right? Think about it.",
            "hints": "",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "To Separate",
                "Alternative": "To Leave",
                "Word Type": "intransitive verb, ichidan verb"
            },
            "vocabReadings": {
                "readings": [
