                "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Kanji:deed} he's currently working on is finding your lost {tooltip:Reading:key} ({span:lang=ja:\u304d}). He doesn't have to help, you're the one who lost your own house-combination-car key. But he's so used to doing deeds, he can't help but help."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:canopy} and a {tooltip:Radical:helicopter} are being {tooltip:Radical:compare}d by a {tooltip:Kanji:deer}. The deer is standing very still (as deer do) and looking very slowly between the empty canopy and the helicopter sitting next to it.",
            "hints": "Picture the deer comparing the two structures. It probably has no idea what they are, but it's trying very hard to understand what it's seeing anyway.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Deer"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u308d\u304f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304b, \u3057\u304b",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Deer get hit by cars all the time, but it was still really shocking . I mean it was right in front of you. The car could have hit you too!",
                "mnemonic": "You walk forward, about to call out to the {tooltip:Kanji:deer}, when a {tooltip:Reading:ca}r ({span:lang=ja:\u304b}) suddenly appears out of no where and runs the deer over! Was this all just a trap set up to murder the deer?!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "In {tooltip:Radical:prison}, when you have access to a {tooltip:Radical:big} number of {tooltip:Radical:fingers}, {tooltip:Radical:mouth}s, and {tooltip:Radical:legs} you can {tooltip:Kanji:exchange} them for things that are worth more to you. What are you gonna do with all these body parts in prison? You should exchange them for cigarettes and toilet alcohol.",
            "hints": "You just have so many fingers, mouths, and legs you've pummeled off of the other prisoners. Exchange them for better loot!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Exchange"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304b\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304b",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Think back to when you were a kid and they would roll out those TVs on stands into the classrooms. Imagine watching a documentary on Genghis Khan's exchange routes where he bought and sold body parts. Educational!",
                "mnemonic": "You learned this type of {tooltip:Kanji:exchange} from the mighty Genghis {tooltip:Reading:Khan} ({span:lang=ja:\u304b\u3093}). You read a book or watched a show about him once and you're pretty positive that Genghis Khan would exchange the body parts of his enemies all over the continents of Asia and Europe for nicer stuff."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:fang} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:fang} kanji are exactly the same! Fantastic. Oh, also it means {tooltip:Kanji:tusk}. It means fang and tusk.",
            "hints": "Since the fang radical is the same as the fang kanji, that should be easy to remember. Tusks aren\u2019t too different from fangs, so that shouldn\u2019t be too hard to remember either.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Fang",
                "Alternative": "Tusk"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3052, \u304c",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304d\u3070",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture a gecko with cute little fangs. You can keep thinking they're cute as long as one never bites you. They may be little, but gecko fangs are no joke!",
                "mnemonic": "Lots of animals have {tooltip:Kanji:fang}s, even ones that don't look like they do. The coolest fang-having animal is the {tooltip:Reading:ge}cko ({span:lang=ja:\u3052})! They have pretty sharp little fangs in their mouths and they'll bite you if you keep tapping their glass cages like that. Cut it out."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There\u2019s a {tooltip:Radical:stick} next to the {tooltip:Radical:sun}, reflecting on its {tooltip:Kanji:former} glory. The stick used to be a great tree, and the stick thinks that if it stays by the sun long enough it might return to its former form as a towering tree.",
            "hints": "Pretend you are the stick. Really focus on your former self as you feel the sun warm you with its rays. Imagine yourself growing, larger and larger.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Former"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
