                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Most people who do forge work have tans. And now you know why.",
                "mnemonic": "You're trying to work the {tooltip:Kanji:forge}, but it's harder than you thought. You end up forging outside all day and get a wicked {tooltip:Reading:tan} ({span:lang=ja:\u305f\u3093})."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:crab} moves a {tooltip:Radical:stone} because he is playing the game {tooltip:Kanji:Go}. You know this game, right? You put black or white stones on a board until you win the game. This is the Japanese game known as Go. This crab is pretty good.",
            "hints": "Look at this crab placing stones on the Go board. Hear them click and clack as the crab places them with expert precision.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Go"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3054",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Go is Go. Easy!",
                "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Kanji:Go} is pronounced {tooltip:Reading:Go} ({span:lang=ja:\u3054}) in Japanese, too. How convenient is that? Like, super convenient. So easy even a crab could remember!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "When you get {tooltip:Radical:sick} you always eat a {tooltip:Radical:hot pepper}. It's a {tooltip:Kanji:habit} you've picked up and you can't seem to shake it. One sniffle, one cough, and before you know it, you're eating a hot pepper or two.",
            "hints": "Imagine if you had a habit of eating hot peppers whenever you thought you might be getting sick. To be fair, it flushes out your sinuses and tastes good, so it's not the worst habit you could have.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Habit"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3078\u304d",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304f\u305b",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Unfortunately, now you've gotten into the habit of telling everyone what to do when they get sick. \"Yeah, yeah, eat a hot pepper, we know. But couscous is better,\" they say before you can speak. To each their own, I suppose.",
                "mnemonic": "Everyone you know has a different {tooltip:Kanji:habit} when they get sick. Instead, they eat {tooltip:Reading:cous}cous ({span:lang=ja:\u304f\u305b}). But you think couscous is gross. It's bland and does nothing for your stuffy noses and scratchy throats. I wonder how you can convince them to replace their habit with your own."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The part of the {tooltip:Radical:grain} people {tooltip:Radical:favor} the most is the {tooltip:Kanji:head of the plant}. You don't eat the stems and leaves of rice or wheat plants, right? You eat the head where the grains are.",
            "hints": "Imagine yourself walking through a field of rice plants. Now look very closely at the head of the plant in front of you. See the rice bunched up there? That's the good stuff.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Head of Plant",
                "Alternative": "Ear Of Plant"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3059\u3044",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307b",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine working in a field of rice and using a hoe so you don't injure the head of the plant. You're sweating and it's hot, but you keep at it.",
                "mnemonic": "When you want to harvest the {tooltip:Kanji:head of the plant} you'd like to eat, you need to use a {tooltip:Reading:hoe} ({span:lang=ja:\u307b}). Using a hoe, you can dig up the bottom of the plant without injuring the head."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "I have {tooltip:Radical:five} things in my {tooltip:Radical:mouth} and {tooltip:Kanji:I} need them all for {tooltip:Kanji:me}. I own these five things, and I keep them in my mouth so people like you cannot take them from me.",
            "hints": "What five things would you keep in your mouth to protect them from me? Imagine these five things in your mouth and think of how important they are to you as you taste them on your tongue.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "I",
                "Alternative": "Me"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3054",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u308f\u304c",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine how you would sound asking for \u201cwaga\u201d with your mouth full of five things. You can carry out this experiment in real life if you would like, just be careful not to choke, okay?",
                "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Kanji:Me}, personally, {tooltip:Kanji:I} am quite thirsty so I ask for some \u201c{tooltip:Reading:waga}\u201d ({span:lang=ja:\u308f\u304c}). I have five things in my mouth, so it is hard for me to pronounce the word \u201cwater\u201d. So it sounds like \u201cwaga\u201d when I ask for it. \u201cCoulg I pweash hab sum waga?! I deed waga!!\u201d"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You take some {tooltip:Radical:gold} and cover your {tooltip:Radical:yoga} {tooltip:Radical:brush} in it, turning it into a {tooltip:Kanji:key}. You shove the brush you normally use for yoga into the lock to your car and pour the gold over it. Now it's the right shape to be a key!",
            "hints": "You lost your old key, you see. But you're a go with the flow type of person, thanks to all the yoga, so you make a new one!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Key"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3051\u3093",
