                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "\"Weeee!\" says the commander.",
                "mnemonic": "You watch the {tooltip:Kanji:commander} in all his glory... going back and forth on a {tooltip:Reading:swi}ng ({span:lang=ja:\u3059\u3044})."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "If you {tooltip:Radical:say} something the same way they say it in the {tooltip:Radical:capital}, everyone in the whole country will be able to {tooltip:Kanji:comprehend} what you're trying to say. If you try to speak in your hometown's dialect no one will. But everyone can comprehend what you want if you use the capital's dialect to say it.",
            "hints": "Imagine the confused faces of the people in the countryside as you try to talk to them. Decide to say it the way it's said in the capital and see their faces light up as they comprehend what you're trying to say.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Comprehend",
                "Alternative": "Reality"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u308a\u3087\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3042\u304d\u3089, \u307e\u3053\u3068\u306b",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture yourself rowing away in your new row boat as the townspeople wave goodbye. They were much nicer once they comprehended what you were asking for. Maybe you'll row your way back here someday.",
                "mnemonic": "Now that the townspeople finally {tooltip:Kanji:comprehend} what you were trying to say, they sell you the {tooltip:Reading:row} ({span:lang=ja:\u308a\u3087\u3046}) boat you were trying to buy. You were using the wrong word, but {span:lang=ja:\u308a\u3087\u3046} is what they needed to hear to understand you, and now you can row your way back home."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Radical:Flowers} are growing out of the {tooltip:Radical:ground} and over your pet {tooltip:Radical:pig}'s {tooltip:Radical:forehead} and it's causing the poor thing to live in total {tooltip:Kanji:darkness}. The flowers are hanging down over its eyes and won't let any light in at all. And some of the flowers seem to be growing directly out of the pig's forehead too. The pig has just been wandering and bumping into things all day because of it.",
            "hints": "Imagine being a pig with flowers hanging down off your forehead and into your eyes so that darkness was all you knew. It's very sad, actually.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Darkness"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3082\u3046, \u307c\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304a\u304a, \u304f\u3089, \u3053\u3046\u3080",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine mowing the darkness away. Be careful! If you mow the pig's eyes, the darkness will last forever!",
                "mnemonic": "To end the {tooltip:Kanji:darkness} you {tooltip:Reading:mow} ({span:lang=ja:\u3082\u3046}) the flowers. You have to drive your lawnmower over its head too, because they're growing out of its forehead too. They'll grow back soon though and cover your pig's world in darkness once again and you'll have to do it all over again."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:sun} is rising and there is a {tooltip:Radical:net} covering {tooltip:Radical:someone}, who's being forced to watch as {tooltip:Kanji:dawn} appears. They've never seen dawn before, and they didn't want to, but you're making them.",
            "hints": "Picture someone tied down in a net as dawn breaks.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Dawn"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3087",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3042\u3051\u307c\u306e",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Look at Akebono looking at you as you look at dawn breaking.",
                "mnemonic": "The someone who saw {tooltip:Kanji:dawn} for the first time goes on to become sumo wrestling champ {tooltip:Reading:Akebono} Taro ({span:lang=ja:\u3042\u3051\u307c\u306e}). He always gazes at the sunrise at dawn before he wrestles and it fills him with strength."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "If you get a {tooltip:Radical:big} {tooltip:Radical:hair} stuck in your {tooltip:Radical:private} {tooltip:Radical:soul} it will be a {tooltip:Kanji:disaster}! Have you ever gotten a hair in your eye or your mouth that you just couldn't get out? You cough, your eyes water, it's awful! You can't focus on anything until it's gone. Now imagine if a big 'ol hair got stuck in your own private soul!!! Disaster!!!",
            "hints": "This disaster is ruining your life. You can't eat, sleep, all you can do is panic trying to get the hair out.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Disaster"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3055\u3093, \u3056\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307f\u3058, \u3044\u305f, \u3080\u3054",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Santa-san has forsaken you. Your life is a wreck. This is a total disaster just because of one big hair.",
                "mnemonic": "This {tooltip:Kanji:disaster} is so huge that even {tooltip:Reading:San}ta-san ({span:lang=ja:\u3055\u3093}) left his vacation in {tooltip:Reading:Zan}zibar ({span:lang=ja:\u3056\u3093}) to come tell you you're on the naughty list for being such a disaster person. WHAT?! Even you Santa-san?"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "When what you're writing is as {tooltip:Radical:tall} as the {tooltip:Radical:grain}s growing outside, you know you're done with your first {tooltip:Kanji:draft}. Some teachers want papers to be a certain number of words or pages, but that's silly. You should measure your draft in grains!",
            "hints": "Imagine holding your draft up outside in a field of grains. You're a couple leaves short. Time to get back to the drawing board.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Draft"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3053\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u305f\u304c\u304d, \u308f\u3089",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Now {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061} is out there measuring your draft against the tall grain plants. You better hope it's long enough, because if it isn't, Emperor {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061}'s probably going to execute you. ",
                "mnemonic": "Who asked you to write such a tall {tooltip:Kanji:draft} in the first place? Emperor {span:lang=ja:\u3053\u3046\u3044\u3061}, who wants you to draft up the world's largest book about how great of an emperor he is."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "If you can find the {tooltip:Radical:door} in {tooltip:Radical:winter} shaped like a {tooltip:Radical:mouth} it will {tooltip:Kanji:enlighten} you.",
            "hints": "Imagine searching for this special door in the cold wind and snow of winter. The search for enlightenment always has something to do with suffering and searching on your own like this.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Enlighten"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3051\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3055\u3068, \u3072\u3089",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "All you need to do to attain enlightenment is to eat this cake... out of this mouth. Eh. Maybe enlightenment isn't worth it.",
                "mnemonic": "The door {tooltip:Kanji:enlight}ened you by opening up and revealing the {tooltip:Reading:ca}ke ({span:lang=ja:\u3051\u3044}) in its door-mouth. \"Cake is the key to enlightenment, my child. Now eat it from my mouth.\""
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Press your {tooltip:Radical:fingers} in someone's {tooltip:Radical:skin} to {tooltip:Kanji:expose} what's underneath. This is pretty gross, but sometimes it's more important that you expose the cause of the problem, than avoiding something uncomfortable.",
            "hints": "Close your eyes. Picture someone's arm. Now touch their skin with your fingers and peel it back to expose the muscles and bones underneath.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Expose"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3072",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine the heat steaming out, like it's coming out of a teapot, not out of somebody's arm skin.",
                "mnemonic": "You {tooltip:Kanji:expose} a small hole under their skin and {tooltip:Reading:he}at ({span:lang=ja:\u3072}) comes pouring out. Aha! You knew there was something wrong under here. Apparently there's something under this person's skin that's exuding way too much heat."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:drunkard} {tooltip:Radical:cross}es {tooltip:Radical:thread} across special {tooltip:Radical:spikes} to separate the {tooltip:Kanji:fiber}s. The best way to separate the fibers of thread is to get a drunkard to do it. If you're too precise it will just snap. You gotta be loose with it! Just flop them onto the spikes and the fibers will come apart by themselves!",
            "hints": "Imagine a drunkard crossing threads onto spikes and pulling the tiny fibers free. You don't think you could do that. Maybe if you got drunk too...",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Fiber",
                "Alternative": "Slender"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305b\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Watch the centaur cross the fibers and separate them with his spikes. How strange.",
                "mnemonic": "You stare at the {tooltip:Kanji:fiber}s easily coming apart when you realize that this drunkard seems to have a few extra legs... and hair and a tail? This isn't just a drunk guy, it's a {tooltip:Reading:cen}taur ({span:lang=ja:\u305b\u3093}). Maybe separating fibers is really a centaur trick, and that's why normal people can't do it..."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "If you {tooltip:Radical:loiter} around a {tooltip:Radical:surplus} of anything, you need to be {tooltip:Kanji:gentle} and touch everything {tooltip:Kanji:gently}. ",
            "hints": "Unfortunately it's not specific as to what kind of surplus you're loitering around, so you'll need to imagine a surplus of something memorable to you. Then, just imagine loitering around there, being really gentle with everything.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Gently",
                "Alternative": "Gentle"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3058\u3087",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304a\u3082\u3080",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine Jo-Anne coming back to her surplus, then asking you if you were gentle with the goods. You were, you treated everything very gently.",
                "mnemonic": "You have to be {tooltip:Kanji:gentle} and handle these things {tooltip:Kanji:gently} because they belong to {tooltip:Reading:Jo}-Anne ({span:lang=ja:\u3058\u3087}). You're loitering around her surplus of (whatever you decided to imagine)."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Radical:Flowers} that are thought to be little {tooltip:Radical:tent}s sent from {tooltip:Radical:heaven} are {tooltip:Kanji:hollyhock}s. They're shaped almost like little teepees and would make the perfect tent for a faerie or a little yokai! ",
            "hints": "Picture a tiny yokai using a hollyhock as a tent. Aww cute!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Hollyhock"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304d",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3042\u304a\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine yourself in a field of hollyhocks and the {span:lang=ja:\u3042\u304a\u3044} of them match the {span:lang=ja:\u3042\u304a\u3044} of the heavens! It's like you're standing on a giant flower filled mirror of the sky.",
                "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Kanji:Hollyhock}s are mostly {span:lang=ja:\u3042\u304a\u3044} (blue) because they copy the color of the heavens, where they came from."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "While the {tooltip:Radical:moon} is out, a {tooltip:Radical:gladiator} is riding a {tooltip:Radical:horse}, yelling about {tooltip:Kanji:inflation}. No one would listen to him during the day, so he waited for the moon to rise so he could complain about prices doing the same.",
            "hints": "Picture the gladiator riding his horse in the light of the moon yelling, \"Do you have any idea how important it is to increase the average pay rate to account for inflation? All of us gladiators are going to be unable to pay the bills soon because inflation isn't being taken into account! You'll suffer too! Listen to me!\"",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Inflation"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3068\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3042\u304c, \u306e\u307c",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine if you were in this gladiator's place. In five years, you wouldn't be able to afford to eat thanks to the bosses in {span:lang=ja:\u3068\u3046\u304d\u3087\u3046} refusing to adjust for inflation. Back in my day, onigiri only cost FIFteen yen!",
                "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Kanji:Inflation} rises the most in {span:lang=ja:\u3068\u3046\u304d\u3087\u3046}, and the gladiator knows that because that's where most of them live and fight (and die)."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You use your {tooltip:Radical:fingers} to take down the {tooltip:Radical:old} {tooltip:Radical:flag} and {tooltip:Kanji:install} a new one. Flags get old, just like computers and furniture and people. So when one gets too old, you have to get on up there and install a new one in its place.",
            "hints": "Imagine reaching out and grabbing onto the old flag with your fingers. Feel the dirty grime and little holes it's gotten. Now install something new!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Install"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304d\u3087",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3059",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture a newly installed suit flying in the air on a pole.",
                "mnemonic": "You've decided to {tooltip:Kanji:install} a {tooltip:Reading:su}it ({span:lang=ja:\u3059}) where the old flag used to be. I mean, a suit is probably more durable than a flag, am I right? This way you won't have to install flags as often."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You cut the {tooltip:Radical:flowers} off the {tooltip:Radical:grain} with your {tooltip:Radical:knife}. The flowers that fall off are {tooltip:Kanji:jasmine} flowers.",
            "hints": "It's weird that jasmine flowers are growing out of your grain, but that's why you're cutting them off.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Jasmine"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u308a, \u308c\u3044, \u3089\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "You have a farm that grows grain and reeds. Jasmine flowers are growing out of both of those things, ruining your crops! Perhaps you should become a jasmine tea farmer instead...",
                "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Kanji:jasmine} is growing out of everything, not just grain! It's also growing out of the {tooltip:Reading:ree}ds ({span:lang=ja:\u308a})!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:thread} that goes through {tooltip:Radical:Korea} is the {tooltip:Kanji:latitude} line splitting up North and South Korea.",
            "hints": "Why thread? Back in the old days globes were sewn together with cloth and thread, so there was a literal thread dividing the Koreas!{splitParent}Or you can just picture an actual, super long piece of thread going around the entire globe. That works too.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Latitude"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306c\u304d, \u3088\u3053\u3044\u3068",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine being an eagle yourself, flying around the entire globe, following the latitude line that splits Korea down the middle.",
                "mnemonic": "The only way you can see this {tooltip:Kanji:latitude} line is to ride an {tooltip:Reading:ea}gle ({span:lang=ja:\u3044}) over it. The line isn't visible from a car, you're too close, and a plane is too high. You have to see it from an eagle's point of view."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:melon} radical and the {tooltip:Kanji:melon} kanji are exactly the same! Wahoo!",
            "hints": "Make sure you remember this radical means melon and you'll remember the kanji meaning too!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Melon"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304b, \u3051",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3046\u308a",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine hitting the gas pedal in your car as you head for a big melon. Watch it burst into a million pieces as your car makes contact, hurling melon bits all over your windshield. It's a melon firework!",
                "mnemonic": "What's the best use for a {tooltip:Kanji:melon}? Not eating it, but running over it in your {tooltip:Reading:ca}r ({span:lang=ja:\u304b})! Melons make a nice big splat sound when you drive over them, and that's what you plan to do to every melon you see."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Radical:Tiger}s and {tooltip:Radical:wolverine}s have one big thing in common and it's that the government likes to {tooltip:Kanji:oppress} them. It doesn't matter if you're the animal or a mutant X-Men hybrid. The government will go out of its way to oppress you if you are one. ",
            "hints": "Imagine being a tiger or a wolverine or a tiger-wolverine and being oppressed by the government just because you're different. Imagine being stuck in a cage. Not being allowed to vote. It's awful!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Oppress"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304e\u3083\u304f",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3044\u305f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine being a tiger/wolverine girl and being so oppressed that you can only sit in a cage, playing with goopy Gak all day. This blows...",
                "mnemonic": "Tiger and wolverine people are so {tooltip:Kanji:oppress}ed that the only thing they're allowed to amuse themselves with is {tooltip:Reading:Gak} ({span:lang=ja:\u304e\u3083\u304f}). You know, that weird goo they made us play with when we were kids? Well that's not so fun when it's all you have."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:cross} carrying {tooltip:Radical:drunkard} wants you to go to a {tooltip:Radical:rice paddy} {tooltip:Radical:together} so you can {tooltip:Kanji:receive} something from them.",
            "hints": "You really don't want to receive whatever it is this drunkard wants to give you, and it's even sketchier that they want you to go to a rice paddy together to do it.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Receive"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3044\u305f\u3060",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "The tie looks harmless enough, but you still receive it skeptically. The drunkard is just holding up his cross and smiling at you. Hmm.",
                "mnemonic": "Against your better judgement, you decided to go and {tooltip:Kanji:receive} the thing. You close your eyes, hold out your hands, and feel a silky, light {tooltip:Reading:tie} ({span:lang=ja:\u305f\u3044}) being placed in them."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:boat} owned by a {tooltip:Radical:Yoga} {tooltip:Radical:King} needs to be able to let him keep doing his exercises while he's on it. That's why his boat is a {tooltip:Kanji:rowboat}, so he can work on his yoga arms as he gets around.",
            "hints": "Picture the Yoga King rowing a rowboat. Maybe he's using his legs. Maybe he's using his butt? You don't know. Yoga people are super flexible, he could be using anything to move this rowboat.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Rowboat"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3066\u3044",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Go rent a rowboat and try eating tater tots in it. For fun, throw a bunch of them in the water and watch them float. Maybe you'll catch a fish!",
                "mnemonic": "As he rows the {tooltip:Kanji:rowboat} with his butt, you see the Yoga King eating something with his toes. They're {tooltip:Reading:ta}ter tots ({span:lang=ja:\u3066\u3044}). Tater tots are the perfect snack for a rowboat. They're small, delicious, and they float if you drop them in the water."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "This is a {tooltip:Radical:boat}, but it's missing some pieces. That's because it's really rusty, so parts are falling off of it and it looks {tooltip:Kanji:rust colored}. In ancient Chinese literature, this rusty red color represented {tooltip:Kanji:sincerity}, and you'll often see this kanji take that meaning in jukugo words. ",
            "hints": "Picture this broken, rust colored boat. It's a reddish-brown color and it's definitely missing some important pieces.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Rust Colored",
                "Alternatives": "Red, Sincerity "
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305f\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u306b",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Watch your skin slowly change as you tan to match the rust colored boat. Feel your skin in the sun. This may not be good for your health, but it's great for fashion!",
                "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Kanji:rust colored} boat has inspired you, so you sit down next to it in the sun and {tooltip:Reading:tan} ({span:lang=ja:\u305f\u3093}). You want to be the exact same rust color as the boat, and in a few hours you're sure you'll match."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:thread}s a {tooltip:Radical:criminal} wears will always be {tooltip:Kanji:scarlet}. Scarlet is the color of freshly spilt blood, which is why bad guys wear it so much.",
            "hints": "Picture a person wearing all scarlet all the time. Don't they give you a criminal-y vibe?",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Scarlet"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3072",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3042\u304b, \u3042\u3051",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Picture a criminal, covered in this scarlet thread, heat radiating off of him.",
                "mnemonic": "This {tooltip:Kanji:scarlet} color holds a lot of {tooltip:Reading:he}at ({span:lang=ja:\u3072}). Scarlet is the color of fire, and it has the heat of fire too."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Put a {tooltip:Radical:turkey} on {tooltip:Radical:ice} and it will only get {tooltip:Kanji:semi} frozen. You add more ice, and it stays the same. Only some of it will freeze. What the heck!",
            "hints": "Imagine covering a turkey in ice and every time you check it, it's only semi frozen. It doesn't matter what you do, it just refuses to freeze all the way through.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Semi"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3058\u3085\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "The lesson is to never try to freeze something in June. If you do it will only semi freeze.",
                "mnemonic": "Finally you realize it will only {tooltip:Kanji:semi} freeze because it's {tooltip:Reading:June} ({span:lang=ja:\u3058\u3085\u3093})! It's too hot out for your turkey to freeze all the way. The ice keeps melting off the top and semi thawing it!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You want to have a quaint {tooltip:Radical:cottage} {tooltip:Radical:wedding}, so you have to go to the cottage wedding {tooltip:Kanji:shop} to get everything you need. This shop is the only place you can buy everything you need. It's not like you can make a cottage from scratch!",
            "hints": "Picture yourself walking into the cottage wedding shop. Look at all the different cottages you get to choose from. And there's wedding cake too?! This shop has everything!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Shop",
                "Alternative": "Store"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307b",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine walking around the shop and then falling in love with a hoe with pretty tassels and flowers attached to it. Yes, it's only for decoration, but you're going to buy and use it during your cottage wedding.",
                "mnemonic": "You're browsing everything in the {tooltip:Kanji:shop} when a garden {tooltip:Reading:hoe} ({span:lang=ja:\u307b}) catches your eye. Yes. This will make your cottage wedding the absolute best one. The hoe will give you away at your wedding."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:dirt} {tooltip:Radical:nurse} is someone who nurses dirt. When nursed, dirt becomes {tooltip:Kanji:soil}.",
            "hints": "Soil generally has more nutrients in it. It makes sense for dirt that's been nursed to become soil.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Soil",
                "Alternative": "Earth"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3058\u3087\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3064\u3061",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine Jourm working with soil (not dirt!). Improving it by mixing in various ingredients. The soil this year is particularly lush, for some reason...",
                "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Kanji:Soil} is a thing {tooltip:Reading:Jou}rm ({span:lang=ja:\u3058\u3087\u3046}) the huge farmhand works on improving. If he makes the soil better, the farm will yield more crops."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "When a {tooltip:Radical:horse} sees a {tooltip:Radical:spider} it will start running at a {tooltip:Kanji:speed} no one ever thought a horse could go! Horses are super afraid of spiders, you see, so if they see one, they'll bolt and reach top speed! That's actually the only way to get a horse to run that fast. ",
            "hints": "Imagine the eyes of a horse seeing a spider and then running at top speed to get the heck away from it.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Speed"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3085\u3093, \u3059\u3093",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3059\u3050",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Soon the world will be filled with horses running at full speed all thanks to the spider increase of the 2000s! Soon we will be in the future of top speeds!",
                "mnemonic": "Once people realize that they can increase the {tooltip:Kanji:speed} of their horses, {tooltip:Reading:soon} ({span:lang=ja:\u3057\u3085\u3093}) everyone will be taking spiders with them to scare their horses into running faster. I say soon because it hasn't quite caught on yet. But if you help to spread the word it can happen even sooner."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "There's so much {tooltip:Radical:grain} in your {tooltip:Radical:mouth} you need to use a {tooltip:Radical:knife} to remove it. Only when there is a {tooltip:Kanji:surplus} can you put that much grain in your mouth.",
            "hints": "Imagine seeing so much grain that you can stuff your mouth so full you can't swallow or spit it out. You have to use a knife to remove it. After you get all the grain out, comment on the surplus of grain.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Surplus"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3058\u3087\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3042\u307e\u3064\u3055\u3048, \u3042\u307e",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Remember how Jourm made the soil better? Perhaps this is some of that work paying off. Thanks to that and everything else, there is a great surplus.",
                "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Kanji:surplus} is thanks to the hard work of folks like {tooltip:Reading:Jou}rm ({span:lang=ja:\u3058\u3087\u3046}) the big farmhand. He worked hard to make sure there was a surplus of grain this year."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Radical:roof} {tooltip:Radical:flowers} {tooltip:Radical:see} everything. But, they only see. That is because they learned the art of {tooltip:Kanji:tolerance}.",
            "hints": "These roof flowers only see because they do not judge. They accept everything. We humans can learn a thing or two about tolerance from these roof flowers.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Tolerance"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304b\u3093",
