                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine getting sick this rapidly. All of a sudden, there's a shih tzu barking in your head, it hurts! And... oh no, where's the toilet?!",
                "mnemonic": "This {tooltip:Kanji:rapid} sickness makes your head pound like there's a {tooltip:Reading:shih tzu} ({span:lang=ja:\u3057\u3064}) yapping and barking nonstop. And it's making you sh*t a lot."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "{tooltip:Radical:Flowers} that grow in the {tooltip:Radical:west} are colored by the sun setting so they turn bright red. That's why red flowers that grow in the west are used to make {tooltip:Kanji:red dye}. They're the closest to the actual color of the red sun, so they're prized by painters and other artists for their perfect color for dye.",
            "hints": "Picture yourself sitting in the west as the red sun sets, smashing those pretty red flowers into a paste to make red dye. Your fingers get dyed red too and you crush the petals into a paste.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Red Dye"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u305b\u3093",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3042\u304b\u306d",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Hear the little girl say {span:lang=ja:\u3042\u304b\u306d} while pointing to the red dye. She's right. And the way she says it is so darn cute.",
                "mnemonic": "As you're smashing the flowers into {tooltip:Kanji:red dye}, a little girl comes up to you and points at the paste. She says, \"{span:lang=ja:\u8d64\u306d} ({span:lang=ja:\u3042\u304b\u306d})?\" (That's red, right?) You look down at the red dye on your fingers and nod. She's right, it is red."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You took someone's {tooltip:Radical:alcohol} and {tooltip:Radical:drop} {tooltip:Radical:drop} {tooltip:Radical:drop}'d it all into the {tooltip:Radical:river}. Now you have to {tooltip:Kanji:repay} them. Alcohol costs a lot of money, you know.",
            "hints": "Picture yourself dropping the alcohol, drop by drop, into the river. Uh oh, the person just caught up to you, so you better repay them.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Repay"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3085\u3046, \u3057\u3085, \u3068\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3080\u304f",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine waiting as this person tries on like twenty different pairs of shoes. They're getting them for free! Why do they have to be picky?! Just let me repay you already!",
                "mnemonic": "In order to {tooltip:Kanji:repay} them for the alcohol you wasted, you offer to give them your {tooltip:Reading:shoe}s ({span:lang=ja:\u3057\u3085\u3046}). They don't want your shoes though, so you have to bring them to a store and watch them try on a bunch of different pairs so you can repay them that way."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You see a {tooltip:Radical:mouth} coming out of the top of a {tooltip:Radical:tree} so you go into {tooltip:Kanji:shock}. Your brain just can't comprehend what's in front of you.",
            "hints": "A tree... with... a... mouth? No, it can't be... I... a-. Your face warps into a permanent state of shock.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Shock"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u307b\u3046",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3042\u304d, \u304a\u308d\u304b, \u307b\u3051, \u307c",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "I'd be shocked if you ever left your home again, honestly. But hey, there's always anime.",
                "mnemonic": "The {tooltip:Kanji:shock} you've suffered results in you needing to stay {tooltip:Reading:ho}me ({span:lang=ja:\u307b\u3046}) from now on. You don't get to travel, explore, or even get groceries. You're homebound from now on, buddy."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You cast the {tooltip:Radical:Mona Lisa} in {tooltip:Radical:gold} and attach it to a stick to turn it into a {tooltip:Kanji:sickle}! It's really big and bulky, but when you swing it fast enough your new sickle cuts lots of wheat! Just don't tell anyone what you did with the famous Mona Lisa painting and you should be able to get away with this!",
            "hints": "Imagine wielding your gold Mona Lisa sickle. It's sooo heavy, but it's really shiny and it cuts grass and wheat pretty well!",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Sickle",
                "Alternative": "Scythe"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3051\u3093, \u308c\u3093",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304b\u307e",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Hear all the guests say, \"Mona Lisa... comma the sickle? WHAT'S HAPPENED HERE?!\" As panic ensues. You like your addition and you're very proud.",
                "mnemonic": "Now that you've turned the Mona Lisa into a {tooltip:Kanji:sickle}, you have to change the little description under its name in the Louvre. You draw a giant {tooltip:Reading:comma} ({span:lang=ja:\u304b\u307e}) next to \"Mona Lisa\" and write \"THE SICKLE.\" Then you shove it back on the wall as best you can."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "A {tooltip:Radical:wolverine} that {tooltip:Radical:stick}s its {tooltip:Radical:legs} in {tooltip:Radical:rice} will become very {tooltip:Kanji:solemn}, very fast. This is weird to you because wolverines always seem so happy. Well his dad died from his legs getting stuck in rice, so just leave the poor solemn creature alone.",
            "hints": "Imagine if your dad died from getting his legs stuck in some rice, now feel solemn too.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Solemn"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3057\u3085\u304f, \u3059\u304f",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3064\u3064\u3057",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine seeing how violently you shook him. You shook him so hard his solemn mood disappeared! ",
                "mnemonic": "Remember how {tooltip:Kanji:solemn} wolverine looked when his dad died? You hated seeing him like this so you ran up and {tooltip:Reading:shook} ({span:lang=ja:\u3057\u3085\u304f}) him. You shook the solemn sadness right out of him! You should do that again now!"
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "When you leave {tooltip:Radical:flowers} on a {tooltip:Radical:tombstone} they look good at first, but after a few days or some harsh weather, there's nothing left but {tooltip:Kanji:stem}s. That's why you have to replace flowers on tombstones all the time. If you don't it will look like you only left the stems there, and that's pretty disrespectful.",
            "hints": "Imagine being in a graveyard looking at all the tombstones and seeing nothing but stems on all of them. All the pretty flowery parts have died and blown away.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Stem"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304d\u3087\u3046, \u3051\u3044",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304f\u304d",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Look at all the cookie covered tombstones. Now your bag is full of stems, but you can use them to make more cookies to bring back next time. Don't worry, nobody eats them. Stem cookies are probably poisonous.",
                "mnemonic": "You have an idea that can make these tombstones look better than just having leftover {tooltip:Kanji:stem}s all over them. You brought {tooltip:Reading:cookie}s ({span:lang=ja:\u304f\u304d})! Cookies don't fall apart in the wind, and everyone loves cookies, alive or dead. You replace the stems with the cookies and everything looks a lot nicer."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "You're {tooltip:Radical:sick} because you put an {tooltip:Radical:arrow} in your {tooltip:Radical:mouth} and tried to eat it. To be honest, that's just really {tooltip:Kanji:stupid}. You have to be stupid to try to eat an arrow. You deserve to be sick.",
            "hints": "How stupid do you have to be to put arrows into your mouth until it makes you sick? Pretty darn stupid, it seems.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Stupid"
            },
            "kanjiReadings": {
                "readings": [
                    {
                        "name": "On\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u3061",
                        "primary": true
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Kun\u2019yomi",
                        "reading": "\u304a\u308d\u304b, \u3057",
                        "primary": false
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "Nanori",
                        "reading": "None",
                        "primary": false
                    }
                ],
                "hints": "Imagine if everyone knew that cheese cures the stupid out of people. Then maybe we would have more cheese and less stupid in the world!",
                "mnemonic": "The only way to cure this serious case of {tooltip:Kanji:stupid} is to make you eat {tooltip:Reading:chee}se ({span:lang=ja:\u3061}). Not only will it fix your sick stomach by cushioning all the arrows that you shoved in your stupid mouth, but it should send smart cheese waves to your brain and cure you of that stupid all together."
            }
        }

{
            "mnemonic": "Clearing his {tooltip:Radical:flowers} of {tooltip:Radical:icicle}s, the {tooltip:Radical:samurai} returns home to his {tooltip:Kanji:villa}.",
            "hints": "Picture the samurai's villa. It's in the countryside and covered in flowers and icicles. Even though it's spring, it's still cold, so all the new flowers are very cold.",
            "meanings": {
                "Primary": "Villa"
            },
